/* True or False minigame statement pool — common myths/misconceptions + surprising truths.
   Each item generated and adversarially fact-checked for accuracy. { q: statement, a: true|false, why: explanation, cat: category }.

   IT IS NOT ONLY HISTORY (Aug 2026, on request: "add more possible questions — they needn't only be about
   history, but any science"). The pool opened with five historical categories and now carries seven more —
   Physics, Chemistry, Astronomy, Biology, Earth science, Mathematics, Medicine — beside the original
   Science & invention. The category is shown to the reader, so it is the subject the statement is ABOUT
   rather than where it came from.
   The bar is the site's own and does not move for a wider subject: nothing here is a claim that "everyone
   knows", every `why` says what the evidence actually is, and a figure that is contested or has been
   revised says so rather than being rounded into certainty.

   PSYCHOLOGY IS THE THIRTEENTH CATEGORY (Aug 2026, on request), and it is the one that most needs the bar
   above rather than a relaxation of it: it is the subject with the largest stock of confidently repeated
   claims that were never established — learning styles, left- and right-brained people, the Mozart
   effect — and equally the one where a famous study is now read very differently from the
   way it was first reported. So a statement here says what was actually measured (Milgram's 26 of 40,
   the 54 per cent of a lie-detection meta-analysis), and where a celebrated experiment has not held up
   the `why` says so plainly rather than repeating the textbook version.
   TWO OF ITS THIRTEEN ARE ABOUT STUDYING ITSELF — the testing effect and the spacing effect — which is
   not a joke at the reader's expense: they are the two best-replicated findings in the whole category
   and they are what this site is built on, so a reader who meets them here is being told where the
   flashcards come from. */

/* ONE STATEMENT PER LINE (Sep 2026). The whole pool was a single line, so every change to it — a spelling,
   a conversion, a new statement — arrived as a one-line diff nobody could read, which is the shape
   `data.js` was given one object per line to avoid. Nothing about the data moved: the array reparses
   entry-for-entry identical to what it was.

   AN ENTRY MAY CARRY `src`, AND THE `why` MAY CARRY MARKERS (Sep 2026, on request: "'True or False'
   minigame explanations should have gloss terms, source citations and metric/imperial uk/us versions").
   `src` is a list of Chicago notes ending in a URL a reader can open, exactly as a card's `sources` is,
   and the `why` points into it with `<sup class="fn" data-fn="N"></sup>` written EMPTY — the digit is
   drawn at render time, so re-ordering the list can never leave a stale number in the sentence. The page
   renders the `why` through `sanitizeHTML` rather than escaping it, so `<i>` for a work's title comes
   with them, and auto-links the glossary through the prose.
   THE THIRD OF THE THREE IS A CONTENT RULE AND NOT A CODE ONE. The units and spelling passes are standing
   observers over the whole document already; what they need is prose written the site's way — METRIC
   FIRST with the imperial in brackets, and BRITISH spelling, the transform being one-way from en-GB. A
   figure with no bracket is shown in metric to an imperial reader and an American spelling is shown to
   everybody. `node .claude/check-truefalse.js` is what measures all of it. */
window.TRUEFALSE = [
{"q":"The Great Wall of China is the only human-made structure visible to the naked eye from the Moon.","a":false,"why":"No individual human-made structure is visible to the naked eye from the Moon, about 384,000 kilometres (239,000 miles) away, and NASA notes that the Wall is difficult or impossible to make out even from Earth orbit without the high-powered lenses used to photograph it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The relevant dimension is not its length but its width, usually under 6 metres (20 feet): to pick out something that narrow from 160 kilometres (100 miles) up would take a visual acuity about 7.7 times the normal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> NASA itself calls the Wall’s reputation a space-based myth, and the first confirmed photograph of it from orbit, in 2004, needed a lens of 180 millimetres (7 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["NASA, “Great Wall,” image article, 2 April 2009, https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/great-wall/. [Open access]","Norberto López-Gil, “Is it Really Possible to See the Great Wall of China from Space with a Naked Eye?,” <i>Journal of Optometry</i> 1, no. 1 (2008): 3–4, https://doi.org/10.3921/joptom.2008.3. [Open access]","NASA, “China’s Wall Less Great in View from Space,” 2005 (page last updated 30 November 2007), https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/static/history/PDFs/great_wall_less_great_from_space.pdf. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Great Wall as a single continuous barrier was mostly built by Qin Shi Huang in the 3rd century BCE and the brick wall tourists visit today is that original structure.","a":false,"why":"The brick face and hollow towers most photographs show are Ming work (1368–1644).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Qin frontier walls were rammed earth, their towers solid platforms rather than brick chambers,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and Meng Tian joined and lengthened walls the older states had already built rather than raising one seamless barrier from nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Yousong Cao, Matthew A. Bowker, Manuel Delgado-Baquerizo, and Bo Xiao, “Biocrusts Protect the Great Wall of China from Erosion,” <i>Science Advances</i> 9, no. 49 (2023): eadk5892, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adk5892. [Open access]","Xingyi Li, Yangyang Tong, Xiaoning Ma, Jiachen Chang, and Binyan He, “The Building Concept of Border Defence Facilities of Qin: Watchtowers along the Great Wall in Shaanxi,” <i>Heritage Science</i> 11 (2023): 108, https://doi.org/10.1186/s40494-023-00951-2. [Open access]","Friedrich Hirth, <i>The Ancient History of China to the End of the Chou Dynasty</i> (New York: Columbia University Press, 1908), 250, https://archive.org/stream/ancienthistoryof00hirtuoft. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Confucius founded a religion that worshipped him as a god during his lifetime.","a":false,"why":"Confucianism is the body of teaching that grew from Confucius and his followers, and its two central words are <i>ren</i>, the inner moral sentiment, and <i>li</i>, the outward rule of conduct — an ethical and political tradition rather than a religion with a god in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became an official system of thought and behaviour only later, especially after Dong Zhongshu's work under the Han, and Confucius himself said he was a transmitter and not a maker.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The temples in which he later received ritual offerings in every major city belong to his posthumous career: it was under the Han, some three centuries after his death, that he had become an authority whose texts rulers were expected to read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Bo Hu, Fugui Xing, Miaorong Fan, and Tingshao Zhu, “Research on the Evolution of ‘Ren’ and ‘Li’ in <i>SikuQuanshu</i> Confucian Classics,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 12 (2021): 603344, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.603344. [Open access]","James Legge, prolegomena to <i>Confucian Analects, The Great Learning, and The Doctrine of the Mean</i>, 2nd ed., The Chinese Classics 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1893), 1–2, 14–16, 48–49, 56–90, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg. [Open access]","Mark Csikszentmihalyi, “Confucius,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman (Stanford: Metaphysics Research Lab, 2020; rev. 2024), https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/confucius/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Paper was invented in China centuries before it was used in Europe.","a":true,"why":"Hemp-fibre paper is attested in Chinese finds of the 1st century BCE, a century before Cai Lun reported the process to the throne in 105 CE<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> — the <i>Hou Hanshu</i> has him make it from tree bark, hemp, rags and fishing nets and present it in the first year of Yuanxing<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> — and the craft reached the Islamic world only after Chinese papermakers were taken prisoner at Samarkand in 751, travelling on to Europe centuries later still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Jean-Pierre Drège, review of <i>Zhongguo zaozhi jishu shigao</i>, by Pan Jixing, <i>Bulletin de l’École française d’Extrême-Orient</i> 71 (1982): 263–66, https://www.persee.fr/doc/befeo_0336-1519_1982_num_71_1_1475. [Open access]","Thomas Francis Carter, <i>The Invention of Printing in China and Its Spread Westward</i> (New York: Columbia University Press, 1925), 93–98, https://archive.org/stream/inventionofprint00cart_0/inventionofprint00cart_0_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Fan Ye, <i>Hou Hanshu</i> (Siku quanshu edition), juan 108, “Cai Lun zhuan,” Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E5%BE%8C%E6%BC%A2%E6%9B%B8_(%E5%9B%9B%E5%BA%AB%E5%85%A8%E6%9B%B8%E6%9C%AC)/%E5%8D%B7108. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Gunpowder was written about in China before it was used as a weapon.","a":true,"why":"It was. A Chinese scholar looking back over the record thought the fire trees and silver flowers of Tang festival displays had already contained the mixture, and set down what it does: saltpetre with pine charcoal explodes forward, saltpetre with sulphur explodes sideways, and iron dust with camphor makes it throw sparks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest formulas that survive are in a military encyclopaedia presented to the Song throne in 1044, and they are for an incendiary rather than a propellant — saltpetre, sulphur and charcoal with a long list of resins, oils and poisons, wrapped in five layers of paper and thrown from a catapult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Daoist alchemical manual preserved in the Daoist canon lists among the errors of the unwary those who burn sulphur, realgar and saltpetre together with honey, so that flames leap up to scorch hands and faces and burn down the house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["William Frederick Mayers, “On the Introduction and Use of Gunpowder and Firearms among the Chinese,” <i>Journal of the North-China Branch of the Royal Asiatic Society</i>, n.s., 6 (1871): 73–79, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.533372. [Open access]","Zeng Gongliang and Ding Du, comps., <i>Wujing zongyao</i>, qianji juan 12 (1044), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E6%AD%A6%E7%B6%93%E7%B8%BD%E8%A6%81/%E5%89%8D%E9%9B%86/%E5%8D%B7%E5%8D%81%E4%BA%8C. [Open access]","<i>Zhenyuan miaodao yaolüe</i> 真元妙道要略 (Daoist alchemical text, Daozang), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%9C%9F%E5%85%83%E5%A6%99%E9%81%93%E8%A6%81%E7%95%A5. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Foot-binding was a custom practiced across all of China since the time of Confucius.","a":false,"why":"The practice begins some fifteen centuries after Confucius: a study of bound feet in museum collections dates it in Han China to the eleventh century, and notes that the Chinese government banned it in 1912.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor was it general when it began — it started among the nobility, and only by the seventeenth century had it spread across the social strata, and then for the sake of marriage prospects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The custom probably began no earlier than the tenth century and ended at different times in different parts of China, between the mid-nineteenth and the mid-twentieth century,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the bones themselves show the type and severity of binding varying considerably over time and place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Natalie Reznikov, Carina Phillips, Martyn Cooke, Amin Garbout, Farah Ahmed, and Molly M. Stevens, “Functional Adaptation of the Calcaneus in Historical Foot Binding,” <i>Journal of Bone and Mineral Research</i> 32, no. 9 (2017): 1915–25, https://doi.org/10.1002/jbmr.3185. [Open access]","Melissa J. Brown and Damian Satterthwaite-Phillips, “Economic Correlates of Footbinding: Implications for the Importance of Chinese Daughters’ Labor,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 13, no. 9 (2018): e0201337, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0201337. [Open access]","Yongsheng Zhao, Lin Guo, Yuni Xiao, Yueming Niu, Xiaowen Zhang, Deliang He, and Wen Zeng, “Osteological Characteristics of Chinese Foot-Binding in Archaeological Remains,” <i>International Journal of Paleopathology</i> 28 (2020): 48–58, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijpp.2019.12.003. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"Roman \"vomitoria\" were rooms where banquet guests would vomit so they could keep eating.","a":false,"why":"A vomitorium is a door rather than a room: the name belonged to the doors opening from an amphitheatre's staircases and corridors on to the seating inside, and the Colosseum had sixty-four of them to pour the crowd out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The banqueting story attaches to the word nothing the Romans did with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word’s ancient attestation is in Macrobius, who derives it from the verb for spewing: the openings in a place of spectacle are called <i>vomitoria</i> because through them the crowd pours itself in a mass into the seats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the Colosseum, flights of steps beneath the seating led to every part of the cavea through these openings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Philip Smith, “Amphitheatrum,” in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i>, ed. William Smith (London: John Murray, 1875), 82–90, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Amphitheatrum.html. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, “Amphitheatrum Flavium,” in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 6–11, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Amphitheatrum_Flavium.html. [Open access]","Macrobius, <i>Saturnalia</i> 6.4.3, in <i>Macrobius</i>, ed. Franz Eyssenhardt (Leipzig: Teubner, 1868), 368, https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_vs4fAAAAMAAJ. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Great Pyramid of Giza was built largely by paid Egyptian labourers, not by enslaved Hebrew slaves.","a":true,"why":"The people who raised it lived beside it: Heit el-Ghurab, the workmen's town of the Fourth Dynasty, stood on the Giza plateau with an important Nile harbour beside it, and the plateau holds the tombs of the officials and of the workers as well as the royal pyramids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its excavators, digging since 1988 some 400 metres (1,300 feet) south of the Sphinx, found a purpose-built town of bakeries, workshops, storerooms and long galleries that housed the builders of the pyramid complexes of Menkaure, Khafre and probably Khufu,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and on the hillside just west of it the town’s cemetery, where low-ranking overseers lie in modest mastabas ringed by the smaller tombs of the workers they supervised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The workers quartered in its barracks served by rotation — farmers brought in from all the Egyptian nomes, three months at a time, to take their turn at the building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is an Egyptian workforce, housed where it worked and buried on the same plateau as the officials it worked under.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Ghada Darwish AL-Khafif, Rokia El-Banna, Nancy Khattab, Tamer Gad Rashed, and Salwa Dahesh, “The Immunodetection of Non-Falciparum Malaria in Ancient Egyptian Bones (Giza Necropolis),” <i>BioMed Research International</i> 2018 (2018): 9058108, https://doi.org/10.1155/2018/9058108. [Open access]","Ancient Egypt Research Associates, “The Lost City of the Pyramid Builders (Heit el-Ghurab),” https://aeraweb.org/projects/lost-city/. [Open access]","Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities (Egypt), “Workers’ Town and Cemetery,” <i>Discover Egypt’s Monuments</i>, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/monuments/workers-town-and-cemetery/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Gladiator fights in the Colosseum almost always ended with the loser being killed.","a":false,"why":"Sparing the loser had a name of its own: a vanquished gladiator whose life was granted got his discharge for the day, the <i>missio</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A show advertised <i>sine missione</i>, in which the conquered were never spared, was a different and notorious thing, and Augustus forbade it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The odds were real but far from certain death: one estimate puts the chance of dying in a given fight in the 1st century CE at about one in nine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["“Gladiatores,” in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i>, ed. William Smith (London: John Murray, 1875), 574–77, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Gladiatores.html. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Life of Augustus</i> 45.3, trans. J. C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Augustus*.html. [Open access]","Sandra Lösch, Negahnaz Moghaddam, Karl Grossschmidt, Daniele U. Risser, and Fabian Kanz, “Stable Isotope and Trace Element Studies on Gladiators and Contemporary Romans from Ephesus (Turkey, 2nd and 3rd Ct. AD) — Implications for Differences in Diet,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 9, no. 10 (2014): e110489, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0110489. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Roman concrete, used in structures like the Pantheon's dome, has in many cases proven more durable in seawater than much modern concrete.","a":true,"why":"Roman marine concrete mixed volcanic ash with lime, and seawater percolating through it went on growing aluminous tobermorite and phillipsite in the cement long after it had set, so that harbour structures two thousand years old are still intact where modern Portland-cement concrete in seawater is not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Vitruvius already knew the trick: a natural powder which, mixed with lime and rubble, makes piers that set hard under water even when built in the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Modern work on the material confirms that, unlike their modern counterparts, Roman mortars and concretes have stayed durable for millennia even in direct contact with seawater.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Marie D. Jackson, Sean R. Mulcahy, Heng Chen, Yao Li, Qinfei Li, Piergiulio Cappelletti, and Hans-Rudolf Wenk, “Phillipsite and Al-tobermorite Mineral Cements Produced through Low-Temperature Water-Rock Reactions in Roman Marine Concrete,” <i>American Mineralogist</i> 102, no. 7 (2017): 1435–50, http://www.minsocam.org/msa/ammin/toc/2017/open_access/AM102P1435.pdf. [Open access]","Vitruvius, <i>The Ten Books on Architecture</i> 2.6.1, trans. Morris Hicky Morgan (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0073:book=2:chapter=6. [Open access]","Linda M. Seymour, Janille Maragh, Paolo Sabatini, Michel Di Tommaso, James C. Weaver, and Admir Masic, “Hot Mixing: Mechanistic Insights into the Durability of Ancient Roman Concrete,” <i>Science Advances</i> 9, no. 1 (2023): eadd1602, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.add1602. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Cleopatra lived closer in time to the moon landing than to the construction of the Great Pyramid of Giza.","a":true,"why":"The Great Pyramid was raised for Khufu in about 2589–2566 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cleopatra's kingdom ended with the Roman annexation of Egypt in 30 BCE, some twenty-five centuries after it was built<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> — and Apollo 11 put men on the Moon on 20 July 1969, a little under twenty centuries after her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, “Giza Plateau,” Egypt’s Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, accessed 21 August 2026, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/archaeological-sites/giza-plateau/. [Open access]","Jona Lendering, “Ptolemies,” Livius.org, last modified 2020, https://www.livius.org/articles/dynasty/ptolemies/. [Open access]","“Apollo 11,” NASA, https://www.nasa.gov/mission/apollo-11/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Spartans famously inscribed the words \"Molon labe\" onto their shields as their standard military emblem.","a":false,"why":"Plutarch gives “come and take them” as Leonidas’s spoken reply to Xerxes’s written demand to hand over his arms at Thermopylae, not as anything written on a shield.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus, the earliest account of the battle, has no such exchange: he records the struggle over Leonidas’s body and the stone lion later set up for him at the mouth of the pass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What a Spartan shield carried was a blazon, not a sentence — Pausanias has a thousand picked Messenians pass themselves off at Elis by carrying Laconian devices on their shields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Plutarch, <i>Sayings of Spartans</i>, “Leonidas son of Anaxandridas” 11 (<i>Moralia</i> 225D), trans. Frank Cole Babbitt, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1931), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/Sayings_of_Spartans*/main.html. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 7.225, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=225. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 4.28.5, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=4:chapter=28:section=5. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The ancient Olympic Games in Greece were held in honour of Zeus and barred married women from attending as spectators.","a":true,"why":"The games were the athletic festival of the sanctuary of Zeus at Olympia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias says it was a law of Elis to throw from Mount Typaeum any woman caught at the games on the days forbidden to women, and that only one ever was — Callipateira, let go unpunished because her father, her brothers and her son had all won there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Girls were another matter: he says maidens were not debarred from looking on, and that the priestess of Demeter Chamyne watched from a marble altar opposite the umpires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Paul Christesen, review of <i>Games and Sanctuaries in Ancient Greece: Olympia, Delphi, Isthmia, Nemea, Athens</i>, by Panos Valavanis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.01.20, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.01.20. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 5.6.7, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%205.6.7. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 6.20.9, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%206.20.9. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Colosseum was originally built and named in honour of the emperor Julius Caesar.","a":false,"why":"Its own name was the Amphitheatrum Flavium, after the Flavian house: it was begun by Vespasian<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and opened under his son Titus in 80 CE,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> more than a century after Julius Caesar’s death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name Colosseum is medieval and comes from the colossal statue that stood beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, “Amphitheatrum Flavium,” in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 6–11, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Amphitheatrum_Flavium.html. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Life of Vespasian</i> 9.1, trans. J. C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Vespasian*.html. [Open access]","Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i> 66.25, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 8 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/66*.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Vikings wore horned helmets into battle.","a":false,"why":"Until 2020 only one helmet was known from the Viking Age at all — the plain iron cap from the warrior's burial at Gjermundbu, north of Oslo — and it has no horns, while none of the contemporary sources mentions horned headgear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Horns would have been in the way in a fight and worse aboard a crowded warship, and where the art does show them the figures are berserkers, so the museum reads them as display or cult rather than battle dress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Since 2020 the helmet from Yarm in England has been accepted as a second, and the Oslo museum that keeps the Gjermundbu helmet, “the only Viking age helmet from Norway”, welcomed the news that it “is no longer the only Viking helmet in the world”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The horned helmets of Scandinavian prehistory are another matter: the imagery belongs to the Bronze Age, around 1000–750 BCE, when horns marked out a very particular class of warrior in Scandinavia, Sardinia and Iberia alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Medieval & early modern","src":["National Museum of Denmark, “Viking helmets,” https://en.natmus.dk/historical-knowledge/denmark/prehistoric-period-until-1050-ad/the-viking-age/weapons/helmets/. [Open access]","Vegard Vike, “The Viking Helmet from Yarm,” Museum of Cultural History, University of Oslo, 11 September 2020, https://khm.uio.no/english/news/the-viking-helmet-from-yarm.html. [Open access]","Helle Vandkilde, Valentina Matta, Laura Ahlqvist, and Heide W. Nørgaard, “Anthropomorphised Warlike Beings with Horned Helmets: Bronze Age Scandinavia, Sardinia, and Iberia Compared,” <i>Praehistorische Zeitschrift</i> 97, no. 1 (2022): 130–58, https://doi.org/10.1515/pz-2021-2012. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Educated people in Columbus's time generally believed the Earth was flat.","a":false,"why":"The shape of the Earth, and its size, were settled by Hellenistic scientists by the 3rd century BCE and were known to the learned throughout the Middle Ages<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> — Pliny the Elder had put it plainly in the 1st century CE: “we always speak of the ball of the earth, and we admit it to be a globe bounded by the poles”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sphericity was taught out of Sacrobosco's <i>De sphaera</i>, the standard university textbook, whose diagrams argue it with stick figures walking round the globe and a ship whose lower sailor is blocked by the Earth's bulge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What Columbus's contemporaries disputed was the size of the globe rather than its shape, and his own measurement of its circumference was less accurate than that of many of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Medieval & early modern","src":["Dennis Reinhartz, review of <i>Inventing the Flat Earth: Columbus and Modern Historians</i>, by Jeffrey Burton Russell, <i>Teaching History: A Journal of Methods</i> 18, no. 1 (1993): 30–31, https://doi.org/10.33043/TH.18.1.30-31. [Open access]","Richard J. Oosterhoff, “A Lathe and the Material Sphaera: Astronomical Technique at the Origins of the Cosmographical Handbook,” in <i>De sphaera of Johannes de Sacrobosco in the Early Modern Period: The Authors of the Commentaries</i>, ed. Matteo Valleriani (Cham: Springer, 2020), 33, https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-30833-9_2. [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> 2.64, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley (London: Taylor and Francis, 1855), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0137:book=2:chapter=64. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The accused in the Salem witch trials were executed by being burned at the stake.","a":false,"why":"Bridget Bishop’s death warrant of June 1692 orders the sheriff to “cause her to be hanged by the neck untill she be dead”, and his return on the same sheet records that he did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The one execution at Salem that was not a hanging was Giles Corey’s: he refused to plead and was pressed to death under stones that September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nobody there was burned at all: Robert Calef’s contemporary account of the trials has Bishop “executed June 10” and Corey “prest to death” on 16 September, and no fire for anyone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Medieval & early modern","src":["“Warrant for the Execution of Bridget Bishop, and Officer’s Return,” 8–10 June 1692, SWP no. 013, in <i>Salem Witchcraft Papers</i>, University of Virginia, https://salem.lib.virginia.edu/n13.html. [Open access]","“SWP No. 037: Giles Corey Pressed to Death, September 16, 1692,” in <i>Salem Witchcraft Papers</i>, University of Virginia, https://salem.lib.virginia.edu/n37.html. [Open access]","Robert Calef, <i>More Wonders of the Invisible World</i> (1700; Salem: Cushing and Appleton, 1823), https://archive.org/details/morewondersofinv00cale. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Renaissance began in Italy.","a":true,"why":"Italy first, and one book made the case so thoroughly that the period has been described in its terms ever since. Jacob Burckhardt's <i>Civilisation of the Renaissance in Italy</i> sets the Italian of the city-states down as “the first-born among the sons of modern Europe”, says that the medieval veil over self and world “in Italy first melted into air”, and describes “the mighty impulse which came forth from Italy” that the rest of Europe was free to accept or to repel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Florence and its neighbours had the wealth to pay for it, the Medici foremost among the patrons, and Vasari opens his history of painting’s revival there, with Cimabue born in Florence in 1240 “to give the first light to the art of painting”;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Symonds saw in the despots’ Italy “that development of arts and industry which raised Italy to a first place among civilized nations”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Medieval & early modern","src":["Jacob Burckhardt, <i>The Civilisation of the Renaissance in Italy</i>, trans. S. G. C. Middlemore (London: George Allen and Unwin), pt. 2, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2074. [Open access]","Giorgio Vasari, “Life of Giovanni Cimabue,” in <i>Lives of the Most Eminent Painters, Sculptors and Architects</i>, trans. Gaston du C. de Vere, vol. 1 (London: Macmillan and the Medici Society, 1912), https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/25326. [Open access]","John Addington Symonds, <i>Renaissance in Italy: The Age of the Despots</i> (New York: Henry Holt, 1888), 78, https://archive.org/details/renaissanceinita88symo. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Medieval Europeans commonly believed tomatoes and chili peppers were essential cooking ingredients.","a":false,"why":"Tomatoes and chile peppers are American plants: the domesticated chilli, <i>Capsicum annuum</i>, arose in Mexico, where preceramic remains of it have been recovered in the Valley of Tehuacán.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Before Columbus's voyages there were no tomatoes in Italy and no chile peppers in Thailand, and they reached the Old World only afterwards — long after the Middle Ages had ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor did any of it happen quickly: American beans are not certainly shown in European painting until the middle of the 16th century, decades after the first voyages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Medieval & early modern","src":["“The Columbian Exchange,” Castillo de San Marcos National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/casa/learn/historyculture/columbian-exchange.htm. [Open access]","James R. Myers, Alice K. Formiga and Jules Janick, “Iconography of Beans and Related Legumes Following the Columbian Exchange,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 13 (2022): 851029, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8964180/. [Open access]","Kraig H. Kraft, Cecil H. Brown, Gary P. Nabhan, Eike Luedeling, José de Jesús Luna Ruiz, Geo Coppens d’Eeckenbrugge, Robert J. Hijmans, and Paul Gepts, “Multiple Lines of Evidence for the Origin of Domesticated Chili Pepper, <i>Capsicum annuum</i>, in Mexico,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 17 (2014): 6165–70, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1308933111. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The medieval 'right of the first night,' giving a lord the right to sleep with a vassal's bride, is well documented as a widely practiced custom.","a":false,"why":"Karl Schmidt searched the charters of nine countries for it and found instead that the legend in its modern sense took shape only towards the end of the fifteenth century or the beginning of the sixteenth, fed by older tales of ancient tyrants who violated brides and were punished for it, by travellers' reports of distant peoples, and by confusion about the marriage dues serfs genuinely did owe their lords.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The doctrine that such a right had held across most or all of medieval Europe he called a learned superstition — <i>ein gelehrter Aberglaube</i> — kept up by scholars who had never seriously examined the evidence for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A review of the sources a century on reached the same verdict: the late-medieval belief in such a privilege was real, and lords exploited it in humiliating symbolic dues, but actual intercourse under the alleged right is hard to prove and there is no hard evidence that it ever happened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Veuillot had already called it in 1854 “a fable against which the very reason of those who publish it protests”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Medieval & early modern","src":["Karl Schmidt, <i>Jus primae noctis: Eine geschichtliche Untersuchung</i> (Freiburg im Breisgau: Herder, 1881), ch. 94, 379, https://archive.org/details/jusprimaenoctise00schmuoft. [Open access]","Jörg Wettlaufer, “The Jus Primae Noctis as a Male Power Display: A Review of Historic Sources with Evolutionary Interpretation,” <i>Evolution and Human Behavior</i> 21, no. 2 (2000): 111–23, https://doi.org/10.1016/S1090-5138(99)00032-X. [Paywalled]","Louis Veuillot, <i>Le droit du seigneur au moyen âge</i> (Paris: L. Vivès, 1854), 297, https://archive.org/details/ledroitduseigne00veuigoog. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Universities such as Bologna, Oxford, and Paris were founded during the Middle Ages.","a":true,"why":"The institution’s own name was <i>studium generale</i>, a school attended by scholars from all parts, and by the second half of the 12th century very few places answered that description — Paris, Bologna, Salerno and Oxford were nearly the whole of the list.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What made the title worth having was the <i>jus ubique docendi</i>, a licence to teach anywhere, granted by pope or emperor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor are they older than that: Oxford was not founded by King Alfred, Bologna does not go back to Theodosius, and Paris did not exist in Charlemagne’s time or for nearly four centuries after it — the oldest universities “just grew”, without founder or fixed date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bologna itself counts from 1088, the year conventionally taken as the founding of its Studium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Medieval & early modern","src":["Hastings Rashdall, <i>The Universities of Europe in the Middle Ages</i>, vol. 2, pt. 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 3–5, https://archive.org/details/universitieseur05rashgoog. [Open access]","Charles Homer Haskins, <i>The Rise of Universities</i> (New York: Henry Holt, 1923), 5–6, 9, https://archive.org/details/riseofuniversiti00hask. [Open access]","Università di Bologna, “Our History: Nine Centuries of History,” https://www.unibo.it/en/university/who-we-are/our-history. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Napoleon Bonaparte was unusually short for his time, standing only about five feet two inches tall.","a":false,"why":"The figure is real and the measure is French. Napoleon's autopsy recorded the body at “five feet two inches and four lines”, and the report's own footnote adds that this is French measure, “equal to five feet six inches” English.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His valet Constant gives the same measure, “five feet, two inches, three lines”,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the Fondation Napoléon, working the autopsy’s figure through at 32.47 centimetres (12.78 inches) to the <i>pied</i> and 2.7 to the <i>pouce</i>, arrives at 1.686 metres (5 feet 6 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is about 1.69 metres (5 feet 6 inches), average or a shade above for a Frenchman of his day: the French <i>pouce</i> was longer than the English inch, and British caricature did the rest.","cat":"Modern history","src":["F. Antommarchi, <i>The Last Days of the Emperor Napoleon</i>, vol. 2 (London: Henry Colburn, 1825), 162–63, https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.111416. [Open access]","Louis Constant Wairy, <i>Recollections of the Private Life of Napoleon</i>, trans. Walter Clark (1895), https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3580. [Open access]","Marcel Dunan, “La taille de Napoléon,” napoleon.org (Fondation Napoléon), 17 August 2006, https://www.napoleon.org/histoire-des-2-empires/articles/la-taille-de-napoleon/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The guillotine was still being used to carry out executions in France within living memory of the moon landings.","a":true,"why":"France kept the guillotine to the very end: the last execution was carried out at Marseille in 1977, on a machine of a pattern already in use at the close of the nineteenth century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A proposal by several deputies in 1970 to replace it with lethal injection, on the American model, went nowhere — by then the argument had moved on to abolishing the penalty itself, which France did in 1981.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Apollo 11 had put men on the Moon eight years before that last execution, in July 1969.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["Nicolas Picard, “1981年の死刑廃止以降のフランスにおける死刑の法的・政治的問題,” trans. Maki Fukuda, <i>神戸法學雜誌</i> 74, no. 1 (2024): 43–106, https://doi.org/10.24546/0100490365. [in Japanese] [Open access]","“Apollo 11,” NASA, https://www.nasa.gov/mission/apollo-11/. [Open access]","Assemblée nationale, “Peine de mort: 18 septembre 1981, l’abolition de la peine de mort est votée à l’Assemblée nationale,” https://www.assemblee-nationale.fr/histoire/peinedemort/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"During the storming of the Bastille in 1789, the revolutionaries freed thousands of political prisoners held in the fortress.","a":false,"why":"It held seven men, and not one of them was there for politics. Frantz Funck-Brentano, who worked on the fortress's own surviving papers — thrown into the moat and scattered through the prison on 14 July, recovered, and kept since 1798 at the Bibliothèque de l’Arsenal with the gaol registers and the prisoners’ files<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> — counted them: “four forgers whose arrest had been ordered by the Châtelet, whose case had been regularly tried, and whose proper place was an ordinary prison; two madmen who ought to have been at Charenton; and the Comte de Solages”, a young nobleman confined at his family's request and kept on a pension from his father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The crowd had come for the gunpowder and arms stored inside; the prisoners were an afterthought, and the conquerors were “somewhat surprised at the small number”<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> — Carlyle, too, has “the seven Prisoners” hearing the muffled din of the assault far down in their vaults.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["Frantz Funck-Brentano, <i>Legends of the Bastille</i>, trans. George Maidment (London: Downey, 1899), https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/43231. [Open access]","Thomas Carlyle, <i>The French Revolution: A History</i> (1837), bk. 5, ch. 6, “Storm and Victory,” https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1301. [Open access]","Bibliothèque nationale de France, “Archives de la Bastille,” Bibliothèque de l’Arsenal, https://www.bnf.fr/fr/archives-de-la-bastille. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"World War I formally ended with the armistice that took effect at the eleventh hour of November 11, 1918.","a":false,"why":"The armistice signed at Compiègne stopped the fighting and did no more than that: it ran for thirty days with an option to extend, and either side could denounce it at forty-eight hours’ notice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The war was ended in law by the peace treaties, the first of them signed with Germany in the Galerie des Glaces at Versailles on 28 June 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["\"The Special Representative in France House to the Secretary of State, Paris, November 11, 1918,\" document 420 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, 1918, Supplement 1, The World War, Volume I</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1933), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1918Supp01v01/d420. [Open access]","\"Protocol No. 2, Plenary Session of June 28, 1919,\" document 12 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume III</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1943), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv03/d12. [Open access]","“The Versailles Treaty, June 28, 1919,” <i>The Avalon Project: Documents in Law, History and Diplomacy</i>, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/subject_menus/versailles_menu.asp. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Adolf Hitler became Germany's leader by winning an outright majority of the popular vote in a free national election.","a":false,"why":"He was not voted into office. The party behind him was the largest in the Reichstag after July 1932 but had never won a majority and lost ground in November, and it was President Paul von Hindenburg who appointed him chancellor on 30 January 1933, at the head of a coalition whose conservatives believed they could contain him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What followed was legal in form: a decree suspending civil rights, an election fought under it, and a law handing the power to legislate to the cabinet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","Michael Wildt, “Machteroberung 1933,” in <i>Nationalsozialismus: Aufstieg und Herrschaft</i>, Informationen zur politischen Bildung (Bonn: Bundeszentrale für politische Bildung, online 19 January 2022), https://www.bpb.de/izpb/137194/machteroberung-1933. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The famous gunfight associated with Tombstone, Arizona did not actually take place inside the O.K. Corral.","a":true,"why":"The National Park Service's own nomination for Tombstone gives the ground plan. “The corral offices were on Allen Street between Third and Fourth, but the lot ran through to Fremont Street on the north. It was in the open lot between Fly's Photo Studio and the Harwood House where the rustlers waited”; the Earps and Holliday walked down Fremont Street, “passed Fly's Studio”, turned left into the yard and confronted them there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thirty to sixty seconds and thirty-four shots later it was over — in “the vacant space west of Fly's photograph gallery”, as Wyatt Earp's own statement at the hearing has it,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and opposite Fly's lodging-house in the testimony Breakenridge printed, with Billy Clanton against “the little house just below Fly's building”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same document calls the fight “the gun fight at the OK Corral” two paragraphs earlier, which is the whole of the confusion: the name is where the lot led, not where the men stood.","cat":"Modern history","src":["Marilyn Larew, “Tombstone,” National Register of Historic Places inventory-nomination form (Washington, DC: Historic Sites Survey Division, National Park Service, February 1978), item 8, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000171_text. [Open access]","Wyatt S. Earp, statement in the preliminary hearing in the Earp–Holliday case before Judge Wells Spicer, Tombstone, 16 November 1881, transcribed at Douglas O. Linder, <i>Famous Trials</i>, https://famous-trials.com/earp/502-statement. [Open access]","William M. Breakenridge, <i>Helldorado: Bringing the Law to the Mesquite</i> (Boston: Houghton Mifflin, 1928), 147–53, https://archive.org/details/helldoradobringi00brea. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Observations of a total solar eclipse in 1919 measured the sun bending starlight by about the amount Einstein’s general relativity predicted.","a":true,"why":"Two expeditions photographed stars close to the sun during the eclipse of 29 May 1919, one at Sobral in Brazil and one on the island of Príncipe. The Sobral plates gave a deflection at the sun’s limb of 1.98 seconds of arc with a probable error of about 0.12, against the 1.75 that Einstein’s theory predicts, and the authors concluded that their results “appear to point quite definitely” to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same paper notes that the theory was already confirmed by the motion of Mercury’s perihelion, so the eclipse was not the first test it had passed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A centenary commentary on the paper calls the expedition a turning point in the history of relativity, as much for the public interest its success aroused as for the test itself,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the standard review of the theory's experimental standing still pairs the deflection of light with Mercury's perihelion as its two immediate confirmations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["F. W. Dyson, A. S. Eddington and C. Davidson, “A Determination of the Deflection of Light by the Sun’s Gravitational Field, from Observations Made at the Total Eclipse of May 29, 1919,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London A</i> 220 (1920): 291–333, https://archive.org/details/philtrans06337895. [Open access]","Malcolm Longair, “Bending Space-Time: A Commentary on Dyson, Eddington and Davidson (1920) ‘A Determination of the Deflection of Light by the Sun's Gravitational Field’,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society A</i> 373, no. 2039 (2015): 20140287, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsta.2014.0287. [Open access]","Clifford M. Will, “The Confrontation between General Relativity and Experiment,” <i>Living Reviews in Relativity</i> 17 (2014): 4, https://doi.org/10.12942/lrr-2014-4. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Thomas Edison invented the first electric light bulb.","a":false,"why":"British inventors had been demonstrating electric light with the arc lamp long before Edison, the first constant electric light was shown in 1835, and scientists worked on the incandescent lamp for the forty years after that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Edison patented his bulb in 1879 and again in 1880<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and made it commercially practical, but Joseph Swan patented one in England and Sawyer and Man held a United States patent of their own — the Edison and Sawyer–Man systems fought over it all the way to the Supreme Court in 1895<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> — so the lamp cannot be credited to a single inventor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Science & invention","src":["U.S. Department of Energy, “The History of the Light Bulb,” https://www.energy.gov/articles/history-light-bulb. [Open access]","<i>Consolidated Electric Light Co. v. McKeesport Light Co.</i>, 159 U.S. 465 (1895), Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School, https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/159/465. [Open access]","Thomas A. Edison, “Electric Lamp,” U.S. Patent 223,898, issued 27 January 1880, https://patents.google.com/patent/US223898A/en. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Albert Einstein failed mathematics as a schoolboy.","a":false,"why":"Einstein was studying mathematics, and the calculus in particular, from about 1891, when he was twelve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He did fail an entrance examination — the one for the ETH in Zürich — and went on to secondary school at Aarau to sit it again, graduating in 1900 as a teacher of mathematics and physics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Nobel Foundation's biography has him entering the Zürich Polytechnic in 1896 to be trained as a teacher of physics and mathematics,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the American Institute of Physics' account of his Munich schooldays has him “outstanding in mathematics” at a school he hated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Science & invention","src":["J J O’Connor and E F Robertson, “Albert Einstein,” <i>MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive</i>, University of St Andrews, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Einstein/. [Open access]","“Albert Einstein – Biographical,” <i>NobelPrize.org</i>, from <i>Nobel Lectures, Physics 1901–1921</i> (Amsterdam: Elsevier, 1967), https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/1921/einstein/biographical/. [Open access]","American Institute of Physics, Center for History of Physics, “Formative Years I,” <i>Einstein: Image and Impact</i>, online exhibit, https://history.aip.org/exhibits/einstein/early1.htm. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Isaac Newton served as Master of the Royal Mint and had counterfeiters prosecuted and executed.","a":true,"why":"Newton was appointed Warden of the Mint in 1696 and Master in 1699.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pursuing coiners was the Warden’s own business, and the most notorious of them, William Chaloner — who had answered his arrest by accusing Newton before a parliamentary committee of conniving at abuses at the Mint, a charge Newton answered point by point in a memorandum for the committee<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> — was hanged at Tyburn in March 1699.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Science & invention","src":["David Brewster, <i>Memoirs of the Life, Writings, and Discoveries of Sir Isaac Newton</i>, vol. 1 (Edinburgh: Edmonston and Douglas, 1860), 272–73, https://archive.org/details/memoirsoflifewri01brewuoft. [Open access]","David Brewster, <i>Memoirs of the Life, Writings, and Discoveries of Sir Isaac Newton</i>, vol. 2 (Edinburgh: Edmonston and Douglas, 1860), 144–48, https://archive.org/details/memoirsoflifewri02brewuoft. [Open access]","R. T. G., “Newton, Sir Isaac (1642–1727),” in <i>Dictionary of National Biography</i>, ed. Sidney Lee, vol. 40 (New York: Macmillan, 1894), 370–93, at 384, https://archive.org/details/dictionaryofnati40stepuoft. [Open access]","Isaac Newton, holograph summary of the submissions put to a committee examining Chaloner's case, MINT 19/1/503, National Archives, Kew, transcribed at <i>The Newton Project</i>, MINT00847, https://www.newtonproject.ox.ac.uk/view/texts/diplomatic/MINT00847. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Benjamin Franklin proved lightning was electrical by flying a kite that was struck by a lightning bolt.","a":false,"why":"Franklin’s own account of the 1752 kite, sent to Peter Collinson and read to the Royal Society that December,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> has a sharp wire at its top drawing “the electric fire” out of a thunder cloud, with the experimenter standing in a doorway so that the silk ribbon at the end of the twine stays dry — a charge collected, not a bolt received.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A direct strike would probably have killed him, as one killed Professor Richmann at Saint Petersburg, struck by a globe of blue fire that jumped from his apparatus to his head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Science & invention","src":["Benjamin Franklin, <i>New Experiments and Observations on Electricity, Made at Philadelphia in America</i>, 3rd ed. (London: D. Henry and R. Cave, 1760), 106–7, https://archive.org/details/newexperimentsob00fran. [Open access]","Joseph Priestley, <i>The History and Present State of Electricity, with Original Experiments</i>, 2nd ed. (London, 1769), 338–39, https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_the-history-and-present-_priestley-joseph_1769. [Open access]","Benjamin Franklin, “A Letter of Benjamin Franklin, Esq; to Mr. Peter Collinson, F.R.S. concerning an Electrical Kite,” read 21 December 1752, <i>Philosophical Transactions</i> 47 (1753): 565–67, https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_philosophical-transactio_royal-society-great-bri_1753_47. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Nikola Tesla's alternating-current system won the \"War of the Currents\" over Edison's direct-current system as the standard for power distribution.","a":true,"why":"Direct current is not easily converted to higher or lower voltages and alternating current is, through a transformer, which is what Tesla thought would settle it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Westinghouse, holding Tesla's polyphase patents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> undercut General Electric to electrify the 1893 Chicago World's Fair and then won the Niagara Falls contract; Buffalo was lit from the falls in 1896,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> General Electric came over to alternating current too, and alternating current still predominantly carries electricity today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Science & invention","src":["“The War of the Currents: AC vs. DC Power,” U.S. Department of Energy, https://www.energy.gov/articles/war-currents-ac-vs-dc-power. [Open access]","Nikola Tesla, “Electro-Magnetic Motor,” U.S. Patent 381,968, issued 1 May 1888, https://patents.google.com/patent/US381968A. [Open access]","James Gardner and Ralph J. Christian, “Adams Power Plant Transformer House,” National Register of Historic Places inventory-nomination form (Washington, DC: National Park Service, September 1978), https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/75001212_text. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Charles Darwin coined the phrase \"survival of the fittest\" in On the Origin of Species.","a":false,"why":"Darwin credits the expression to somebody else in his own text: “the expression often used by Mr. Herbert Spencer, of the Survival of the Fittest, is more accurate, and is sometimes equally convenient.”<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The phrase does not appear anywhere in the first edition of 1859 at all, and Darwin took it up only in the later ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Spencer had introduced the phrase in his <i>Principles of Biology</i> as his own mechanical name for what “Mr Darwin has called ‘natural selection’”,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and it was Wallace who, in a letter of July 1866, urged Darwin to adopt it in future editions of the <i>Origin</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","cat":"Science & invention","src":["Charles Darwin, <i>The Origin of Species by Means of Natural Selection</i>, 6th ed. (1872), ch. 3, Project Gutenberg eBook 2009, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2009/pg2009.txt. [Open access]","Charles Darwin, <i>On the Origin of Species by Means of Natural Selection</i>, 1st ed. (1859), Project Gutenberg eBook 1228, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1228/pg1228.txt. [Open access]","Herbert Spencer, <i>The Principles of Biology</i>, vol. 1 (New York: D. Appleton, 1898), §165, 444–45, https://archive.org/details/principlesofbiol01spen. [Open access]","Alfred Russel Wallace to Charles Darwin, 2 July 1866, <i>Darwin Correspondence Project</i>, letter DCP-LETT-5140, https://www.darwinproject.ac.uk/letter/DCP-LETT-5140.xml. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The mathematician who wrote the first published algorithm intended for a machine, often called the first computer programmer, was a woman.","a":true,"why":"Ada Lovelace's notes on Babbage's Analytical Engine, published in 1843 under the initials A. A. L., describe how the engine could be programmed and give what many consider the first computer program.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Babbage's own account complicates the credit<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>: he had offered to work the Bernoulli-number problem out to save her the trouble, and she sent it back to him having found a grave mistake in his process.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Science & invention","src":["J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Augusta Ada Byron King, Countess of Lovelace,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, August 2002, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Lovelace/. [Open access]","L. F. Menabrea, “Sketch of the Analytical Engine Invented by Charles Babbage, Esq.,” translated with notes by A. A. L. [Ada Lovelace], in <i>Scientific Memoirs</i>, ed. Richard Taylor, vol. 3 (London: Richard and John E. Taylor, 1843), 666–731, https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.53369. [Open access]","Charles Babbage, <i>Passages from the Life of a Philosopher</i> (London: Longman, Green, Longman, Roberts, & Green, 1864), ch. 8, 135–36, https://archive.org/details/passagesfromlife00babb. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"China was recruiting its officials by written competitive examination for centuries before any European state tried the method.","a":true,"why":"The imperial examination was the written competition through which the Chinese state recruited its officials, and for most of a thousand years it was the main road into government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Song edict of 1044 ordered a college in every prefecture and county, with an examination each autumn after three hundred days of study.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When W. A. P. Martin set the two side by side in 1901 he had to argue the case the other way about: England, France and Prussia had each begun using competitive examinations in some branches of their service, but 'in these countries the experiment is of recent date and of limited application'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["William Alexander Parsons Martin, <i>The Lore of Cathay; or, The Intellect of China</i> (New York: Fleming H. Revell, 1901), 312–14, https://archive.org/details/loreofcathay01mart. [Open access]","Henri Cordier, <i>Histoire générale de la Chine et de ses relations avec les pays étrangers</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Librairie Paul Geuthner, 1920), 100–101, https://archive.org/details/histoiregnra02cord. [Open access]","William Alexander Parsons Martin, <i>The Lore of Cathay; or, The Intellect of China</i> (New York: Fleming H. Revell, 1901), 308–9, https://archive.org/details/loreofcathay01mart. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>China and the Chinese</i> (New York: Columbia University Press, 1902), lecture 2, “The Chinese Language,” Project Gutenberg, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/18021. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Wu Zetian, who declared her own Zhou dynasty in 690 CE, was the only woman in Chinese history to rule as emperor in her own right rather than as a regent or consort.","a":true,"why":"The <i>Jiu Tangshu</i>’s annals of her reign put it on one day: on the ninth of the ninth month of 690 she revoked the Mandate of Tang, renamed the dynasty Zhou, took the honorific <i>Shengshen huangdi</i> — divine and sagely emperor — and demoted the reigning emperor to heir apparent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She held the imperial title until the first month of 705, when the same annals have her pass it to the crown prince and retire to the Shangyang Palace, where she died that winter at 83.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A study of the Bright Hall she built calls her, in its own words, “the only female emperor in China”,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and a survey of China's women rulers from Han to Qing has the rest governing as regents and her alone declaring herself emperor of her own dynasty, “the climax of the long history of women rulers”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["<i>Jiu Tangshu</i> [Old Book of Tang] 6, “Benji: Zetian huanghou” [Basic annals: Empress Zetian], transcribed at Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E8%88%8A%E5%94%90%E6%9B%B8/%E5%8D%B76. [in Chinese] [Open access]","Delu Wang, “Indra’s Palace on Mount Meru: A Study on the Design Philosophy of Wu Zetian’s Bright Hall,” <i>Religions</i> 15, no. 12 (2024): 1530, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel15121530. [Open access]","Keith McMahon, “Women Rulers in Imperial China,” <i>Nan Nü</i> 15, no. 2 (2013): 179–218, https://doi.org/10.1163/15685268-0152P0001. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"The earliest datable Chinese account of the magnetic compass is not about navigation at all.","a":true,"why":"It is a bench note. Shen Kuo's notebook of about 1088 is the first description that can be dated and read: he rubs a needle on a lodestone and mounts it four ways — floating on water, balanced on a fingernail or a bowl's rim, and best of all hung in still air from a single filament of waxed silk — finds that it settles a little east of south, which is the first record anywhere of magnetic declination, and adds that nobody can fathom why the stone points at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The needle reaches the sea in the record thirty years later, when Zhu Yu wrote that the pilots of the Guangzhou merchant ships read the stars at night and the sun by day, and in overcast weather looked at the south-pointing needle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Western scholars have had the text since Klaproth laid Shen Kuo's passage and the Song evidence for declination before Humboldt in 1834,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and Wylie's survey of the compass in China likewise rests its eleventh-century date on the same page of the <i>Mengxi bitan</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Shen Kuo, <i>Mengxi bitan</i>, juan 24 (c. 1088), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E5%A4%A2%E6%BA%AA%E7%AD%86%E8%AB%87/%E5%8D%B724. [Open access]","Zhu Yu, <i>Pingzhou ketan</i>, juan 2 (1119), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E8%90%8D%E6%B4%B2%E5%8F%AF%E8%AB%87/%E5%8D%B7%E4%BA%8C. [Open access]","Julius Klaproth, <i>Lettre à M. le baron A. de Humboldt sur l'invention de la boussole</i> (Paris: Librairie orientale de Prosper Dondey-Dupré, 1834), 66–69, https://archive.org/details/lettremlebaronad00klap. [Open access]","Alexander Wylie, “The Magnetic Compass in China,” in <i>Chinese Researches</i> (Shanghai, 1897), pt. 3, 155–58, https://archive.org/details/chineseresearche00wyliuoft. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Fortune cookies, served in American Chinese restaurants, actually originated in China and were brought over by early immigrants.","a":false,"why":"The standard account of how American Chinese food was made says it plainly: there were no such things as fortune cookies in China, and hardly any American customer knew it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cookie descends from the Japanese <i>tsujiura senbei</i>, sold in California by Japanese immigrants in the early 20th century, among them the Hagiwara family of San Francisco's Japanese Tea Garden and the Benkyodo confectionery that baked for them;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> production passed to Chinese American businesses during the Second World War, when the Japanese confectioners were interned and their shops closed, which is how it came to end every meal in a Chinese restaurant and no meal in China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Haiming Liu, “Chop Suey as Imagined Authentic Chinese Food: The Culinary Identity of Chinese Restaurants in the United States,” <i>Journal of Transnational American Studies</i> 1, no. 1 (2009), https://doi.org/10.5070/T811006946. [Open access]","Gary Ono, “Japanese American Fortune Cookie: A Taste of Fame or Fortune – Part 1,” <i>Discover Nikkei</i>, Japanese American National Museum, 31 October 2007, https://discovernikkei.org/en/journal/2007/10/31/fortune-cookie/. [Open access]","Gary Ono, “Japanese American Fortune Cookie: A Taste of Fame or Fortune – Part 2,” <i>Discover Nikkei</i>, Japanese American National Museum, 1 November 2007, https://discovernikkei.org/en/journal/2007/11/01/fortune-cookie/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Measurements above the still-sealed tomb of China's first emperor have found mercury far above the local background, matching an ancient text that describes rivers of mercury inside.","a":true,"why":"They have. Laser radar above the mound reads atmospheric mercury of up to 27 nanograms a cubic metre against a local background of 5 to 10, apparently leaking up through cracks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The text is Sima Qian's, written a century after the burial: with mercury they made the hundred watercourses, the Yangtze, the Yellow River and the wide sea, and machinery kept it flowing, with the signs of heaven above and the whole arrangement of the earth below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A 2017 soil survey of the mausoleum and its surroundings found mercury ranging from about 41 to 2,248 nanograms a gram, a fifty-fold spread across the site, while the groundwater stayed within the World Health Organization's drinking-water limit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Guangyu Zhao, Weixing Zhang, Zheng Duan, Ming Lian, Ningbin Hou et al., “Mercury as a Geophysical Tracer Gas: Emissions from the Emperor Qin Tomb in Xi'an Studied by Laser Radar,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 10414, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-67305-x. [Open access]","Sima Qian, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, trans. Édouard Chavannes, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), 193–94, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","Lifeng Gao, “Evaluation of Mercury Environmental Pollution Based on Luminescence Analysis,” <i>Chemical Engineering Transactions</i> 59 (2017): 913–18, https://doi.org/10.3303/CET1759153. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The world's first government-issued paper money was printed in China during the Song dynasty, centuries before banknotes appeared in Europe.","a":true,"why":"Sichuan used iron coin so heavy for its value that traders wrote bills against it instead and called them jiaozi; when the sixteen families issuing them could no longer redeem what they had promised, the Song government opened an office at Yizhou to issue the bills itself and forbade anyone else to make them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A note office followed in the capital in 970, and by 998 some 1.7 million strings of cash were circulating in paper<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> — Morse has 1,700,000 strings of government notes outstanding in 997 and credits Zhang Yong with the Sichuan bills that replaced the iron coin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Willem Vissering, <i>On Chinese Currency: Coin and Paper Money</i> (Leiden: E. J. Brill, 1877), 167–69, https://archive.org/details/onchinesecurrenc00vissuoft. [Open access]","Thomas Francis Carter, <i>The Invention of Printing in China and Its Spread Westward</i> (New York: Columbia University Press, 1925), 72–74, https://archive.org/details/carter-invention-printing-china. [Open access]","Hosea Ballou Morse, <i>The Trade and Administration of the Chinese Empire</i> (London: Longmans, Green, 1908), 133–34, https://archive.org/details/tradeadministra00mors. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Dishes like chop suey and General Tso's chicken that anchor American Chinese menus are traditional recipes brought directly from China.","a":false,"why":"There is a real Chinese dish behind the name — <i>chao zasui</i>, stir-fried animal intestines, which the mostly Cantonese first immigrants pronounced in a way English ears took for “chop suey” — Wong Chin Foo described “chow chop svey”, a stew of chickens’ livers and gizzards, fungi, bamboo buds, tripe and bean sprouts, to New York readers in 1888.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What Americans then ate under that name was something else: “what Americans came to know as chop suey, General Tso's chicken, and egg foo young were very different from their Chinese counterparts”, and “high-class restaurants in China seldom served chop suey”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The dish that made Chinese restaurants in America was an American invention, and it was carried back to China in the 1940s as something Chinese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 1898 a guide to New York’s Chinatown was calling chop suey “a standard dish” to which he had introduced many a friend, and watching their faces as it arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Haiming Liu, “Chop Suey as Imagined Authentic Chinese Food: The Culinary Identity of Chinese Restaurants in the United States,” <i>Journal of Transnational American Studies</i> 1, no. 1 (2009), https://doi.org/10.5070/T811006946. [Open access]","Wong Chin Foo, “The Chinese in New York,” <i>The Cosmopolitan</i> 5, no. 4 (June 1888): 304, https://archive.org/details/sim_cosmopolitan_1888-06_5_4. [Open access]","Louis J. Beck, <i>New York’s Chinatown: An Historical Presentation of Its People and Places</i> (New York: Bohemia Publishing Company, 1898), “Various Views of Life and Character in Chinatown,” https://archive.org/details/cu31924023507217. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"In the early 1400s Admiral Zheng He commanded a Ming fleet whose largest ships and sheer numbers dwarfed anything Christopher Columbus would later sail.","a":true,"why":"The <i>History of Ming</i> puts more than 27,800 officers and men aboard the first voyage of 1405, and says 62 great ships were built for it, each 44 <i>zhang</i> long and 18 broad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Columbus sailed in 1492 with three, of which the largest was the ship he never names in his own journal, the other two being the caravels <i>Pinta</i> and <i>Niña</i> out of Palos<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> — Peter Martyr's first Decade has the same three, one decked and two undecked caravels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A fleet of 62 purpose-built ships and 27,800 men is of another order from three vessels and their crews, even though whether any ship could really have been 44 <i>zhang</i> long is a question scholars have examined closely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["<i>Mingshi</i> [History of Ming] 304, “Huanguan zhuan: Zheng He” [Biographies of the eunuchs: Zheng He], transcribed at Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E6%98%8E%E5%8F%B2/%E5%8D%B7304. [in Chinese] [Open access]","Christopher Columbus, <i>The Journal of Christopher Columbus (During His First Voyage, 1492–93)</i>, trans. Clements R. Markham (London: Hakluyt Society, 1893), 17n, 21, https://archive.org/details/journalofchristo00colu_0. [Open access]","Sally K. Church, “Zheng He: An Investigation into the Plausibility of 450-ft Treasure Ships,” <i>Monumenta Serica</i> 53 (2005): 1–43, https://doi.org/10.1179/mon.2005.53.1.001. [Paywalled]","Peter Martyr d'Anghera, <i>De Orbe Novo: The Eight Decades of Peter Martyr d'Anghera</i>, trans. Francis Augustus MacNutt, vol. 1 (New York: G. P. Putnam's Sons, 1912), 57–58, https://archive.org/details/deorbenovoeightd01angh. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Confucius was a celebrated and influential figure across China during his own lifetime.","a":false,"why":"The offices he first held in Lu asked only that he keep his accounts straight and see that the oxen and sheep on the public fields were fat; he rose to be minister of Crime there and was never chief minister of the state, though it has often been said he was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What made his learning the one learning worth a court's patronage was a Han decision centuries after his death, taken along with the withdrawal of that patronage from every rival school.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Friedrich Hirth, <i>The Ancient History of China to the End of the Chóu Dynasty</i> (New York: Columbia University Press, 1908), 228–31, https://archive.org/details/ancienthistoryc00hirtgoog. [Open access]","James Legge, prolegomena to <i>Confucian Analects, The Great Learning, and The Doctrine of the Mean</i>, 2nd ed., The Chinese Classics 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1893), 1–2, 14–16, 48–49, 56–90, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg. [Open access]","Yutong Liu, “On the Supremacy of Confucianism and the Periodization of Confucian Classics Learning in the Han Dynasty,” <i>Asian Studies</i> 12, no. 1 (2024): 87–112, https://doi.org/10.4312/as.2024.12.1.87-112. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Bi Sheng invented movable-type printing in China around the 1040s, roughly four centuries before Johannes Gutenberg built his press in Europe.","a":true,"why":"In the Qingli period of 1041 to 1049 a commoner named Bi Sheng cut the first movable type anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The account is Shen Kuo's, set down within forty years: characters cut in sticky clay, each as thin as the rim of a coin and fired hard, packed into an iron frame on a paste of pine resin, wax and paper ash.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gutenberg's Bible, which the Library of Congress calls the first great book printed in Western Europe from movable type, was probably completed late in 1455 — four centuries on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Thomas Francis Carter, <i>The Invention of Printing in China and Its Spread Westward</i> (New York: Columbia University Press, 1925), 159–61, https://archive.org/details/carter-invention-printing-china. [Open access]","Shen Kuo, <i>Mengxi bitan</i>, juan 18 (c. 1088), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E5%A4%A2%E6%BA%AA%E7%AD%86%E8%AB%87/%E5%8D%B718. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “The Gutenberg Bible,” <i>Bible Collection</i>, exhibition, https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/bibles/the-gutenberg-bible.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Cesarean sections are named after Julius Caesar because he was delivered by one, which is why his mother did not survive the birth.","a":false,"why":"The name goes back to an ancestor rather than to Julius: Pliny says the first of the Caesars was so called from his having been removed by an incision in his mother's womb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> And Caesar's own mother Aurelia was alive and running his household when he was praetor in his late thirties — Plutarch has her keeping watch over his wife, and putting a stop to the rites Clodius broke into,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and Suetonius has her give evidence at Clodius' trial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The National Library of Medicine's history of the operation reaches the same verdict: the derivation from Caesar's birth “seems unlikely since his mother Aurelia is reputed to have lived to hear of her son's invasion of Britain”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> 7.7, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0137%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Caesar</i> 9–10, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library 7 (1919), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Caesar*.html. [Open access]","U.S. National Library of Medicine, “Cesarean Section – A Brief History: Part 1,” https://www.nlm.nih.gov/exhibition/cesarean/part1.html. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Divus Julius</i> 74, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Julius*.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Ancient Greek marble statues and temples, which we picture as pure white, were originally painted in bright, vivid colours.","a":true,"why":"Imaging of the Parthenon sculptures in the British Museum found <i>Egyptian blue</i> used extensively, painting elaborate figurative designs onto the carved drapery, together with a purple colourant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sculptors had already worked the marble into textures standing for skin, wool and linen, and the colour went on over those; the study’s own conclusion is that the fifth-century appearance of the building needs reconsidering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ancients took the painting for granted — Pliny has Praxiteles, asked which of his marbles pleased him best, answer “those to which Nicias has set his hand”, Nicias being a painter<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> — and forty years of research on the polychromy of Greek and Roman sculpture at the Liebieghaus in Frankfurt has produced the colour reconstructions shown worldwide as <i>Gods in Color</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Giovanni Verri, Hero Granger-Taylor, Ian Jenkins, Tracey Sweek, Katarzyna Weglowska and William Thomas Wootton, “The goddess’ new clothes: the carving and polychromy of the Parthenon Sculptures,” <i>Antiquity</i> 97, no. 395 (2023): 1173–92, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2023.130. [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> 35.40, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0137%3Abook%3D35%3Achapter%3D40. [Open access]","Liebieghaus Skulpturensammlung, “Liebieghaus Polychromy Research Project,” https://www.liebieghaus.de/en/polychromy-research. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Cleopatra, the last ruler of Egypt's Ptolemaic dynasty, was descended from Macedonian Greeks rather than being ethnically native Egyptian.","a":true,"why":"Ptolemaic Egypt was governed from Alexandria by the descendants of Ptolemy son of Lagus, from the end of the 4th century BCE until Rome annexed it in 30 BCE, and the last of that line was Cleopatra; government was Greek-speaking and the settler class held the land and the offices, while the villages went on being run in Egyptian by Egyptian scribes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo runs the line from Alexander's successor Ptolemy son of Lagus down to Auletes, “the father of Cleopatra”,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and Pausanias records that the Macedonians held that first Ptolemy to be a son of Philip of Macedon, whatever Lagus's name on him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Jona Lendering, “Ptolemies,” Livius.org, last modified 2020, https://www.livius.org/articles/dynasty/ptolemies/. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 17.1.11, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D17%3Achapter%3D1%3Asection%3D11. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.6.2, trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The idea that a Roman crowd gave a defeated gladiator 'thumbs down' to signal his death is a well-documented fact from ancient sources.","a":false,"why":"The Latin is <i>verso pollice</i>, a turned thumb, and no ancient writer says which way it turned. Juvenal has the mob order a killing “when the thumbs of the rabble are turned up” — the direction there is his Victorian translator's, not the poet's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The only nearby remark in Pliny points the other way: “when we wish to signify applause, we have a proverb even which tells us we should press the thumbs”, and it is again the translator's own footnote, not Pliny, that adds “the thumb was turned upwards as a mark of favour, downwards, as a mark of disfavour”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The familiar image comes from Gérôme's 1872 painting, and the museum that owns it says plainly that “there are no historical records that confirm” the thumbs-down reading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Juvenal, <i>Satire</i> 3, in <i>The Satires of Juvenal, Persius, Sulpicia, and Lucilius</i>, trans. Lewis Evans, Harper's New Classical Library (New York: Harper & Brothers, 1881), https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/50657. [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> 28.5, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley, vol. 5 (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1856), https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/60688. [Open access]","Phoenix Art Museum, “Pollice Verso,” museum blog, 28 August 2024, https://phxart.org/blog/pollice-verso/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Archaeological evidence indicates Roman gladiators ate a largely plant-based, grain-heavy diet and were nicknamed 'barley-eaters.'","a":true,"why":"Bone chemistry from the gladiator cemetery at Ephesus, 22 gladiators among 53 people measured, found wheat and barley the staple of everyone buried there, with nitrogen values low enough that the study puts it down to a great deal of pulse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What set the gladiators apart was strontium: their ratios were significantly higher than their neighbours’, which the authors read as the plant-ash drink the ancient texts describe, and those same texts are where the nickname <i>hordearii</i>, barley eaters, comes from<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> — Pliny says it was the name “that used to be given to gladiators”, barley being among the oldest of foods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The cemetery itself, the first gladiator mass grave to get a full forensic examination, had already yielded the healed and fatal head wounds of at least 68 people, all but two of them men of twenty to thirty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Sandra Lösch, Negahnaz Moghaddam, Karl Grossschmidt, Daniele U. Risser and Fabian Kanz, “Stable Isotope and Trace Element Studies on Gladiators and Contemporary Romans from Ephesus (Turkey, 2nd and 3rd Ct. AD)—Implications for Differences in Diet,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 10 (2014): e110489, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0110489. [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> 18.14, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0137%3Abook%3D18%3Achapter%3D14. [Open access]","Fabian Kanz and Karl Grossschmidt, “Head Injuries of Roman Gladiators,” <i>Forensic Science International</i> 160, no. 2–3 (2006): 207–16, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.forsciint.2005.10.010. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"The Babylonians understood the relationship later called the Pythagorean theorem more than a thousand years before Pythagoras was born.","a":true,"why":"The tablets are Old Babylonian, of roughly 1900 to 1700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A round school tablet at Yale carries a square with both diagonals drawn and an accurate sexagesimal approximation of the square root of 2 written along one of them, so its scribe is using the relation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plimpton 322 goes further and shows a method for generating Pythagorean triples, whole-number sides of right triangles, a millennium and a half before Euclid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Institute for the Study of the Ancient World, “Before Pythagoras: The Culture of Old Babylonian Mathematics,” exhibition, New York University, 2010, https://isaw.nyu.edu/exhibitions/before-pythagoras/index.html. [Open access]","Institute for the Study of the Ancient World, “Tablet Illustrating Pythagoras’ Theorem and the Square Root of 2,” YBC 7289, Yale Babylonian Collection, https://isaw.nyu.edu/exhibitions/before-pythagoras/items/ybc-7289/. [Open access]","Institute for the Study of the Ancient World, “Table of Whole-Number Sides of Pythagorean Triangles,” Plimpton 322, Columbia University, https://isaw.nyu.edu/exhibitions/before-pythagoras/items/plimpton-322/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Emperor Nero famously played a fiddle while watching Rome burn in 64 CE.","a":false,"why":"Two things are wrong with it. Tacitus gives the singing only as a rumour that went out everywhere — that Nero sang of the destruction of Troy on a private stage while the city burned — and says it is uncertain whether the fire itself was accidental or contrived, since authors have given both accounts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> And the instrument is wrong: Suetonius has him in stage costume on the tower of Maecenas, while Dio describes him performing as a singer to the lyre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Tacitus, <i>Annals</i> 15.38-40, trans. Alfred John Church and William Jackson Brodribb (New York: Random House, 1942), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0078%3Abook%3D15%3Achapter%3D38. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Nero</i> 31, 38, 49, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Nero*.html. [Open access]","Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i> 62.12-18, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/62*.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Great Library of Alexandria was destroyed in a single catastrophic fire that wiped out ancient knowledge overnight.","a":false,"why":"The ancient record describes nothing so tidy. Aulus Gellius says nearly 700,000 volumes were burned during the sack of the city in the first war with Alexandria, and that it was done neither deliberately nor by anyone's order but accidentally, by auxiliary soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ammianus repeats the figure but puts the libraries in the Serapeum rather than the palace quarter, and Plutarch says only that Caesar, forced to use fire against the enemy fleet, watched it spread from the dockyards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The three do not agree about where the books stood or how they came to be lost.","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Aulus Gellius, <i>Attic Nights</i> 7.17, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Gellius/7*.html#17. [Open access]","Ammianus Marcellinus, <i>Res Gestae</i> 22.16.13, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1940), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Ammian/22*.html#16. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lives: Caesar</i> 49, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0244:chapter=49. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Under Emperor Titus, Rome's Colosseum was flooded so that mock naval battles with real ships could be staged inside it.","a":true,"why":"Cassius Dio, describing the games with which Titus opened the amphitheatre in 80 CE, records the arena flooded for a sea-fight, alongside cranes made to fight and four elephants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Suetonius’ account of the same hundred days has five thousand wild beasts of every kind shown in a single day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Martial, writing for the games, tells a spectator newly come from abroad not to be deceived by the ships and the water, for “here, awhile since, was the dry land”,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and the fullest modern study of these aquatic shows holds that Dio's awkward details are not to be dismissed out of hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i> 66.25, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/66*.html#25. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Divus Titus</i> 7, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Titus*.html#7. [Open access]","Martial, <i>On the Spectacles</i> 24 and 28, in <i>The Epigrams of Martial</i>, Bohn's Classical Library (London: George Bell & Sons, 1897), 18–19, https://archive.org/details/epigramsmartial00bohngoog. [Open access]","K. M. Coleman, “Launching into History: Aquatic Displays in the Early Empire,” <i>Journal of Roman Studies</i> 83 (1993): 48–74, https://doi.org/10.2307/300978. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"The Emperor Constantine made Christianity the official state religion of the Roman Empire with his Edict of Milan in 313 CE.","a":false,"why":"The letter Constantine and Licinius issued from Milan, which Lactantius quotes entire, gave Christians liberty to follow their own worship undisturbed — and granted the same liberty to everyone else in the same breath, so it opened the empire to every religion rather than making one of them the religion of state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That came later: in 380 an edict of Gratian, Valentinian and Theodosius ordered every people under their rule to hold the faith Peter gave the Romans, and judged everyone else a heretic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Lactantius, <i>Of the Manner in Which the Persecutors Died</i> 48, trans. William Fletcher, in <i>Ante-Nicene Fathers</i>, vol. 7, ed. Alexander Roberts, James Donaldson and A. Cleveland Coxe (Buffalo, NY: Christian Literature Publishing, 1886), https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/0705.htm. [Open access]","<i>Codex Theodosianus</i> 16.1.2, edict of Gratian, Valentinian and Theodosius to the people of Constantinople, dated at Thessalonica in the consulship of Gratian V and Theodosius I, Latin Wikisource, https://la.wikisource.org/wiki/Codex_Theodosianus/Liber_XVI. [Open access]","<i>Codex Theodosianus</i> 16.1.2, trans. in Henry Bettenson, ed., <i>Documents of the Christian Church</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1943), 31, in Internet Medieval Sourcebook, ed. Paul Halsall, Fordham University, https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/halsall/source/theodcodeXVI.asp. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Mauryan emperor Ashoka renounced war and embraced Buddhism after being horrified by the slaughter he caused in his conquest of Kalinga.","a":true,"why":"Rock Edict 13 is the king's own account of the conquest — 150,000 deported, 100,000 killed and many more dead of other causes — and of the “deep remorse” it left him with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What followed is in the edicts too: officers appointed to work among all religions and for the proper treatment of prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The figures stand the same in Hultzsch's standard edition of the Kalsi text,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and it was his last war of conquest: nothing in the record suggests Ashoka ever again waged an aggressive war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Rock Edict 13, in \"The Edicts of King Asoka,\" an English rendering by Ven. S. Dhammika, Wheel Publication 386/387 (Kandy: Buddhist Publication Society, 1993), Access to Insight (BCBS Edition), 30 November 2013, https://accesstoinsight.org/lib/authors/dhammika/wheel386.html. [Open access]","Rock Edict 5, in \"The Edicts of King Asoka,\" an English rendering by Ven. S. Dhammika, Wheel Publication 386/387 (Kandy: Buddhist Publication Society, 1993), Access to Insight (BCBS Edition), 30 November 2013, https://accesstoinsight.org/lib/authors/dhammika/wheel386.html. [Open access]","Vincent A. Smith, <i>Asoka: The Buddhist Emperor of India</i>, 3rd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1920), 25–26, https://archive.org/details/asokabuddhistemp00smit. [Open access]","E. Hultzsch, ed. and trans., <i>Inscriptions of Asoka</i>, Corpus Inscriptionum Indicarum, vol. 1, new ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1925), Kalsi rock-edict XIII, translation, §§A–B, https://archive.org/details/dli.csl.8684. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Silkworm eggs were smuggled into the Byzantine Empire by monks, freeing it from buying raw silk through Persia.","a":true,"why":"Procopius records the eggs reaching Constantinople about 552, brought by monks who said they had spent a long time in Serinda, a country beyond India.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the story does not show is a Chinese secret stolen: Serinda is a name the sources use for China, but the raw silk of the day was handled by Sogdian merchants, and the worms were more probably brought from Sogdiana than from China itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gibbon retold it with the eggs hidden in a hollow cane and the monks made Persians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Katarzyna Maksymiuk, “‘Industrial espionage’ of Justinian I (527–565),” <i>Historia i Świat</i> 10 (2021): 445–53, https://doi.org/10.34739/his.2021.10.26. [Open access]","Procopius, <i>History of the Wars</i> 8.17.1–8, trans. H. B. Dewing, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 5 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1928), 227–29, LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Procopius/Wars/8C*.html. [Open access]","Edward Gibbon, <i>The History of the Decline and Fall of the Roman Empire</i>, ch. 40, ed. H. H. Milman, Project Gutenberg ebook 25717, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/25717. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Medieval knights in full plate armour were so weighed down that they had to be hoisted onto their horses with a crane.","a":false,"why":"The weight of plate armour was less felt than that of mail, because plate was distributed over the whole body and limbs where mail hung from the shoulders and waist alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Robert de Vere escaped from Radcot Bridge by swimming the river in full armour, Oliver de la Marche describes a knight leaping clear out of his saddle fully armed in 1446, and Shakespeare's Henry V speaks of vaulting into the saddle with his armour on his back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A fifteenth-century field harness weighed some 30 to 50 kilograms (66 to 110 pounds), spread over the whole body,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the Metropolitan Museum's own handbook put a complete suit at 23 to 45 kilograms (50 to 100 pounds), lightest in proportion to its protection in the years 1450 to 1500.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Medieval & early modern","src":["Charles ffoulkes, <i>Armour &amp; Weapons</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1909), 64–65, https://archive.org/details/armourweapons00ffou. [Open access]","Graham N. Askew, Federico Formenti, and Alberto E. Minetti, “Limitations imposed by wearing armour on Medieval soldiers’ locomotor performance,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 279, no. 1729 (2012): 640–44, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3248716/. [Open access]","Bashford Dean, <i>Handbook of Arms and Armor, European and Oriental, Including the William H. Riggs Collection</i> (New York: Metropolitan Museum of Art, 1915), 107, https://archive.org/details/handbookofarms1915metr. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Making prisoners 'walk the plank' was a routine execution method among real pirates.","a":false,"why":"The fullest contemporary account of the great age of piracy is Captain Charles Johnson's <i>General History of the Pyrates</i>, published in London in 1724 while several of the men in it were still being hanged. It is not squeamish: it has marooning, it calls its subjects barbarous more than twenty times, and it has a captured master “cruelly whipp'd naked about the Deck, making his Torture their Sport; after which they cut of his Ears, and last of all shot him through the Head”. The word “plank” does not appear in it once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The phrase is first recorded in Grose's slang dictionary of 1788, as a trick of mutineers for dodging a charge of murder,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the pirate image belongs to the next century — Jim Hawkins hears tales “about hanging, and walking the plank” on the first page of <i>Treasure Island</i> — and after that to the cinema.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Medieval & early modern","src":["Charles Johnson, <i>A General History of the Pyrates</i>, 2nd ed. (London: T. Warner, 1724), https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/40580. [Open access]","Francis Grose, <i>A Classical Dictionary of the Vulgar Tongue</i>, 2nd ed. (London: S. Hooper, 1788), s.v. “Walking the Plank,” https://archive.org/details/aclassicaldicti00grosgoog. [Open access]","Robert Louis Stevenson, <i>Treasure Island</i> (1883), ch. 1, Project Gutenberg ebook 120, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/120/pg120.txt. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The University of Oxford was already teaching students before the Aztec capital of Tenochtitlan was founded.","a":true,"why":"Oxford is one of the handful of places already answering to the name of a university in the second half of the 12th century — Paris, Bologna, Salerno and Oxford were nearly the whole of the list.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Masters were there earlier still: Theobald of Étampes was styling himself “Magister Oxenefordiae” in the early years of the twelfth century, and a contemporary credited him with sixty or a hundred pupils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tenochtitlan grew on its island in the lakes of the Mexican basin only from about 1375, and the empire it led was consolidated around 1430.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Medieval & early modern","src":["Hastings Rashdall, <i>The Universities of Europe in the Middle Ages</i>, vol. 2, pt. 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 3–5, https://archive.org/details/universitieseur05rashgoog. [Open access]","Diego Matadamas-Gomora, Jason Nesbitt, Rodolfo Aguilar Tapia, Leonardo López Luján, Julia Sjödahl, Tatsuya Murakami, and Alejandro Pastrana, “Compositional analysis of obsidian artifacts from the Templo Mayor of Tenochtitlan, capital of the Mexica (Aztec) Empire,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 20 (2025): e2500095122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12107162/. [Open access]","Charles Edward Mallet, <i>A History of the University of Oxford</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Mediaeval University and the Colleges Founded in the Middle Ages</i> (London: Methuen, 1924), 20–21, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.222205. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Mongol Empire was the largest contiguous land empire in all of history.","a":true,"why":"At its peak around 1270 the Mongol Empire covered about 24 million square kilometres (9.3 million square miles), the area Taagepera's survey of large polities and Turchin's later table of “mega-empires” both give it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A survey of sixty-two historical empires puts that first for contiguous territory, ahead of the Russian Empire at its 1895 height — 22.8 million square kilometres (8.8 million square miles), and “the next largest state in history after the Mongols”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The European maritime empires were larger in total but were “widely distributed collections of territories” rather than one connected block, which is why the same study leaves them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Medieval & early modern","src":["Peter Turchin, Jonathan M. Adams, and Thomas D. Hall, “East-West Orientation of Historical Empires and Modern States,” <i>Journal of World-Systems Research</i> 12, no. 2 (2006): 219–29, https://doi.org/10.5195/jwsr.2006.369. [Open access]","Rein Taagepera, “Expansion and Contraction Patterns of Large Polities: Context for Russia,” <i>International Studies Quarterly</i> 41, no. 3 (1997): 475–504, at 482, https://escholarship.org/content/qt3cn68807/qt3cn68807.pdf. [Open access]","Peter Turchin, “A theory for formation of large empires,” <i>Journal of Global History</i> 4, no. 2 (2009): 191–217, Table 2, 200–201, https://peterturchin.com/publications/a-theory-for-formation-of-large-empires/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The official records of the 1666 Great Fire of London list only a handful of deaths.","a":true,"why":"The bills of mortality were the official count — compiled by the Company of Parish Clerks and reported to the Crown — and the bill for the year of the Fire, 19 December 1665 to 18 December 1666, gives 11 deaths for the whole of London under “Burnt and Scalded”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That heading covers every scald and household fire in the city across the entire twelvemonth, so whatever the Fire itself contributed is some part of those 11, on a bill whose plague column for the same year still stands at 1,998.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What a bill counts is what the parish clerks were told of, which is what the statement says and all it says: the official record of the Fire’s dead is a handful, not that a handful is certainly all who died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Walter Besant, writing in 1903, passed on the reputed toll of six and doubted it,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and John Evelyn, walking the ruins on 7 September, noted the stench of “some poor creatures’ bodies” that no clerk had counted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Medieval & early modern","src":["<i>A Collection of the Yearly Bills of Mortality, from 1657 to 1758 Inclusive</i> (London: A. Millar, 1759), the yearly bill for 19 December 1665 to 18 December 1666, https://archive.org/details/b30418124. [Open access]","Walter Besant, <i>London in the Time of the Stuarts</i> (London: Adam & Charles Black, 1903), Project Gutenberg, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/59782. [Open access]","John Evelyn, <i>The Diary of John Evelyn</i>, ed. William Bray, vol. 2 (Washington and London: M. Walter Dunne, 1901), 7 September 1666, Project Gutenberg, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/42081. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Marie Antoinette responded to news that peasants had no bread by saying 'Let them eat cake.'","a":false,"why":"The line is Rousseau’s. Stuck for bread to go with some wine he had helped himself to, he recalls in the <i>Confessions</i> “the thoughtless saying of a great princess, who, on being informed that the country people had no bread, replied, ‘Then let them eat pastry!’” — <i>brioche</i> in his French — and he names nobody; the scene belongs to his years tutoring the children of the provost of Lyons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Marie Antoinette was born in Vienna on 2 November 1755 and did not reach the French court until her marriage at Versailles on 16 May 1770.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rousseau was writing the <i>Confessions</i> in 1764–6,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> when the archduchess was a child of ten in Vienna; Madame Campan, her first lady of the bedchamber, likewise gives her birth as 2 November 1755 and her arrival at the French court as the spring of 1770.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["Jean-Jacques Rousseau, <i>The Confessions of Jean Jacques Rousseau</i>, book 6 (London: privately printed for the members of the Aldus Society, 1903), https://www.gutenberg.org/files/3913/3913-h/3913-h.htm. [Open access]","Château de Versailles, “Marie Antoinette, Queen of France 1755–1793,” https://en.chateauversailles.fr/discover/history/great-characters/marie-antoinette. [Open access]","Madame Campan, <i>Memoirs of the Court of Marie Antoinette, Queen of France</i>, chs. 2–3, Project Gutenberg ebook 3891, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3891/pg3891.txt. [Open access]","Christopher Bertram, “Jean Jacques Rousseau,” <i>Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, revised 21 April 2023, https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rousseau/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Napoleon's soldiers shot off the nose of the Great Sphinx with cannon fire during his 1798 Egyptian campaign.","a":false,"why":"The nose had been gone for decades already. Norden's <i>Voyage d’Égypte et de Nubie</i>, published in 1755, after his death, describes coming down from the second pyramid to the Sphinx and admiring its enormous size while feeling an indignation towards whoever had the brutality to maltreat its nose so strangely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Earlier still, the Dominican traveller Vansleb, in Egypt in 1672–3, reported the nose “broken by a Moor”, and the Arab historians quoted by Selim Hassan name a sheikh who mutilated it in the belief that it would please God.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is more than forty years before the French landed, so whoever took it, it was not Napoleon's gunners. The French expedition’s own savants printed the account of Abd al-Latif, who about 1200 had admired the just proportions of the nose on a face still coloured red.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["Frederic Louis Norden, <i>Voyage d’Égypte et de Nubie</i>, vol. 1 (Copenhagen: De l’imprimerie de la Maison royale des orphelins, 1755), 85, https://archive.org/details/gri_33125008497659. [Open access]","Selim Hassan, <i>The Sphinx: Its History in the Light of Recent Excavations</i> (Cairo: Government Press, 1949), 83, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.515700. [Open access]","Edme-François Jomard, “Remarques et recherches sur les pyramides d’Égypte,” in <i>Description de l’Égypte</i>, 2nd ed., vol. 9, <i>Antiquités, mémoires et descriptions</i> (Paris: C. L. F. Panckoucke, 1829), 474, https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_iXEOhrJzenIC. [in French] [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Catherine the Great of Russia died in a bizarre accident while attempting to have sex with a horse.","a":false,"why":"Memoirs published the year after her death give the morning in detail. She was cheerful and took her coffee as usual; she then went to the water closet, stayed an unusual time, and her attendants opened the door to find the Empress “extended on her back, with her feet towards the door, in an apoplectic fit”. Her physician bled her twice, she never spoke again, and she was pronounced dead the following evening, in November 1796; the post-mortem found two gallstones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no horse anywhere in the account, nor in Tooke's life of 1799 or Masson's memoirs of 1800, which tell it the same way — the closet, the attendants finding her senseless on the floor, the physicians bleeding her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["<i>Authentic Memoirs of the Life and Reign of Catherine II, Empress of All the Russias</i> (1797), 282–83, https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_authentic-memoirs-of-the_1797. [Open access]","William Tooke, <i>The Life of Catharine II, Empress of Russia</i>, 3rd ed., vol. 3 (London: T. N. Longman and O. Rees, 1799), 471–72, https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.71647. [Open access]","Charles François Philibert Masson, <i>Secret Memoirs of the Court of Petersburg, Particularly towards the End of the Reign of Catharine II and the Commencement of That of Paul I</i>, trans. from the French, vol. 1 (1800), 62–64, https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_secret-memoirs-of-the-co_masson-charles-franois_1800_1. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Betsy Ross is the woman who is historically documented to have sewn the very first American flag.","a":false,"why":"The National Archives puts it flatly: her involvement in designing and making the first flag is “largely fictitious”, and the story was probably developed in the 1870s by her grandson William J. Canby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her real connection to the flag was as a Philadelphia flag maker who sewed flags for the United States, and the design itself is credited to Francis Hopkinson, a signer of the Declaration of Independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Preble's standard history of the flag gives the story only as Canby's claim, made in 1870, and disputes his dating of the flag's first use,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> while the Treasury Board's report of 27 October 1780 on Hopkinson's bill for his “fancy work” — his designs for the naval flag, the great seal and the currency — rejected it for want of vouchers and added that he “was not the only person consulted”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["Jessie Kratz, “The American Flag,” Pieces of History, U.S. National Archives, 12 June 2015, https://prologue.blogs.archives.gov/2015/06/12/the-american-flag/. [Open access]","George Henry Preble, <i>History of the Flag of the United States of America</i> (Boston: Houghton, Mifflin, 1894), 265–66, https://archive.org/details/historyofflagofu00preb. [Open access]","<i>Journals of the Continental Congress, 1774–1789</i>, vol. 18, ed. Gaillard Hunt (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1910), 983–84, https://archive.org/details/journals-of-the-continental-congress-1774-1789-vol-18-sept-7-dec-29-1780. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Rosa Parks was the first Black passenger in Montgomery, Alabama, to be arrested for refusing to give up her bus seat.","a":false,"why":"Claudette Colvin, a black teenager, defied the same bus segregation laws and was forcibly removed from a Montgomery bus on 2 March 1955, nine months before Parks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Colvin was one of the four women — with Aurelia Browder, Susie McDonald and Mary Louise Smith — whose case, <i>Browder v. Gayle</i>, was the one that reached the courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fred Gray filed that suit on 1 February 1956 with five plaintiffs, one of whom withdrew under pressure, and left Parks out of it so that the court would have only the segregation laws to decide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["National Park Service, “The Montgomery Bus Boycott,” https://www.nps.gov/articles/montgomery-bus-boycott.htm. [Open access]","“Browder v. Gayle, 352 U.S. 903,” <i>The Martin Luther King, Jr. Encyclopedia</i>, Martin Luther King, Jr. Research and Education Institute, Stanford University, https://kinginstitute.stanford.edu/encyclopedia/browder-v-gayle-352-us-903. [Open access]","Rebecca Woodham, “Claudette Colvin,” <i>Encyclopedia of Alabama</i>, Alabama Humanities Alliance, 13 May 2021, https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/claudette-colvin/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The original Coca-Cola sold in the 1880s and 1890s actually contained cocaine.","a":true,"why":"A history of cocaine in medicine puts it plainly: Coca-Cola was patented in 1887 and “until 1903, this drink contained cocaine”, after which reports of the drug’s effects moved the makers to a process that took the cocaine out of the coca leaves while leaving the essential oils in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other end is in the Supreme Court’s own record of the government’s 1911 seizure case: the company’s trade-mark papers describe extracts of the coca leaf and the cola nut used “for the purpose of obtaining a flavor”, and say the ingredient containing them is, “with cocaine eliminated”, designated Merchandise No. 5.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A formula that has to have the cocaine eliminated from it is one that had cocaine in it,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and later reviews give the same end-date — the cocaine reportedly removed by 1903, the original recipe carrying it until then.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["Melody Redman, “Cocaine: What Is the Crack? A Brief History of the Use of Cocaine as an Anesthetic,” <i>Anesthesiology and Pain Medicine</i> 1, no. 2 (2011): 95–97, https://doi.org/10.5812/kowsar.22287523.1890. [Open access]","<i>United States v. Forty Barrels and Twenty Kegs of Coca-Cola</i>, 241 U.S. 265 (1916), 272, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep241/usrep241265/usrep241265.pdf. [Open access]","Irmgard Bauer, “Travel medicine, coca and cocaine: demystifying and rehabilitating <i>Erythroxylum</i> – a comprehensive review,” <i>Tropical Diseases, Travel Medicine and Vaccines</i> 5 (2019): 20, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6880514/. [Open access]","Vera Marisa Costa, Luciana Grazziotin Rossato Grando, Elisa Milandri, Jessica Nardi, Patrícia Teixeira, Přemysl Mladěnka, and Fernando Remião, “Natural Sympathomimetic Drugs: From Pharmacology to Toxicology,” <i>Biomolecules</i> 12, no. 12 (2022): 1793, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9775352/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"In his final hours George Washington was bled four times by his doctors, and he himself asked for more blood to be taken.","a":true,"why":"Tobias Lear, Washington's secretary, was in the room and wrote the day down. An overseer bled him soon after sunrise on 14 December 1799; when Martha Washington begged that not too much be taken, the General “put up his hand to prevent” the bandage being untied and said “More, more”, and the bleeding was stopped only “after taking about half a pint”. Dr Craik drew more blood on arriving, a third bleeding followed at about eleven, and a fourth after Dr Dick reached Mount Vernon at three — the two physicians' own published report counts the bleeder's “twelve or fourteen ounces”, “two pretty copious bleedings” and a last “thirty-two ounces”,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> about 2.4 litres (82 fluid ounces) in all by a modern reckoning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Washington died between ten and eleven that night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["Tobias Lear, journal entry of 14 December 1799, in <i>Letters and Recollections of George Washington: Being Letters to Tobias Lear and Others</i> (New York: Doubleday, Page, 1906), 131–34, https://archive.org/details/lettersrecollect00wash. [Open access]","White McKenzie Wallenborn, “George Washington’s Terminal Illness: A Modern Medical Analysis of the Last Illness and Death of George Washington,” The Papers of George Washington, University of Virginia, https://washingtonpapers.org/resources/articles/illness/. [Open access]","James Craik and Elisha C. Dick, “Particular Account of the Illness and Death of George Washington,” <i>The Medical and Physical Journal</i> 3, no. 15 (May 1800): 490–91, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5659511/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"In 1932 the Australian army was deployed with machine guns against wild emus and largely failed to defeat them.","a":true,"why":"Hansard records the whole business. The Minister for Defence told the Senate on 18 November 1932 that rifles were useless because only one or two birds could be shot before the rest scattered, so he had lent two machine guns and three regular soldiers, the settlers undertaking to pay for the ammunition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four days later the House heard that the settlers had reported thousands of emus devastating their crops, that the gun crews came to “one officer and two other ranks and two guns”, and that “some hundreds of emus are being destroyed” — this in answer to a member asking outright whether what he called the “Emu war” had been “an expensive failure”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The settlers who asked for the guns were soldier settlers in Western Australia, and the affair has kept the name that member gave it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["Australia, Senate, <i>Parliamentary Debates</i>, 18 November 1932, 2569 (George Pearce, Minister for Defence), https://historichansard.net/senate/1932/19321118_senate_13_137/. [Open access]","Australia, House of Representatives, <i>Parliamentary Debates</i>, 22 November 1932, 2634 (Josiah Francis, Assistant Minister for Defence), https://historichansard.net/hofreps/1932/19321122_reps_13_137/. [Open access]","Murray Johnson, “‘Feathered foes’: Soldier settlers and Western Australia’s ‘Emu War’ of 1932,” <i>Journal of Australian Studies</i> 30, no. 88 (2006): 147–57, https://doi.org/10.1080/14443050609388083. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"The armoured fighting vehicle called the 'tank' got its name from a wartime cover story that the vehicles were mobile water tanks.","a":true,"why":"True, and the cover word is better documented than the cover story. Albert Stern, secretary of the committee that built them, wrote that on 20 October 1915 it was proposed to stop calling the machines landships and to refer to the vessel instead as a “Water Carrier” — a title he thought “totally unsuitable”, because “in Government offices, committees and departments are always known by their initials”. “In our search for a synonymous term”, he goes on, “we changed the word ‘Water Carrier’ to ‘Tank’, and became the ‘Tank Supply’, or ‘T.S.’ Committee. This is how these weapons came to be called ‘Tanks’.”<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Committee of Imperial Defence recommended the name that December, again “for secrecy's sake”<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> — Fuller prints the recommendation, with Swinton's note that “cistern”, “reservoir” and “tank” were all canvassed and the shortest won,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the Tank Corps's own history records the cover story as told at the time: the strange steel boxes were “water-tanks or cisterns” for Petrograd, Egypt or Mesopotamia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["Albert G. Stern, <i>Tanks, 1914–1918: The Log-Book of a Pioneer</i> (London: Hodder and Stoughton, 1919), 39, 48, https://archive.org/details/tankslogbookofpi00ster. [Open access]","J. F. C. Fuller, <i>Tanks in the Great War, 1914–1918</i> (London: John Murray, 1920), 28–30, https://archive.org/details/tanksingreatwar00john. [Open access]","Clough Williams-Ellis and A. Williams-Ellis, <i>The Tank Corps</i> (New York: George H. Doran, 1919), 42–43, https://archive.org/details/tankcorps00clou. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Thomas Crapper, whose name is linked with toilets, actually invented the flush toilet.","a":false,"why":"Crapper was a real Victorian sanitary engineer who sold and improved plumbing — the Science Museum keeps one of his firm’s cisterns, a “valveless waste preventer” of about 1900<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> — but the flushing water closet was patented sixty years before he was born. The Patent Office's own chronological index records patent 1105, dated 11 November 1775, as “a grant unto Alexander Cumming, watchmaker, of his new invented watercloset, which he apprehends will be of great publick utility”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A flushing privy had been described in print earlier still, in Sir John Harington's <i>Metamorphosis of Ajax</i> of 1596.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Science & invention","src":["Bennet Woodcroft, comp., <i>Titles of Patents of Invention, Chronologically Arranged: From March 2, 1617, to October 1, 1852</i>, pt. 1 (London: Queen's Printing Office, 1854), 202, no. 1105, https://archive.org/details/chronologicalin1617grea_0. [Open access]","John Harington, <i>The Metamorphosis of Ajax: A Cloacinean Satire; with the Anatomy and Apology</i> (Chiswick: C. Whittingham, 1814), advertisement, vii–viii, https://archive.org/details/b29340925. [Open access]","Science Museum Group, “Thomas Crapper’s valveless waste preventer, No. 814 cistern, c. 1900,” object 1976-24, https://collection.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk/objects/co49304/thomas-crappers-valveless-waste-preventer. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The word 'bug' for a technical glitch was coined in 1947 when a real moth was found inside the Harvard Mark II computer.","a":false,"why":"The moth was real and was taped into the machine's logbook, but the word was already fifty years old. A standard reference of 1897 defines it flatly — “<i>Bug.</i> Any fault or trouble in the connections or working of electric apparatus” — gives a second entry for the “bug trap” that cured one, and adds that both terms were said to have started in quadruplex telegraphy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A dictionary of 1910 carries the same sense, “a familiar name for any form of trouble encountered in the working of electrical apparatus”, beside a shop word for the same thing,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Houston's of 1892 already had it as a term “originally employed in quadruplex telegraphy”,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and Edison's biographers record him locking his men in until “all the bugs” were “taken out”, and his standing remark when a thing worked: “Well, boys, now let's find the bugs.”<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The 1947 note is itself the giveaway: it records the first actual case of a bug being found, not the first use of the word.","cat":"Science & invention","src":["T. O'Conor Sloane, <i>The Standard Electrical Dictionary: A Popular Dictionary of Words, Terms and Phrases Used in the Practice of Electrical Engineering</i> (New York: Norman W. Henley, 1897), s.v. “Bug” and “Bug Trap”, https://archive.org/details/standardelectri00sloa. [Open access]","N. Hawkins, <i>Hawkins' Electrical Dictionary: A Cyclopedia of Words, Terms, Phrases and Data Used in the Electric Arts, Trades and Sciences</i> (New York: Theodore Audel, 1910), s.v. “Bug”, https://archive.org/details/hawkinselectric05hawkgoog. [Open access]","Edwin J. Houston, <i>A Dictionary of Electrical Words, Terms and Phrases</i> (New York: W. J. Johnston, 1892), 63, s.v. “Bug” and “Bug-Trap,” https://archive.org/details/dictionaryofe00hous. [Open access]","Frank Lewis Dyer and Thomas Commerford Martin, <i>Edison: His Life and Inventions</i> (1910), chs. 8 and 16, Project Gutenberg ebook 820, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/820/pg820.txt. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Surgeons were using ether to put patients to sleep for operations before they understood the need for antiseptic, germ-free surgery.","a":true,"why":"Ether anaesthesia was publicly demonstrated by William Morton at Massachusetts General Hospital on 16 October 1846.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Joseph Lister first used carbolic acid on compound fractures in 1865 and set out his antiseptic method in six <i>Lancet</i> papers in 1867.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For the twenty years between, operative surgery was painless and in deep crisis, with patients still dying of the septic complications that followed a successful operation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Science & invention","src":["Elizabeth M. Makris, Kevin G. Makhoul, Terence B. Lee, and Manisha S. Desai, “Henry Jacob Bigelow (1818–1890): A Champion for Anesthesia and Catalyst for the Advancement of Surgery,” <i>Annals of Surgery Open</i> 3, no. 1 (2022): e118, https://doi.org/10.1097/AS9.0000000000000118. [Open access]","Spyros N. Michaleas, Konstantinos Laios, Alexandros Charalabopoulos, George Samonis, and Marianna Karamanou, “Joseph Lister (1827-1912): A Pioneer of Antiseptic Surgery,” <i>Cureus</i> 14, no. 12 (2022): e32777, https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.32777. [Open access]","Thomas Schlich and Bruno J. Strasser, “Making the Medical Mask: Surgery, Bacteriology, and the Control of Infection, 1870s–1920s,” <i>Medical History</i> 66, no. 2 (2022): 116–134, https://doi.org/10.1017/mdh.2022.5. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Although penicillin was discovered in 1928, it was not turned into a mass-produced, life-saving drug until the 1940s.","a":true,"why":"Fleming noticed in 1928 that a mould contaminating one of his cultures had killed the bacteria around it, but penicillin proved so fragile that attempts to purify it failed and it stayed a bacteriologist's curiosity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Florey and Chain took it up at Oxford from 1938, the first results in sick patients were published in August 1941 with supplies too short to finish some of the treatments, and it was Florey who then interested the United States in making it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> British firms, committed to the war, could not mass-produce it, so Florey turned to the United States, where the strain eventually used for mass production was found on a mouldy cantaloupe in a market.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Science & invention","src":["“Award Ceremony Speech,” The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1945, presented by G. Liljestrand, 10 December 1945, NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/1945/ceremony-speech/. [Open access]","Alexander Fleming, “On the Antibacterial Action of Cultures of a Penicillium, with Special Reference to their Use in the Isolation of <i>B. influenzæ</i>,” <i>British Journal of Experimental Pathology</i> 10, no. 3 (1929): 226–36, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2048009/. [Open access]","Robert Gaynes, “The Discovery of Penicillin—New Insights After More Than 75 Years of Clinical Use,” <i>Emerging Infectious Diseases</i> 23, no. 5 (2017): 849–53, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5403050/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Alexander Graham Bell and a rival inventor filed telephone patent paperwork at the very same patent office on the same day.","a":true,"why":"The Supreme Court set the order out when the patent finally came before it: “Bell's application was filed February 14, 1876, and afterwards, during the same day, Elisha Gray filed a <i>caveat</i>” claiming as his invention the art of transmitting speech through an electric circuit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The shared date was later the foundation of a formal charge that Bell's specification had been altered in the five days that followed; the court weighed it at length and rejected it, holding that “not a shadow of suspicion can rest on any one”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Patent Office's cash book settled the order within the day — Bell's fee was the fifth entry, Gray's the thirty-ninth<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> — and Prescott, writing in 1878 with Gray's caveat before him, gave the same date, 14 February 1876, for both filings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Science & invention","src":["<i>The Telephone Cases</i>, 126 U.S. 1 (1888), https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/126/1. [Open access]","Herbert N. Casson, <i>The History of the Telephone</i> (Chicago: A. C. McClurg, 1910), ch. 3, https://archive.org/details/historyoftelepho00cass. [Open access]","George B. Prescott, <i>The Speaking Telephone, Talking Phonograph, and Other Novelties</i> (New York: D. Appleton, 1878), 71n, https://archive.org/details/speakingtelepho01presgoog. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Nothing with mass can be accelerated to the speed of light in a vacuum.","a":true,"why":"Relativity makes an object's kinetic energy rise without bound as its speed approaches that of light, so an infinite amount of work would be needed to get it there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> However much energy is put into accelerating a mass, its velocity can only approach the speed of light and never reach it: that speed is the ultimate limit for any particle having mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Einstein put it the same way in his own popular account: the energy “approaches infinity as the velocity <i>v</i> approaches the velocity of light <i>c</i>”, so “the velocity must therefore always remain less than <i>c</i>, however great may be the energies used to produce the acceleration”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Physics","src":["OpenStax, “5.9 Relativistic Energy,” <i>University Physics Volume 3</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/5-9-relativistic-energy. [Open access]","Albert Einstein, <i>Relativity: The Special and General Theory</i>, trans. Robert W. Lawson (1920), §15, Project Gutenberg ebook 30155, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/30155. [Open access]","OpenStax, “28.6 Relativistic Energy,” <i>College Physics 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/28-6-relativistic-energy. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Glass is a very slow-moving liquid, which is why old cathedral windows are thicker at the bottom.","a":false,"why":"Edgar Zanotto set out to test the story, which he found was believed inside the scientific community as well as outside it, and which rests almost entirely on the thicker lower edges of medieval stained glass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Working from calculated viscosity curves for several modern and ancient glass compositions, he estimated how long window glass would need to flow and deform at ordinary temperatures, and concluded it could do so only over spans exceeding the whole of human history — so whatever makes those panes uneven, it is not the glass running.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A revised estimate with Gupta the next year cut his figure by several orders of magnitude and reached the same conclusion,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and a 2018 measurement on a medieval glass composition from Westminster Abbey found it far too stiff to flow measurably on any human timescale: about a nanometre in a billion years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Physics","src":["Edgar Dutra Zanotto, “Do Cathedral Glasses Flow?” <i>American Journal of Physics</i> 66, no. 5 (1998): 392–95, https://doi.org/10.1119/1.19026. [Paywalled]","Edgar D. Zanotto and Prabhat K. Gupta, “Do cathedral glasses flow?—Additional remarks,” <i>American Journal of Physics</i> 67, no. 3 (1999): 260–62, https://doi.org/10.1119/1.19236. [Paywalled]","Ozgur Gulbiten, John C. Mauro, Xiaoju Guo, and Olus N. Boratav, “Viscous flow of medieval cathedral glass,” <i>Journal of the American Ceramic Society</i> 101, no. 1 (2018): 5–11, https://doi.org/10.1111/jace.15092. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"Pure water is a poor conductor of electricity.","a":true,"why":"To carry a current a substance needs freely moving charges, and pure water has almost none: only about two molecules in every billion are ionised at 25 °C (77 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What makes tap water, sweat and river water conduct is the dissolved salts, a solution's conductivity rising with the concentration of ions in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Geological Survey puts it bluntly: pure water — distilled or deionised — is “actually an excellent insulator”, and it is the dissolved substances in real water that carry the current.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Chemistry","src":["OpenStax, “11.2 Electrolytes,” <i>Chemistry 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/11-2-electrolytes. [Open access]","USGS Water Science School, “Conductivity (Electrical Conductance) and Water,” U.S. Geological Survey, 22 October 2019, https://www.usgs.gov/special-topics/water-science-school/science/conductivity-electrical-conductance-and-water. [Open access]","OpenStax, “18.2 Conductors and Insulators,” <i>College Physics 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/18-2-conductors-and-insulators. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Electric current travels through a wire at close to the speed of light, yet the electrons themselves creep along at less than a millimetre per second.","a":true,"why":"The signal is a rapidly propagating change in the electric field, and it runs along the wire at speeds on the order of 100 million metres a second — a significant fraction of the speed of light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The charges themselves drift at about a ten-thousandth of a metre a second, which is enough because a wire holds so many free electrons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Worked through for a copper wire, the drift comes out at millimetres a second,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and the signal moves on the order of a million million times faster than the charges that carry it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Physics","src":["OpenStax, “9.2 Model of Conduction in Metals,” <i>University Physics Volume 2</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-2-model-of-conduction-in-metals. [Open access]","OpenStax, “20.1 Current,” <i>College Physics 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/20-1-current. [Open access]","R. Nave, “Microscopic View of Ohm’s Law,” <i>HyperPhysics</i>, Georgia State University, http://hyperphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/electric/ohmmic.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Coriolis effect decides which way water spins as it drains from a bath.","a":false,"why":"The argument is old enough to have its own literature: Shapiro opened his 1962 note by remarking that one perennially hears the controversy, some holding that the swirl always runs one way in each hemisphere and others that it has no fixed direction at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the rotation was finally shown — by Shapiro in Massachusetts and by Binnie at Cambridge — it was on the express condition that every other influence be kept small beside the turning of the Earth, and both men reached the result only after surmounting difficulties in their earlier attempts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is therefore a fact about a tank in which everything else has been suppressed, not about a bath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sibulkin, draining a vessel that had been left relatively still, even saw the direction of rotation reverse as the surface neared the bottom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Physics","src":["Ascher H. Shapiro, “Bath-Tub Vortex,” <i>Nature</i> 196, no. 4859 (1962): 1080–81, https://doi.org/10.1038/1961080b0. [Paywalled]","Lloyd M. Trefethen, R. W. Bilger, P. T. Fink, R. E. Luxton, and R. I. Tanner, “The Bath-Tub Vortex in the Southern Hemisphere,” <i>Nature</i> 207, no. 5001 (1965): 1084–85, https://doi.org/10.1038/2071084a0. [Paywalled]","Merwin Sibulkin, “A Note on the Bathtub Vortex,” <i>Journal of Fluid Mechanics</i> 14, no. 1 (1962): 21–24, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0022112062001044. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"Lightning never strikes the same place twice.","a":false,"why":"Tall, isolated, conductive structures are struck again and again: the Empire State Building is hit an average of 23 times a year,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> the Gaisberg Tower in Austria logged 790 upward flashes between 2000 and 2015,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and Johannesburg’s Brixton tower is struck often enough that three cameras were set up to film it through a thunderstorm season.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lightning follows the easiest path to ground, and a good path stays a good path.","cat":"Physics","src":["National Weather Service, “Lightning Myths,” National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration, https://www.weather.gov/safety/lightning-myths. [Open access]","Isabell Stucke, Deborah Morgenstern, Gerhard Diendorfer, Georg J. Mayr, Hannes Pichler, Wolfgang Schulz, Thorsten Simon, and Achim Zeileis, “Upward Lightning at the Gaisberg Tower: The Larger-Scale Meteorological Influence on the Triggering Mode and Flash Type,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Atmospheres</i> 128, no. 10 (2023): e2022JD037776, https://doi.org/10.1029/2022JD037776. [Open access]","Hugh G. P. Hunt, “Dataset of Photographed Lightning Events Attaching to and around the Brixton Tower, Johannesburg, South Africa for the 2015–2016 Thunderstorm Season,” <i>Data in Brief</i> 30 (2020): 105630, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.dib.2020.105630. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Sound travels faster through water than through air.","a":true,"why":"Sound moves at a much faster speed in water than it does in air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Temperature and pressure then refract it up and down into what is called the sound channel, in which a signal carries for enormous distances without losing much energy, so a hydrophone at the right depth picks up whale song from many kilometres away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tabulated values put it at 331 metres a second (1,086 feet a second) in air at 0 °C (32 °F) against 1,480 metres a second (4,856 feet a second) in fresh water at 20 °C (68 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Physics","src":["National Ocean Service, “How far does sound travel in the ocean?” NOAA, last updated 16 June 2024, https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/sound.html. [Open access]","OpenStax, “17.2 Speed of Sound,” <i>University Physics Volume 1</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/17-2-speed-of-sound. [Open access]","OpenStax, “17.2 Speed of Sound, Frequency, and Wavelength,” <i>College Physics 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/17-2-speed-of-sound-frequency-and-wavelength. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Absolute zero is −273.15 degrees Celsius.","a":true,"why":"Absolute zero is where a system has the least thermal energy it can have, and it is the bottom of the Kelvin scale: 0 K is −273.15 °C (−459.67 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The SI defines the Celsius temperature as the kelvin temperature less 273.15.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It has never been reached — laboratories have come extraordinarily close, cooling atoms to within a fraction of a degree of it, but cannot take away the last of the energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","cat":"Physics","src":["National Institute of Standards and Technology, “Kelvin: Introduction,” SI Redefinition, 14 May 2018, https://www.nist.gov/si-redefinition/kelvin-introduction. [Open access]","OpenStax, “1.2 Thermometers and Temperature Scales,” <i>University Physics Volume 2</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-2-thermometers-and-temperature-scales. [Open access]","Bureau International des Poids et Mesures, <i>The International System of Units (SI)</i>, 9th ed. (Sèvres: BIPM, 2019), 129, https://www.bipm.org/documents/20126/41483022/SI-Brochure-9-EN.pdf. [Open access]","NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, “Cold Atom Laboratory,” NASA, https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/missions/cold-atom-laboratory-cal/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Seen from space, the Sun is white rather than yellow.","a":true,"why":"Sunlight is white: Newton showed it to be a mixture of all the colours of the rainbow, and astronomers speak of the white light of the Sun and stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Air is what tints it — the atmosphere scatters blue more strongly than the rest because blue travels in shorter waves, and the lower the Sun sits the more air its light must cross, so more of the blue is scattered away and the reds and yellows pass straight through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pictures taken from space show it plainly, and the Stanford Solar Center calls it a common misconception that the Sun is yellow, orange or red.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Astronomy","src":["OpenStax, “5.3 Spectroscopy in Astronomy,” <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/5-3-spectroscopy-in-astronomy. [Open access]","“Why Is the Sky Blue?,” NASA Space Place, https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/blue-sky/en/. [Open access]","Stanford Solar Center, “What Color Do YOU Think the Sun Is?,” Stanford University, http://solar-center.stanford.edu/SID/activities/GreenSun.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Mercury is the closest planet to the Sun, so it is also the hottest.","a":false,"why":"Venus is much hotter, its surface sitting at about 467 °C (872 °F) against Mercury’s daytime peak of about 430 °C (800 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Venus’s dense atmosphere traps heat in a runaway greenhouse effect that keeps the surface above 700 K,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> while Mercury, which has no atmosphere to hold the heat, falls to about −180 °C (−290 °F) at night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Astronomy","src":["NASA, “Venus Facts,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/venus/venus-facts/. [Open access]","NASA, “Mercury Facts,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/mercury/facts/. [Open access]","OpenStax, “9.5 Mercury,” <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/9-5-mercury. [Open access]","OpenStax, “10.3 The Massive Atmosphere of Venus,” <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/10-3-the-massive-atmosphere-of-venus. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Astronauts float aboard the International Space Station because there is no gravity at that altitude.","a":false,"why":"Earth’s gravitational field about 400 kilometres (250 miles) up is still 88.8 per cent of its strength at the surface, and it is that gravity which keeps the station in orbit at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The station and everything in it are in continuous free fall around the planet, moving sideways fast enough that the surface curves away beneath them as quickly as they fall, and it is that shared fall — not an absence of gravity — that produces weightlessness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There is, as one textbook puts it, no “zero gravity” in an astronaut’s orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Astronomy","src":["NASA, “What is Microgravity?” NASA, 13 February 2009, https://www.nasa.gov/general/what-is-microgravity/. [Open access]","OpenStax, “6.5 Newton’s Universal Law of Gravitation,” <i>College Physics 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/6-5-newtons-universal-law-of-gravitation. [Open access]","OpenStax, “13.4 Satellite Orbits and Energy,” <i>University Physics Volume 1</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/13-4-satellite-orbits-and-energy. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"A day on Venus lasts longer than a year on Venus.","a":true,"why":"A day on Venus is 243 Earth days long, which is longer than its year — one trip round the Sun takes 225 Earth days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rotation period was pinned down by bouncing radar off the planet, since no surface detail shows through the clouds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It also spins backwards compared with Earth, so the Sun there rises in the west and sets in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Astronomy","src":["NASA, “Venus: Facts,” NASA Science, last updated 14 September 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/venus/venus-facts/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Venus Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/venusfact.html. [Open access]","OpenStax, “10.1 The Nearest Planets: An Overview,” <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/10-1-the-nearest-planets-an-overview. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Moon keeps the same face towards Earth because it does not rotate.","a":false,"why":"The Moon is tidally locked with Earth, which means it spins on its axis exactly once for each orbit it makes — synchronous rotation, and the reason only one side is ever turned towards us.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two periods match: a rotation of 655.7 hours, or 27.3 days, against an orbit of 27.3 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is a match between two motions rather than an absence of one, which is what the question gets wrong.","cat":"Astronomy","src":["NASA, “Moon Phases,” NASA Science, last updated 3 August 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/moon/lunar-phases-and-eclipses/. [Open access]","NASA, “Moon: Facts,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/moon/facts/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Moon Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/moonfact.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Saturn is the only planet in the solar system with rings.","a":false,"why":"Jupiter, Uranus and Neptune all have ring systems: Jupiter's were found by Voyager 1 in 1979, Uranus has two sets of them and Neptune at least five main rings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Saturn's are simply far brighter and wider, where Jupiter's are small dark particles hard to see except when backlit by the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Astronomy","src":["NASA, “Jupiter Facts,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-facts/. [Open access]","NASA, “Uranus Facts,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/uranus/facts/. [Open access]","NASA, “Neptune Facts,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/neptune/neptune-facts/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Sunlight takes about eight minutes to reach Earth.","a":true,"why":"NASA puts the mean distance from the earth to the sun at 149.6 million kilometres (92.96 million miles), running from 147.1 million at the near point of the orbit to 152.1 million at the far one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Light covers 299,792 kilometres (186,282 miles) a second, a speed fixed by definition rather than measured, so the crossing takes 499 seconds — 8 minutes 19 seconds on average, and between 8 minutes 11 seconds and 8 minutes 27 seconds over the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Astronomers put it the other way about: light takes slightly more than eight minutes to cross one astronomical unit, so the latest news from the Sun is always eight minutes old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Astronomy","src":["David R. Williams, “Sun Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/sunfact.html. [Open access]","“Speed of Light in Vacuum,” 2022 CODATA recommended values, National Institute of Standards and Technology, https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?c. [Open access]","OpenStax, “1.6 A Tour of the Universe,” <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/1-6-a-tour-of-the-universe. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The asteroid belt is so densely packed that a spacecraft has to steer carefully to avoid collisions.","a":false,"why":"The belt’s volume is very large and the typical spacing between objects down to a kilometre across is several million kilometres, so from inside it there would be far more empty space than asteroids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> NASA’s estimate runs to between 1.1 and 1.9 million asteroids larger than a kilometre (0.6 miles), with a combined mass less than the Moon’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pioneer 10 was the first spacecraft to fly through it, entering in July 1972 and emerging in February 1973,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and Galileo, Cassini, Rosetta and New Horizons all crossed it afterwards without a collision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Astronomy","src":["OpenStax, “13.1 Asteroids,” <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/13-1-asteroids. [Open access]","NASA, “Asteroids: Facts,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/asteroids/facts/. [Open access]","NASA, “Pioneer 10,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/mission/pioneer-10/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Polaris, the North Star, is the brightest star in the night sky.","a":false,"why":"Sirius is the brightest star in the sky, at magnitude −1.5, where Polaris is magnitude 2.0 — taking Polaris for the brightest is a common misconception.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What Polaris has instead is its position: it is the pole star, the one that moves least as the northern sky turns each day, and though not the brightest it is easily found from the two stars in the cup of the Big Dipper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","cat":"Astronomy","src":["OpenStax, “17.1 The Brightness of Stars,” <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/17-1-the-brightness-of-stars. [Open access]","OpenStax, “2.1 The Sky Above,” <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Robert Nemiroff and Jerry Bonnell, “Sirius: The Brightest Star in the Night,” Astronomy Picture of the Day, NASA, 11 June 2000, https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap000611.html. [Open access]","Robert Nemiroff and Jerry Bonnell, “North Star: Polaris and Surrounding Dust,” Astronomy Picture of the Day, NASA, 28 April 2021, https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap210428.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Human beings have exactly five senses.","a":false,"why":"Aristotle’s list — sight, hearing, touch, taste and smell — is still what most people outside the sciences mean by the senses, and it leaves out the vestibular system that gives us balance and the musculotendinous proprioception that tells us where our limbs are in space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also treats touch as a single sensor, where what is now called the somatosensory system is defined far more broadly than that, taking in pressure, vibration, tickle, itch, temperature and pain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Introductory textbooks now say outright that the notion of five senses is oversimplified, adding balance, body position and movement, pain and temperature to the list.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Thomas Brandt, Marianne Dieterich, and Doreen Huppert, “Human senses and sensors from Aristotle to the present,” <i>Frontiers in Neurology</i> 15 (2024): 1404720, https://doi.org/10.3389/fneur.2024.1404720. [Open access]","OpenStax, “14.1 Sensory Perception,” <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/14-1-sensory-perception. [Open access]","OpenStax, “5.1 Sensation versus Perception,” <i>Psychology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/5-1-sensation-versus-perception. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"People use only ten per cent of their brains.","a":false,"why":"The claim is older than the brain imaging usually quoted against it — a BMJ review traces it to 1907 and to self-improvement writing about untapped latent ability, and finds the attribution to Einstein has no recorded source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against it stand studies of brain damage, brain imaging, localisation of function, microstructural analysis and metabolism: damage to almost any area has specific and lasting effects, and no area of the brain is completely silent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is nonetheless among the most widely held “neuromyths”: nearly half of the British and Dutch teachers surveyed in 2012 agreed with it,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> as did about a third of the public and of educators in a 2017 American survey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Rachel C. Vreeman and Aaron E. Carroll, “Medical myths,” <i>BMJ</i> 335, no. 7633 (2007): 1288–89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2151163/. [Open access]","Sanne Dekker, Nikki C. Lee, Paul Howard-Jones, and Jelle Jolles, “Neuromyths in Education: Prevalence and Predictors of Misconceptions among Teachers,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 3 (2012): 429, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3475349/. [Open access]","Kelly Macdonald, Laura Germine, Alida Anderson, Joanna Christodoulou, and Lauren M. McGrath, “Dispelling the Myth: Training in Education or Neuroscience Decreases but Does Not Eliminate Beliefs in Neuromyths,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 8 (2017): 1314, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5554523/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Goldfish have a memory lasting only about three seconds.","a":false,"why":"Goldfish steering a wheeled tank towards a target gradually adjust their swimming to compensate when the steering is perturbed, and go on over-compensating for a while after the perturbation is taken away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They also do better the second time the same perturbation is introduced — which is memory carried between sessions, not three seconds of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Goldfish have long been a laboratory animal for learning: they acquire a classically conditioned response to a light that signals a shock,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and in a 2025 study they told apart the contingencies of a choice task over 45 days of twice-daily sessions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Zhuoxin Liu, Shachar Givon, Ronen Segev, and Opher Donchin, “Whole Body Motor Adaptation in Goldfish Using Fish Operated Vehicle,” <i>European Journal of Neuroscience</i> 62, no. 5 (2025): e70241, https://doi.org/10.1111/ejn.70241. [Open access]","Masayuki Yoshida and Ruriko Hirano, “Effects of Local Anesthesia of the Cerebellum on Classical Fear Conditioning in Goldfish,” <i>Behavioral and Brain Functions</i> 6 (2010): 20, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2848191/. [Open access]","Victor Ajuwon, Tiago Monteiro, Mark E. Walton, and Alex Kacelnik, “Do Goldfish Like to Be Informed?,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 292, no. 2047 (2025): 20242842, https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2024.2842. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"A banana is botanically a berry, while a strawberry is not.","a":true,"why":"A berry is a fruit that develops from a single ovary with its seeds inside the flesh, and the fruit of the banana plant is described botanically as an elongated, curved berry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A strawberry is an accessory fruit: what is eaten grows from the flower's receptacle rather than from a reproductive organ, and the true fruits are the achenes dotted over its surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Sahromi Sahromi et al., “Physiological Maturity Determination of Wild Banana (<i>Musa acuminata</i> subsp. <i>malaccensis</i> (Ridl.) N.W.Simmonds) for Seed Conservation,” <i>Gates Open Research</i> 10 (2026): 2, https://doi.org/10.12688/gatesopenres.16375.1. [Open access]","Moises Pérez-Rojas et al., “The Role of Cytokinins during the Development of Strawberry Flowers and Receptacles,” <i>Plants</i> 12, no. 21 (2023): 3672, https://doi.org/10.3390/plants12213672. [Open access]","OpenStax, “32.2 Pollination and Fertilization,” <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/32-2-pollination-and-fertilization. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"A human body contains at least as many bacterial cells as human cells.","a":true,"why":"The best current estimate puts a 70-kilogram (150-pound) reference man at about 3.8×10¹³ bacteria against about 3.0×10¹³ human cells — the same order of magnitude — with the gut, and the colon above all, holding most of the bacteria and the whole lot weighing around 0.2 kilograms (7 ounces).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That revised a much-repeated ten-to-one ratio which the authors traced back to a single back-of-the-envelope estimate: 10¹¹ bacteria per gram of gut content multiplied by about a kilogram of alimentary tract.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An independent count of human cells, organ by organ, had arrived at 3.72×10¹³,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and the ten-to-one figure is now described in reviews as stemming from a 1972 back-of-the-envelope calculation, with the refined ratio nearer 1.3 bacteria to every human cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Ron Sender, Shai Fuchs and Ron Milo, “Revised Estimates for the Number of Human and Bacteria Cells in the Body,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 14, no. 8 (2016): e1002533, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002533. [Open access]","Jack Gilbert, Martin J. Blaser, J. Gregory Caporaso, Janet Jansson, Susan V. Lynch, and Rob Knight, “Current Understanding of the Human Microbiome,” <i>Nature Medicine</i> 24, no. 4 (2018): 392–400, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7043356/. [Open access]","Eva Bianconi et al., “An Estimation of the Number of Cells in the Human Body,” <i>Annals of Human Biology</i> 40, no. 6 (2013): 463–71, https://doi.org/10.3109/03014460.2013.807878. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"Blood in the veins is blue until it meets the air.","a":false,"why":"Blood is red wherever it is: bright red where it has just taken up oxygen in the lungs, a duskier red where it has released it in the tissues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That change of shade is haemoglobin, a pigment whose colour depends on how much oxygen it is carrying — so blood that has given its oxygen up, deep in a vein and nowhere near the air, is already a dusky red rather than a blue one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Veins look bluish through the skin because of how skin scatters and absorbs light of different wavelengths, how deep and wide the vessel is, and how the eye judges colour — not because the blood inside is blue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["OpenStax, “18.1 An Overview of Blood,” <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/18-1-an-overview-of-blood. [Open access]","OpenStax, “18.3 Erythrocytes,” <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/18-3-erythrocytes. [Open access]","A. Kienle, L. Lilge, I. A. Vitkin, M. S. Patterson, B. C. Wilson, R. Hibst, and R. Steiner, “Why Do Veins Appear Blue? A New Look at an Old Question,” <i>Applied Optics</i> 35, no. 7 (1996): 1151, https://doi.org/10.1364/AO.35.001151. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"Different regions of the tongue detect different tastes — sweet at the tip, bitter at the back.","a":false,"why":"The National Institutes of Health name this one outright: “A common misconception is that taste cells that respond to different tastes are found in separate regions of the tongue. In humans, the different types of taste cells are scattered throughout the tongue.”<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Taste cells carry receptors for at least five basic qualities — sweet, sour, bitter, salty and umami — and the buds holding them sit on the roof of the mouth and along the throat as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reviews of the evidence call the tongue map mythical — every taste can be experienced all over the tongue, even if bitter and umami register somewhat more strongly towards the back<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> — and the receptors sit on the tongue, in the mouth and in the throat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["National Institute on Deafness and Other Communication Disorders, “Taste Disorders,” National Institutes of Health, https://www.nidcd.nih.gov/health/taste-disorders. [Open access]","Charles Spence, “The Tongue Map and the Spatial Modulation of Taste Perception,” <i>Current Research in Food Science</i> 5 (2022): 598–610, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.crfs.2022.02.004. [Open access]","OpenStax, “5.5 The Other Senses,” <i>Psychology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/5-5-the-other-senses. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"An octopus has three hearts and blue blood.","a":true,"why":"The closed circulatory system of octopuses and their relatives comprises three hearts together with contractile veins: two branchial hearts that send blood through the gills and one systemic heart that pumps it round the body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What they pump is haemolymph heavily enriched with haemocyanin, the copper-bearing blue oxygen-transport pigment that gives the blood its colour, at pressures high for an invertebrate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Michael Oellermann, Bernhard Lieb, Hans-O. Pörtner, Jayson M. Semmens, and Felix C. Mark, “Blue Blood on Ice: Modulated Blood Oxygen Transport Facilitates Cold Compensation and Eurythermy in an Antarctic Octopod,” <i>Frontiers in Zoology</i> 12 (2015): 6, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12983-015-0097-x. [Open access]","Ernesto Maldonado, Emma Rangel-Huerta, Roberto González-Gómez, Gabriel Fajardo-Alvarado, and Piedad S. Morillo-Velarde, “<i>Octopus insularis</i> as a New Marine Model for Evolutionary Developmental Biology,” <i>Biology Open</i> 8, no. 11 (2019): bio046086, https://doi.org/10.1242/bio.046086. [Open access]","Byron Breedlove, “The Curious Case of the Cephalopod Parasites,” <i>Emerging Infectious Diseases</i> 26, no. 8 (2020): 1966–67, https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/26/8/ac-2608_article. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Sharks do not get cancer.","a":false,"why":"The belief goes with a trade: crude shark cartilage is sold as a cancer cure, and the supposed absence of cancer in sharks is the key justification offered for using it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A survey of the Registry of Tumors in Lower Animals set out both malignant and benign tumours in sharks and their relatives, several of them previously unreported, among them two sharks carrying two cancers each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its authors put the cost of the belief at a sharp decline in shark populations and at patients turned away from treatments that work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A randomised, placebo-controlled trial of a shark cartilage product in 83 patients with advanced cancer found no difference in survival,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and case reports keep adding to the list — among them three bamboo sharks in one aquarium population with primary liver tumours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Gary K. Ostrander, Keith C. Cheng, Jeffrey C. Wolf, and Marilyn J. Wolfe, “Shark Cartilage, Cancer and the Growing Threat of Pseudoscience,” <i>Cancer Research</i> 64, no. 23 (2004): 8485–91, https://doi.org/10.1158/0008-5472.CAN-04-2260. [Paywalled]","Charles L. Loprinzi et al., “Evaluation of Shark Cartilage in Patients with Advanced Cancer: A North Central Cancer Treatment Group Trial,” <i>Cancer</i> 104, no. 1 (2005): 176–82, https://doi.org/10.1002/cncr.21107. [Paywalled]","J. L. Gerlach, B. N. Stevens, M. M. Garner, E. E. B. LaDouceur, and L. M. Adams, “Primary Hepatic Neoplasia in Three Adult Bamboo Sharks (<i>Chiloscyllium plagiosum</i> and <i>Chiloscyllium punctatum</i>) in an Aquarium Population,” <i>Journal of Zoo and Wildlife Medicine</i> 54, no. 4 (2024): 865–72, https://doi.org/10.1638/2023-0018. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"Bats are blind.","a":false,"why":"The National Park Service answers it as a myth in terms: bats “can see just fine and actually have pretty good eyesight”, and some of the larger fruit-eating bats see three times better than we do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Echolocation is how some of them find obstacles and prey in low light, at dawn and dusk — sound used alongside sight rather than instead of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Geneticists tracing bats’ visual pigments conclude that earlier expectations of blindness were simply incorrect,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and Egyptian fruit bats tested on navigation give vision more weight than echolocation when deciding where to fly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["National Park Service, “Myth Busters — Bats,” https://www.nps.gov/subjects/bats/myth-busters.htm. [Open access]","Bruno F. Simões, Nicole M. Foley, Graham M. Hughes, Huabin Zhao, Shuyi Zhang, Stephen J. Rossiter, and Emma C. Teeling, “As Blind as a Bat? Opsin Phylogenetics Illuminates the Evolution of Color Vision in Bats,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 36, no. 1 (2019): 54–68, https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msy192. [Open access]","S. Danilovich and Y. Yovel, “Integrating Vision and Echolocation for Navigation and Perception in Bats,” <i>Science Advances</i> 5, no. 6 (2019): eaaw6503, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aaw6503. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Tardigrades have survived direct exposure to the vacuum of space.","a":true,"why":"Tardigrades endure near-complete desiccation, severe radiation and the vacuum of space, the last of those established by published low-Earth-orbit experiments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two experiments flew on the FOTON-M3 mission of September 2007: dried animals exposed to open space in low Earth orbit revived on return,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and others kept inside the capsule came through twelve days of microgravity and space radiation, moulting and laying eggs that hatched normally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They manage it by entering cryptobiosis, a metabolic arrest whose desiccated form is the barrel-shaped tun, which holds its structure for decades and comes back to life in water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Maria Kamilari, Frida Løkkegaard Pust, Andrea Marcantognini, Ask Møbjerg, Ricardo Cardoso Neves, Morten Schiøtt, and Nadja Møbjerg, “At the Edge of Survival: Exploring the Frontiers of Tardigrade Extreme Stress Tolerance,” <i>Molecular Ecology</i> 35, no. 14 (2026): e70471, https://doi.org/10.1111/mec.70471. [Open access]","K. Ingemar Jönsson, Elke Rabbow, Ralph O. Schill, Mats Harms-Ringdahl, and Petra Rettberg, “Tardigrades Survive Exposure to Space in Low Earth Orbit,” <i>Current Biology</i> 18, no. 17 (2008): R729–31, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cub.2008.06.048. [Paywalled]","Lorena Rebecchi, Tiziana Altiero, Roberto Guidetti, Michele Cesari, Roberto Bertolani, Manuela Negroni, and Angela M. Rizzo, “Tardigrade Resistance to Space Effects: First Results of Experiments on the LIFE-TARSE Mission on FOTON-M3 (September 2007),” <i>Astrobiology</i> 9, no. 6 (2009): 581–91, https://doi.org/10.1089/ast.2008.0305. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"Fingernails and hair go on growing for a while after death.","a":false,"why":"Growth of hair and nails needs hormonal regulation the body does not sustain after death, and the forensic anthropologist William Maples called the image “pure moonshine”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What is really happening is that the body dries out and the skin round the hair or nail retracts, so the shrunken soft tissue makes what is left look longer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both are made by dividing cells — the nail root’s matrix and the hair matrix in the follicle — and that division stops at death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Rachel C. Vreeman and Aaron E. Carroll, “Medical myths,” <i>BMJ</i> 335, no. 7633 (2007): 1288–89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2151163/. [Open access]","William R. Maples and Michael Browning, <i>Dead Men Do Tell Tales</i> (New York: Doubleday, 1994), https://archive.org/details/deadmendotelltal00mapl. [Open access]","OpenStax, <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), §5.2 “Accessory Structures of the Skin,” https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/5-2-accessory-structures-of-the-skin. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Honey can remain edible for thousands of years.","a":true,"why":"The unbound water in honey is too little for microbes to multiply in, and undiluted honey is hypertonic enough to pull the water back out of a bacterium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its acidity and the hydrogen peroxide made in it by the enzyme glucose oxidase work the same way, so very little that would spoil it can grow in it at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The microbes that do turn up in it — spores carried in from the flowers, the bees and the hive — mostly lie dormant, unable to multiply in so hygroscopic, hyperosmotic, acid and peroxide-bearing a medium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Ecem Bolat, Ahmet Alperen Canbolat, Sümeyye Sarıtaş, Mauro Lombardo, Anna Maria Witkowska, Hesham El-Seedi, Mikhael Bechelany, and Sercan Karav, “Honey as a Natural Flavorful Product: A Comprehensive Review of Its Potential Biological Activities and Recent Studies,” <i>Food Science &amp; Nutrition</i> 14, no. 4 (2026): e71645, https://doi.org/10.1002/fsn3.71645. [Open access]","Manisha Deb Mandal and Shyamapada Mandal, “Honey: Its Medicinal Property and Antibacterial Activity,” <i>Asian Pacific Journal of Tropical Biomedicine</i> 1, no. 2 (2011): 154–60, https://doi.org/10.1016/S2221-1691(11)60016-6. [Open access]","Peter B. Olaitan, Olufemi E. Adeleke, and Iyabo O. Ola, “Honey: A Reservoir for Microorganisms and an Inhibitory Agent for Microbes,” <i>African Health Sciences</i> 7, no. 3 (2007): 159–65, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2269714/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Diamond and graphite are both made of nothing but carbon.","a":true,"why":"They are allotropes of carbon — different structural forms of one element, each with its own physical properties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diamond is a colourless transparent crystal and the hardest known material, where graphite is black, shiny and soft, and the two differ in density by more than half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In diamond every carbon atom is bonded to four others in a rigid three-dimensional network, while in graphite each is bonded to three in flat sheets that slide over one another — which is why the one is hard and the other soft and slippery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chemistry","src":["Royal Society of Chemistry, “Carbon,” Periodic Table, https://periodic-table.rsc.org/element/6/carbon. [Open access]","OpenStax, “10.5 The Solid State of Matter,” <i>Chemistry 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/10-5-the-solid-state-of-matter. [Open access]","Linus Pauling, <i>General Chemistry: An Introduction to Descriptive Chemistry and Modern Chemical Theory</i> (San Francisco: W. H. Freeman, 1947), https://archive.org/details/generalchemistry00paul. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Helium was found on the Sun before it was found on Earth.","a":true,"why":"Pierre Janssen saw an unexplained yellow line in the solar spectrum during the eclipse of 1868, and Norman Lockyer, recording the same line from London, named the new element after <i>helios</i>, the Greek for sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was not found on Earth until 1895, by William Ramsay in London and independently by Per Teodor Cleve and Nils Langlet in Uppsala.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ramsay released it from the rare mineral cleveite while looking for sources of argon, and Langlet found it a little later in Cleve's own laboratory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chemistry","src":["Royal Society of Chemistry, “Helium,” Periodic Table, https://periodic-table.rsc.org/element/2/helium. [Open access]","Nobel Prize Outreach, “Sir William Ramsay — Biographical,” <i>The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 1904</i>, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/1904/ramsay/biographical/. [Open access]","William Ramsay, <i>The Gases of the Atmosphere: The History of Their Discovery</i>, 3rd ed. (London: Macmillan, 1905), https://archive.org/details/gasesofatmospher00rams. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Adding salt to a pan of water makes it boil sooner.","a":false,"why":"Dissolving anything in water lowers its vapour pressure, so the solution has to reach a higher temperature than pure water before its vapour pressure matches the air above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Salted water therefore boils later rather than sooner, and how much later depends on the number of dissolved particles rather than on what they are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chemistry","src":["OpenStax, “11.4 Colligative Properties,” <i>Chemistry 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/11-4-colligative-properties. [Open access]","OpenStax, “11.4 Colligative Properties,” <i>Chemistry: Atoms First 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-atoms-first-2e/pages/11-4-colligative-properties. [Open access]","Linus Pauling, <i>General Chemistry: An Introduction to Descriptive Chemistry and Modern Chemical Theory</i> (San Francisco: W. H. Freeman, 1947), https://archive.org/details/generalchemistry00paul. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Stainless steel resists rust because chromium in the alloy forms an invisible protective film.","a":true,"why":"A corrosion-resistant, or stainless, steel is at least 11 per cent chromium by mass<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> — the industry's own definition puts the floor at 10.5 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That chromium reacts with oxygen to form a transparent nanoscopic layer of oxide, which is a barrier to any further oxygen getting in and so keeps the ruddy flaky products of iron oxidation from forming at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> This passive film, as metallurgists call it, is made of the oxides and hydroxides of iron and chromium, and the alloy's corrosion resistance stands or falls with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chemistry","src":["Royal Society of Chemistry, “Chromium,” Periodic Table, https://periodic-table.rsc.org/element/24/chromium. [Open access]","British Stainless Steel Association, “What Is Stainless Steel,” https://bssa.org.uk/bssa_articles/what-is-stainless-steel/. [Open access]","Seung-Heon Choi, Young-Ran Yoo, and Young-Sik Kim, “Dynamic Variation in the Semiconductive Tendency of the Passive Film on Duplex Stainless Steel in Corrosion Environments,” <i>Materials</i> 17, no. 23 (2024): 5963, https://doi.org/10.3390/ma17235963. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Mount Everest is the tallest mountain on Earth when measured from its base.","a":false,"why":"Everest's summit is the highest point above mean sea level, at 8,848 metres (29,029 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> But Mauna Kea, a Hawaiian volcano that begins deep beneath the Pacific, rises more than 10,210 metres (33,500 feet) from base to peak, which is what makes it the tallest mountain on Earth measured that way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Earth science","src":["National Ocean Service, “What is the highest point on Earth as measured from Earth's center?” NOAA, last updated 16 June 2024, https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/highestpoint.html. [Open access]","NASA, “Highest, Tallest, and Closest to the Stars,” Astronomy Picture of the Day, 25 February 2016, https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap160225.html. [Open access]","Michael P. W. Grocott and Denny Z. H. Levett, “Everest 60 Years On: What Next?” <i>Extreme Physiology &amp; Medicine</i> 2 (2013): 20, https://doi.org/10.1186/2046-7648-2-20. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Earth is closest to the Sun in July.","a":false,"why":"Earth reaches perihelion, its closest approach to the Sun, on or about 3 January, and aphelion, its furthest, on or about 4 July, a difference in distance of only about 3 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The seasons follow the 23.5-degree tilt of Earth's axis, whose slow variation is what makes the seasonal contrast more or less extreme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Earth science","src":["Steve Graham, “Milutin Milankovitch,” NASA Earth Observatory, 24 March 2000, https://earthobservatory.nasa.gov/features/Milankovitch. [Open access]","OpenStax, “4.2 The Seasons,” <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-2-the-seasons. [Open access]","NASA, “When Is Earth Closest to the Sun?” StarChild Question of the Month, January 2003, https://starchild.gsfc.nasa.gov/docs/StarChild/questions/question53.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"A lightning bolt is hotter than the surface of the Sun.","a":true,"why":"The National Weather Service puts the air inside a lightning channel at about 27,800 °C (50,000 °F), which it reckons roughly five times the temperature of the sun’s surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Photometry of 23 return strokes put their average peak at 34,800 K, and the channel was still at 20,000 K ten milliseconds later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> NASA’s own figure for the photosphere is an effective temperature of 5,772 K, about 5,500 °C (9,930 °F) — and the sun is hotter than that all the way down, 15.7 million K at the centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Earth science","src":["National Weather Service, “Understanding Lightning Science,” National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration, https://www.weather.gov/safety/lightning-science-overview. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Sun Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/sunfact.html. [Open access]","Jacob Wemhoner, Adonis F. R. Leal, Caitano L. da Silva, and Richard Sonnenfeld, “Atomic Oxygen Photometric Temperature of Lightning and Its Sub-Processes with SOPAPILLA,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 4068, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-34189-8. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Sahara is the largest desert on Earth.","a":false,"why":"A desert is defined by how little precipitation it receives rather than by heat — under 250 millimetres (10 inches) a year — and by that measure the largest on Earth is Antarctica,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> whose ice sheet alone covers almost 14 million square kilometres (5.4 million square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Sahara, at about 8.5 million square kilometres (3.3 million square miles), is the largest hot desert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Earth science","src":["Sam Lambrechts, Anne Willems, and Guillaume Tahon, “Uncovering the Uncultivated Majority in Antarctic Soils: Toward a Synergistic Approach,” <i>Frontiers in Microbiology</i> 10 (2019): 242, https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2019.00242. [Open access]","National Snow and Ice Data Center, “Ice Sheets,” accessed 17 September 2026, https://nsidc.org/learn/parts-cryosphere/ice-sheets. [Open access]","Aicha Tedjani et al., “A Systematic Review of <i>Astragalus</i> Plants in the Septentrional Algerian Sahara,” <i>Frontiers in Nutrition</i> 12 (2025): 1683447, https://doi.org/10.3389/fnut.2025.1683447. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Diamonds are formed from coal squeezed under pressure.","a":false,"why":"Diamonds crystallise from fluids and melts circulating in the Earth's mantle, rather than from anything buried at the surface<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> — coal is a crustal compound never found at mantle pressures, and diamond does not even form by direct conversion of solid carbon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The deepest of them record melts released from subducting ocean floor 300 to 700 kilometres (190 to 430 miles) down, and thirteen dated from Brazil and Guinea crystallised between 450 and 650 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Earth science","src":["Aleksandr Rakipov, Alan B. Woodland, Fabrizio Nestola, Matilde Galiè, Martha G. Pamato et al., “Trace Element Systematics Constrain the Origin of Fluids That Form Gem-Quality Diamonds,” <i>Communications Earth &amp; Environment</i> 7, no. 1 (2026): 469, https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-026-03662-1. [Open access]","Suzette Timmerman, Thomas Stachel, Janne M. Koornneef, Karen V. Smit, Rikke Harlou et al., “Sublithospheric Diamond Ages and the Supercontinent Cycle,” <i>Nature</i> 623, no. 7988 (2023): 752–56, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-06662-9. [Open access]","Karen V. Smit and Steven B. Shirey, “Diamonds from the Deep: How Do Diamonds Form in the Deep Earth?” <i>Gems &amp; Gemology</i> 54, no. 4 (2018), https://www.gia.edu/gems-gemology/winter-2018-how-do-diamonds-form-in-the-deep-earth. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The shore of the Dead Sea is the lowest exposed land on Earth.","a":true,"why":"Its surface lies about 430 metres (1,410 feet) below sea level, which makes it the lowest point on the planet's land surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lower ground exists only under ice or water, such as the bedrock trenches beneath the Antarctic ice sheet — the deepest yet sounded, under Byrd Glacier, is about 2,870 metres (9,420 feet) below sea level<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> — and the shoreline itself keeps moving, the lake dropping as it shrinks.","cat":"Earth science","src":["Hala I. Al-Daghistani, Sima Zein, and Manal A. Abbas, “Microbial Communities in the Dead Sea and Their Potential Biotechnological Applications,” <i>Communicative & Integrative Biology</i> 17, no. 1 (2024): 2369782, https://doi.org/10.1080/19420889.2024.2369782. [Open access]","Jacob H. Jacob, Emad I. Hussein, Muhamad Ali K. Shakhatreh, and Christopher T. Cornelison, “Microbial Community Analysis of the Hypersaline Water of the Dead Sea Using High-Throughput Amplicon Sequencing,” <i>MicrobiologyOpen</i> 6, no. 5 (2017): e00500, https://doi.org/10.1002/mbo3.500. [Open access]","P. Fretwell, H. D. Pritchard, D. G. Vaughan, J. L. Bamber, N. E. Barrand et al., “Bedmap2: Improved Ice Bed, Surface and Thickness Datasets for Antarctica,” <i>The Cryosphere</i> 7, no. 1 (2013): 375–93, https://doi.org/10.5194/tc-7-375-2013. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"There are more real numbers between 0 and 1 than there are whole numbers altogether.","a":true,"why":"Georg Cantor had proved by December 1873 that the real numbers cannot be set in one-to-one correspondence with the whole numbers, and published it in 1874; the rationals, and even the algebraic numbers, he had already shown to be countable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The reals are therefore an infinity of a strictly larger kind than the integers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Mathematics","src":["J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Georg Ferdinand Ludwig Philipp Cantor,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, October 1998, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Cantor/. [Open access]","José Ferreirós, “The Early Development of Set Theory,” <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, first published 10 April 2007, revised 3 December 2025, https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/settheory-early/. [Open access]","Joseph Warren Dauben, <i>Georg Cantor: His Mathematics and Philosophy of the Infinite</i> (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1979), https://archive.org/details/georgcantorhisma0000daub_k6x7. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The exact value of pi has been calculated in full.","a":false,"why":"Lambert proved in 1761 that π is irrational, so its decimal expansion neither ends nor repeats and no finite calculation can complete it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lindemann went further in 1882 and proved it transcendental — not the root of any algebraic equation with rational coefficients.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Mathematics","src":["J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Carl Louis Ferdinand von Lindemann,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Lindemann/. [Open access]","J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Johann Heinrich Lambert,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Lambert/. [Open access]","Petr Beckmann, <i>A History of π (Pi)</i> (Boulder, CO: Golem Press, 1970), https://archive.org/details/historyofpipi0000beck. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"In a room of 23 people, the odds are better than even that two of them share a birthday.","a":true,"why":"The comparison is not between one person and the rest but between all 253 possible pairs, which makes a match far likelier than intuition suggests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With 23 people the probability is about 50.7 per cent, and with 70 it is over 99.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Mathematics","src":["Charles M. Grinstead and J. Laurie Snell, <i>Introduction to Probability</i>, 2nd rev. ed. (Providence, RI: American Mathematical Society, 1997), 77–79, https://math.dartmouth.edu/~prob/prob/prob.pdf. [Open access]","William Feller, <i>An Introduction to Probability Theory and Its Applications</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Wiley, 1950), ch. II, https://archive.org/details/introductiontopr01fell. [Open access]","Frederick Mosteller, <i>Fifty Challenging Problems in Probability with Solutions</i> (New York: Dover, 1987; first published 1965), problem 31, https://archive.org/details/fiftychallenging00most. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Cracking your knuckles causes arthritis.","a":false,"why":"Real-time magnetic resonance imaging of ten finger joints pulled apart shows the sound comes from a cavity forming in the joint fluid at the moment the surfaces separate, rather than from a bubble collapsing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A case-control study of 215 people aged 50 to 89 found hand osteoarthritis about as common among habitual knuckle-crackers as among everyone else — 18.1 per cent against 21.5 — with no association at any joint however long or often they had done it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two earlier surveys had found the same: 300 patients aged 45 and over showed no more hand arthritis among the 74 habitual crackers than among the rest,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and a geriatric sample showed no link between the habit and degenerative change in the knuckle joints.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","cat":"Medicine","src":["Gregory N. Kawchuk, Jerome Fryer, Jacob L. Jaremko, Hongbo Zeng, Lindsay Rowe, and Richard Thompson, “Real-Time Visualization of Joint Cavitation,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 4 (2015): e0119470, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0119470. [Open access]","Kevin deWeber, Mariusz Olszewski, and Rebecca Ortolano, “Knuckle Cracking and Hand Osteoarthritis,” <i>Journal of the American Board of Family Medicine</i> 24, no. 2 (2011): 169–74, https://doi.org/10.3122/jabfm.2011.02.100156. [Open access]","J. Castellanos and D. Axelrod, “Effect of Habitual Knuckle Cracking on Hand Function,” <i>Annals of the Rheumatic Diseases</i> 49, no. 5 (1990): 308–9, https://doi.org/10.1136/ard.49.5.308. [Open access]","R. L. Swezey and S. E. Swezey, “The Consequences of Habitual Knuckle Cracking,” <i>Western Journal of Medicine</i> 122, no. 5 (1975): 377–79, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1129752/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Most body heat is lost through the head.","a":false,"why":"The figure comes from the army: the U.S. Army's own survival manual tells soldiers to keep the head covered because \"you can lose 40 to 45 percent of body heat from an unprotected head.\"<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It rests on an old military experiment in which the subjects wore arctic survival suits but no hats, so the head was the only part exposed and duly lost the most heat; in swimsuits they would have lost no more than about a tenth of it that way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A later study found nothing special about the head as a radiator — in 17 °C (63 °F) water, submerging it raised total heat loss by only about a tenth, roughly its share of the body's surface — though chilling it did cool the core faster than that share predicts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Medicine","src":["U.S. Department of the Army, <i>Survival</i>, Field Manual 21-76 (5 June 1992), ch. 15, “Cold Weather Survival,” https://archive.org/details/milmanual-mcrp-3-02f-fm-21-76-survival. [Open access]","Rachel C. Vreeman and Aaron E. Carroll, “Festive Medical Myths,” <i>BMJ</i> 337 (2008): a2769, https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.a2769. [Paywalled]","Thea Pretorius, Gerald K. Bristow, Alan M. Steinman, and Gordon G. Giesbrecht, “Thermal Effects of Whole Head Submersion in Cold Water on Nonshivering Humans,” <i>Journal of Applied Physiology</i> 101, no. 2 (2006): 669–75, https://doi.org/10.1152/japplphysiol.01241.2005. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"Antibiotics do nothing against the viruses that cause colds and flu.","a":true,"why":"Most colds and flu are caused by viruses, and antibiotics are not effective against them; the drugs that work on viruses are the antivirals, used for influenza, HIV and herpes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Taking an antibiotic for a viral infection cannot shorten it, and it adds to the resistance that makes the drug less useful when a bacterial infection genuinely needs it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Medicine","src":["European Centre for Disease Prevention and Control, “Factsheet for the general public — Antimicrobial resistance,” https://www.ecdc.europa.eu/en/antimicrobial-resistance/facts/factsheets/general-public. [Open access]","Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, “Healthy Habits: Antibiotic Do's and Don'ts,” Antibiotic Prescribing and Use, https://www.cdc.gov/antibiotic-use/about/index.html. [Open access]","MedlinePlus, “Colds and Flu — Antibiotics,” Medical Encyclopedia, U.S. National Library of Medicine, reviewed 14 January 2026, https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002070.htm. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Taking vitamin C stops you catching a cold.","a":false,"why":"A Cochrane review pooling 29 trial comparisons over 11,306 people found that regular supplementation did not reduce how often people in the general community caught colds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same review found colds shortened by 8 per cent in adults and 14 per cent in children, and one narrow group whose risk of catching one was roughly halved: 598 marathon runners, skiers and soldiers on subarctic exercises.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Medicine","src":["Harri Hemilä and Elizabeth Chalker, “Vitamin C for Preventing and Treating the Common Cold,” <i>Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews</i> 2013, no. 1 (2013): CD000980, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8078152/. [Open access]","Robert M. Douglas and Harri Hemilä, “Vitamin C for Preventing and Treating the Common Cold,” <i>PLoS Medicine</i> 2, no. 6 (2005): e168, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pmed.0020168. [Open access]","MedlinePlus, “Vitamin C,” Medical Encyclopedia, U.S. National Library of Medicine, reviewed 21 January 2025, https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002404.htm. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The appendix is a useless leftover with no known function.","a":false,"why":"The vermiform appendix was long considered vestigial, and accumulating evidence now puts it in the gut-associated lymphoid tissue and in the regulation of the gut's own microbes, with a microbial population of its own rather than a passive extension of what passes through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One influential proposal makes it a protected reservoir of commensal biofilms that can help repopulate the colon after a diarrhoeal or infectious upset — a hypothesis rather than a settled fact, but a long way from no known function.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Medicine","src":["Shuai Wang, Jingbo Lv, Li Wang, and Xueyuan Cao, “Appendectomy and Colorectal Cancer: A Mini Review from the Perspective of Gut Microbiota and Mucosal Immunity,” <i>Frontiers in Cellular and Infection Microbiology</i> 16 (2026): 1859485, https://doi.org/10.3389/fcimb.2026.1859485. [Open access]","I. A. Kooij, S. Sahami, S. L. Meijer, C. J. Buskens, and A. A. te Velde, “The Immunology of the Vermiform Appendix: A Review of the Literature,” <i>Clinical &amp; Experimental Immunology</i> 186, no. 1 (2016): 1–9, https://doi.org/10.1111/cei.12821. [Paywalled]","R. Randal Bollinger, Andrew S. Barbas, Errol L. Bush, Shu S. Lin, and William Parker, “Biofilms in the Large Bowel Suggest an Apparent Function of the Human Vermiform Appendix,” <i>Journal of Theoretical Biology</i> 249, no. 4 (2007): 826–31, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jtbi.2007.08.032. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"A microwave oven cooks food from the inside out.","a":false,"why":"Microwaves are absorbed as they go in, so a slab of food heated in one is hottest at or near the surface and coldest at its centre — the more so the thicker it is against the penetration depth, which this study takes as 12 millimetres (0.5 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where the layer is thick relative to that depth the model gives “a very uneven profile … with the highest temperature at or near the surface” and the lowest at the centre, and the authors’ conclusion is that the two have to be properly balanced to avoid heating the layer unevenly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The grain of truth is real and the same paper explains it: surface cooling, by evaporation and by exchange with the air, moves the peak a few millimetres under the surface as heating goes on — inward from the outside, not outward from the middle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The energy goes into the water in the food and becomes heat as it is absorbed,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and how far it gets before that happens — the penetration depth — follows from the dielectric behaviour of water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Science & invention","src":["J. Houšová and K. Hoke, “Temperature Profiles in Microwave Heated Solid Foods of Slab Geometry: Influence of Process Parameters,” <i>Czech Journal of Food Sciences</i> 19, no. 3 (2001): 111–20, https://doi.org/10.17221/6586-CJFS. [Open access]","Michael Vollmer, “Physics of the Microwave Oven,” <i>Physics Education</i> 39, no. 1 (2004): 74–81, https://doi.org/10.1088/0031-9120/39/1/006. [Paywalled]","U.S. Food and Drug Administration, “Microwave Oven Radiation,” Resources for You (Radiation-Emitting Products), https://www.fda.gov/radiation-emitting-products/resources-you-radiation-emitting-products/microwave-oven-radiation. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The first published computer program was written by a woman.","a":true,"why":"The notes appended to Menabrea's memoir in <i>Scientific Memoirs</i> for 1843, signed only A. A. L., end by following in detail the steps through which the engine could compute the numbers of Bernoulli, with a diagram and table worked out for one of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their author was Ada Lovelace, and what they set out is what many consider the first computer program.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","cat":"Science & invention","src":["J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Augusta Ada Byron King, Countess of Lovelace,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, August 2002, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Lovelace/. [Open access]","Ada Augusta, Countess of Lovelace, “Notes by the Translator, Note G,” appended to L. F. Menabrea, “Sketch of the Analytical Engine Invented by Charles Babbage, Esq.,” in <i>Scientific Memoirs</i>, ed. Richard Taylor, vol. 3 (London: Richard and John E. Taylor, 1843), https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.53369. [Open access]","Betty Alexandra Toole, <i>Ada, the Enchantress of Numbers: Prophet of the Computer Age</i>, rev. and abridged ed. (Mill Valley, CA: Strawberry Press, 1998), https://archive.org/details/adaenchantressof00tool. [Open access]","J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Charles Babbage,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Babbage/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"People are either left-brained and logical or right-brained and creative.","a":false,"why":"Some functions are lateralised — the left-lateralised hubs include Broca’s area and Wernicke’s area — but a 2013 study that measured lateralisation between 7,266 regions in 1,011 people found the pattern to be local rather than whole-brain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its data were not consistent with individuals having a generally ‘left-brained’ or ‘right-brained’ network.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The popular pairing of the hemispheres with logic and creativity is a fantasy grown from a real asymmetry — creative thought activates a network that favours neither side<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> — and research on which hemisphere dominates a behaviour has been inconsistent enough that textbooks now stress how the two interact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Psychology","src":["Jared A. Nielsen, Brandon A. Zielinski, Michael A. Ferguson, Janet E. Lainhart, and Jeffrey S. Anderson, “An Evaluation of the Left-Brain vs. Right-Brain Hypothesis with Resting State Functional Connectivity Magnetic Resonance Imaging,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 8 (2013): e71275, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0071275. [Open access]","Michael C. Corballis, “Left Brain, Right Brain: Facts and Fantasies,” <i>PLoS Biology</i> 12, no. 1 (2014): e1001767, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1001767. [Open access]","OpenStax, “3.4 The Brain and Spinal Cord,” <i>Psychology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/3-4-the-brain-and-spinal-cord. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Listening to Mozart has been shown to make children more intelligent.","a":false,"why":"The effect began with a 1993 report of better performance on a spatial task after listening to one Mozart sonata,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> a finding a 2023 meta-analysis puts down to low study power and bias-related measurement artefacts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A 1999 meta-analysis of the follow-up studies had already found any enhancement small, confined to one kind of task and no change in IQ or reasoning in general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The claims about lasting gains in children’s intelligence were added afterwards by others, and are what carried the idea into public belief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Psychology","src":["Sandra Oberleiter and Jakob Pietschnig, “Unfounded Authority, Underpowered Studies, and Non-Transparent Reporting Perpetuate the Mozart Effect Myth: A Multiverse Meta-Analysis,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 3175, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-30206-w. [Open access]","Frances H. Rauscher, Gordon L. Shaw, and Catherine N. Ky, “Music and Spatial Task Performance,” <i>Nature</i> 365, no. 6447 (1993): 611, https://doi.org/10.1038/365611a0. [Paywalled]","Christopher F. Chabris, “Prelude or Requiem for the ‘Mozart Effect’?,” <i>Nature</i> 400, no. 6747 (1999): 826–27, https://doi.org/10.1038/23608. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"In Stanley Milgram's obedience experiments most volunteers went on delivering what they believed were dangerous electric shocks when an experimenter told them to.","a":true,"why":"Milgram recruited forty men and had them shock a learner in 15-volt steps up to 450 volts, with both the shocks and the learner's answers staged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the experimenter told them to carry on, 65 per cent went to the maximum setting — and to the point at which the learner had stopped responding altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A partial replication in 2006, halted at 150 volts for the participants' sake, found obedience only slightly lower than Milgram had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","cat":"Psychology","src":["OpenStax, “12.4 Conformity, Compliance, and Obedience,” <i>Psychology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/12-4-conformity-compliance-and-obedience. [Open access]","Stanley Milgram, “Behavioral Study of Obedience,” <i>Journal of Abnormal and Social Psychology</i> 67 (1963): 371–78, https://doi.org/10.1037/h0040525. [Paywalled]","S. Alexander Haslam and Stephen D. Reicher, “Contesting the ‘Nature’ of Conformity: What Milgram and Zimbardo's Studies Really Show,” <i>PLoS Biology</i> 10, no. 11 (2012): e1001426, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1001426. [Open access]","Jerry M. Burger, “Replicating Milgram: Would People Still Obey Today?,” <i>American Psychologist</i> 64, no. 1 (2009): 1–11, https://doi.org/10.1037/a0010932. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"Once a memory has been stored, recalling it leaves it unchanged, like replaying a recording.","a":false,"why":"Remembering is reconstruction rather than playback: bringing an old memory up combines it with newer ones, so events can be added and what we think we remember can change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In animals a consolidated fear memory that is reactivated returns to a labile state and has to be stored anew, with fresh protein synthesis, or is lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Elizabeth Loftus's misinformation work is the standing demonstration — shown inaccurate information after an event, a person may misremember the event itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Psychology","src":["OpenStax, “8.3 Problems with Memory,” <i>Psychology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/8-3-problems-with-memory. [Open access]","Elizabeth F. Loftus, “Planting Misinformation in the Human Mind: A 30-Year Investigation of the Malleability of Memory,” <i>Learning & Memory</i> 12, no. 4 (2005): 361–66, https://doi.org/10.1101/lm.94705. [Paywalled]","Karim Nader, Glenn E. Schafe, and Joseph E. Le Doux, “Fear Memories Require Protein Synthesis in the Amygdala for Reconsolidation after Retrieval,” <i>Nature</i> 406, no. 6797 (2000): 722–26, https://doi.org/10.1038/35021052. [Paywalled]","Daniel L. Schacter and Donna Rose Addis, “The Cognitive Neuroscience of Constructive Memory: Remembering the Past and Imagining the Future,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 362, no. 1481 (2007): 773–86, https://doi.org/10.1098/rstb.2007.2087. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"People can reliably spot a liar from body language at well above chance.","a":false,"why":"Across the experimental literature the overall accuracy of judging whether a statement is true or false is about 54 per cent, just above the 50 per cent that chance would give.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Individual behavioural cues to lying are faint and unreliable,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and the ones people are most confident about, such as avoiding eye contact — the dominant belief about liars in a survey of 75 countries — are stereotypes about liars rather than findings about them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","cat":"Psychology","src":["Sarah Volz, Marc-André Reinhard, and Patrick Müller, “Is It the Judge, the Sender, or Just the Individual Message? Disentangling Person and Message Effects on Variation in Lie-Detection Judgments,” <i>Perspectives on Psychological Science</i> 18, no. 6 (2023): 1368–87, https://doi.org/10.1177/17456916221149943. [Open access]","He Li et al., “Nonverbal cues to deception: insights from a mock crime scenario in a Chinese sample,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 15 (2024): 1331653, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1331653. [Open access]","Charles F. Bond Jr. and Bella M. DePaulo, “Accuracy of Deception Judgments,” <i>Personality and Social Psychology Review</i> 10, no. 3 (2006): 214–34, https://doi.org/10.1207/s15327957pspr1003_2. [Paywalled]","Bella M. DePaulo et al., “Cues to Deception,” <i>Psychological Bulletin</i> 129, no. 1 (2003): 74–118, https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.129.1.74. [Paywalled]","The Global Deception Research Team, “A World of Lies,” <i>Journal of Cross-Cultural Psychology</i> 37, no. 1 (2006): 60–74, https://doi.org/10.1177/0022022105282295. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"The murder that gave psychology the 'bystander effect' was reported with details that later turned out to be substantially wrong.","a":true,"why":"The 1964 killing of Kitty Genovese in New York was reported as having been watched by 38 witnesses who did nothing, and the study that went back to the archive material found no evidence for the presence of 38 witnesses, none that witnesses observed the murder, and none that they remained inactive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A later archival study adds that the tale of the unresponsive bystanders was first put about by the police, and that the killer was caught through the intervention of two bystanders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The authors of the first call the story a modern parable whose telling has served to limit what the discipline asked about emergency helping, and note the part it plays in psychology textbooks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are careful to set it apart from what they call the robust bystander research tradition: the effect itself rests on controlled experiments — more than a hundred effect sizes by a 2011 meta-analysis — and the account of the night it is named after does not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Psychology","src":["Rachel Manning, Mark Levine, and Alan Collins, “The Kitty Genovese Murder and the Social Psychology of Helping: The Parable of the 38 Witnesses,” <i>American Psychologist</i> 62, no. 6 (2007): 555–62, https://doi.org/10.1037/0003-066X.62.6.555. [Paywalled]","Saul M. Kassin, “The Killing of Kitty Genovese: What Else Does This Case Tell Us?,” <i>Perspectives on Psychological Science</i> 12, no. 3 (2017): 374–81, https://doi.org/10.1177/1745691616679465. [Paywalled]","Peter Fischer et al., “The Bystander-Effect: A Meta-Analytic Review on Bystander Intervention in Dangerous and Non-Dangerous Emergencies,” <i>Psychological Bulletin</i> 137, no. 4 (2011): 517–37, https://doi.org/10.1037/a0023304. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"Opposites attract: people tend to pair up with partners unlike themselves.","a":false,"why":"The consistent finding is the reverse. Assortative mating — the tendency to choose a partner similar to oneself — is described as a ubiquitous pattern in mate choice, well documented in humans across physical, personality and demographic characteristics, and in other species besides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An introductory textbook puts it flatly: there is no evidence that opposites attract, and we are drawn to the people most like us.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A 2023 meta-analysis of 22 traits, with a UK Biobank analysis of 133 more, found widespread similarity between partners, strongest for social attitudes, education and substance use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Psychology","src":["Kaitlyn T. Harper and Brendan P. Zietsch, “Assortative Mating Is a Natural Consequence of Heritable Variation in Preferences and Preferred Traits,” <i>Psychological Science</i> 36, no. 10 (2025): 771–79, https://doi.org/10.1177/09567976251365900. [Open access]","OpenStax, “12.7 Prosocial Behavior,” <i>Psychology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/12-7-prosocial-behavior. [Open access]","Tanya B. Horwitz et al., “Evidence of Correlations between Human Partners Based on Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses of 22 Traits and UK Biobank Analysis of 133 Traits,” <i>Nature Human Behaviour</i> 7, no. 9 (2023): 1568–83, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41562-023-01672-z. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"Teaching pupils in their own 'learning style' — visual, auditory or kinaesthetic — measurably improves how much they learn.","a":false,"why":"People readily report a preferred style and the belief is common in teaching: 58 per cent of 114 UK university academics surveyed in 2017 said they believed in using learning styles,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and the matching idea was the most widely believed of fifteen ‘neuromyths’ put to British and Dutch schoolteachers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the experiments test is whether matching instruction to that style helps: the 2008 review that set out what a fair test needs — learners sorted by style, randomly assigned to methods, and a crossover in the results — found virtually no evidence of that pattern,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and a review restricted to studies that screened learners for a preference, taught them in matched and unmatched conditions and looked for a crossover interaction found the effects very low and not significant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Psychology","src":["Philip M. Newton and Mahallad Miah, “Evidence-Based Higher Education — Is the Learning Styles ‘Myth’ Important?,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 8 (2017): 444, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2017.00444. [Open access]","Karoline Aslaksen and Håvard Lorås, “The Modality-Specific Learning Style Hypothesis: A Mini-Review,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 9 (2018): 1538, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2018.01538. [Open access]","Harold Pashler, Mark McDaniel, Doug Rohrer, and Robert Bjork, “Learning Styles: Concepts and Evidence,” <i>Psychological Science in the Public Interest</i> 9, no. 3 (2008): 105–19, https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1539-6053.2009.01038.x. [Paywalled]","Sanne Dekker, Nikki C. Lee, Paul Howard-Jones, and Jelle Jolles, “Neuromyths in Education: Prevalence and Predictors of Misconceptions among Teachers,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 3 (2012): 429, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2012.00429. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Testing yourself on material produces better long-term retention than spending the same time re-reading it.","a":true,"why":"A doctoral student at Illinois showed practice tests improving performance in 1909, and “more than 100 years of research has revealed that taking practice tests (versus merely rereading the material to be learned) can substantially boost student learning”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The review that rated ten study strategies put practice testing among the two most effective of them and rereading among the least, despite rereading being what most students do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the 2006 experiments that revived the field, students who took recall tests on a passage remembered substantially more of it two days and a week later than those who re-read it, even though re-reading left them more confident;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> a 2014 meta-analysis found the advantage of testing over restudy across the literature,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and it holds even against elaborate study with concept maps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","cat":"Psychology","src":["John Dunlosky, “Strengthening the Student Toolbox: Study Strategies to Boost Learning,” <i>American Educator</i> 37, no. 3 (Fall 2013), https://www.aft.org/ae/fall2013/dunlosky. [Open access]","Henry L. Roediger III and Jeffrey D. Karpicke, “Test-Enhanced Learning: Taking Memory Tests Improves Long-Term Retention,” <i>Psychological Science</i> 17, no. 3 (2006): 249–55, https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9280.2006.01693.x. [Paywalled]","C. A. Rowland, “The Effect of Testing versus Restudy on Retention: A Meta-Analytic Review of the Testing Effect,” <i>Psychological Bulletin</i> 140, no. 6 (2014): 1432–63, https://doi.org/10.1037/a0037559. [Paywalled]","Jeffrey D. Karpicke and Janell R. Blunt, “Retrieval Practice Produces More Learning than Elaborative Studying with Concept Mapping,” <i>Science</i> 331, no. 6018 (2011): 772–75, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1199327. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"Spreading study sessions out over days produces better long-term recall than putting the same total hours into one sitting.","a":true,"why":"Students “will retain knowledge and skills for a longer period of time when they distribute their practice than when they mass it, even if they use the same amount of time massing and distributing their practice”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A synthesis of 317 experiments found the advantage of spaced over massed study general,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and a study that taught facts to more than 1,350 people, with a review up to 3.5 months later and a final test up to a year after that, found the best gap between sessions grew as the test was set further off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Many students believe the opposite, because massed study makes the material feel familiar while it is in front of them — which is part of why cramming keeps being chosen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Psychology","src":["John Dunlosky, “Strengthening the Student Toolbox: Study Strategies to Boost Learning,” <i>American Educator</i> 37, no. 3 (Fall 2013), https://www.aft.org/ae/fall2013/dunlosky. [Open access]","Nicholas J. Cepeda, Harold Pashler, Edward Vul, John T. Wixted, and Doug Rohrer, “Distributed Practice in Verbal Recall Tasks: A Review and Quantitative Synthesis,” <i>Psychological Bulletin</i> 132, no. 3 (2006): 354–80, https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.132.3.354. [Paywalled]","Nicholas J. Cepeda, Edward Vul, Doug Rohrer, John T. Wixted, and Harold Pashler, “Spacing Effects in Learning: A Temporal Ridgeline of Optimal Retention,” <i>Psychological Science</i> 19, no. 11 (2008): 1095–1102, https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9280.2008.02209.x. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"The Stanford prison experiment is trusted because its results have been reproduced by other researchers.","a":false,"why":"It has not been replicated: the one comparable study, run for the BBC in 2002, deliberately gave the guards no guidance and reached different findings<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> — its guards failed to identify with their role, were reluctant to impose their authority and were eventually overcome by the prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Stanford archives show the research team asked the guards to be firmly in control of the prisoners,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and students given only the protocol could guess at ninety per cent what behaviour was expected of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Participants shown the wording of the Stanford guards' orientation in a later experiment expected hostile and oppressive behaviour of the investigator, of other guards and of themselves — the effect of a script.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","cat":"Psychology","src":["Thibault Le Texier, “Debunking the Stanford Prison Experiment,” PsyArXiv preprint, 8 August 2019, https://doi.org/10.31234/osf.io/mjhnp. [Open access]","Stephen Reicher and S. Alexander Haslam, “Rethinking the Psychology of Tyranny: The BBC Prison Study,” <i>British Journal of Social Psychology</i> 45, no. 1 (2006): 1–40, https://doi.org/10.1348/014466605X48998. [Paywalled]","S. Alexander Haslam, Stephen D. Reicher, and Jay J. Van Bavel, “Rethinking the Nature of Cruelty: The Role of Identity Leadership in the Stanford Prison Experiment,” <i>American Psychologist</i> 74, no. 7 (2019): 809–22, https://doi.org/10.1037/amp0000443. [Paywalled]","Jared Bartels, “Revisiting the Stanford Prison Experiment, Again: Examining Demand Characteristics in the Guard Orientation,” <i>Journal of Social Psychology</i> 159, no. 6 (2019): 780–90, https://doi.org/10.1080/00224545.2019.1596058. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"A famous 1957 experiment proved that split-second messages spliced into a cinema film made the audience buy more popcorn.","a":false,"why":"The claim did not survive its own public test: the market researcher James Vicary, who in 1957 announced that flashed “Eat popcorn” and “Drink Coca-Cola” messages had lifted a cinema's sales,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> tried the technique on cinema audiences and failed to prove that it worked, later admitting that his figures had been made up,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and subliminal influence had been largely debunked by the end of the 1950s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The trade kept trying it anyway — in 1962 the producer Harry Saltzman helped organise a London experiment on whether subliminal messages could heighten a cinema audience’s emotional response, a short-lived trend called “psychorama”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Saltzman’s own conclusion was that subliminal messaging would not increase box-office takings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Psychology","src":["Marcia Holmes, “Brainwashing the Cybernetic Spectator: <i>The Ipcress File</i>, 1960s Cinematic Spectacle and the Sciences of Mind,” <i>History of the Human Sciences</i> 30, no. 3 (2017): 3–24, https://doi.org/10.1177/0952695117703295. [Open access]","Gaëlle M. Bustin, Daniel N. Jones, Michel Hansenne, and Jordi Quoidbach, “Who Does Red Bull Give Wings To? Sensation Seeking Moderates Sensitivity to Subliminal Advertisement,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 6 (2015): 825, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2015.00825. [Open access]","Samuil Pavlevchev, Minah Chang, Alessandra Natascha Flöck, and Peter Walla, “Subliminal Word Processing: EEG Detects Word Processing below Conscious Awareness,” <i>Brain Sciences</i> 12, no. 4 (2022): 464, https://doi.org/10.3390/brainsci12040464. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"A placebo can produce a measurable improvement even when the patient is told outright that it is a placebo.","a":true,"why":"In a 2010 trial 80 patients with irritable bowel syndrome were randomised either to pills openly described as inert or to no treatment with the same contact with staff, and the placebo group reported significantly greater improvement at both 11 days and three weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every outcome measured was one the patients reported about themselves rather than a measure of the disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later trials found the same in chronic low back pain, where three weeks of pills honestly labelled placebo cut pain and disability against usual care,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and meta-analyses of the open-label trials — five by 2017, eleven by 2021 — report moderate to large effects, with the caveat that nobody in such a trial can be blinded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","cat":"Psychology","src":["Ted J. Kaptchuk, Elizabeth Friedlander, John M. Kelley, M. Norma Sanchez, Efi Kokkotou, Joyce P. Singer, Magda Kowalczykowski, Franklin G. Miller, Irving Kirsch, and Anthony J. Lembo, “Placebos without Deception: A Randomized Controlled Trial in Irritable Bowel Syndrome,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 5, no. 12 (2010): e15591, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0015591. [Open access]","Cláudia Carvalho, Joaquim Machado Caetano, Lidia Cunha, Paula Rebouta, Ted J. Kaptchuk, and Irving Kirsch, “Open-Label Placebo Treatment in Chronic Low Back Pain: A Randomized Controlled Trial,” <i>Pain</i> 157, no. 12 (2016): 2766–72, https://doi.org/10.1097/j.pain.0000000000000700. [Open access]","Melina von Wernsdorff, Martin Loef, Brunna Tuschen-Caffier, and Stefan Schmidt, “Effects of Open-Label Placebos in Clinical Trials: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 3855, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-83148-6. [Open access]","J. E. G. Charlesworth et al., “Effects of Placebos without Deception Compared with No Treatment: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis,” <i>Journal of Evidence-Based Medicine</i> 10, no. 2 (2017): 97–107, https://doi.org/10.1111/jebm.12251. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"Every even number greater than two has been proved to be the sum of two prime numbers.","a":false,"why":"This is Goldbach's conjecture, made in 1742 in a letter to Euler, and it is still an open question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its odd-number cousin, that every odd number above 5 is the sum of three primes, was proved in 2013,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> leaving the even case standing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Computers have checked it for every even number up to at least 400 trillion — to 4 × 10¹⁸ by 2014 — which is evidence and not a proof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","cat":"Mathematics","src":["J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Christian Goldbach,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, August 2006, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Goldbach/. [Open access]","H. A. Helfgott, “The Ternary Goldbach Conjecture Is True,” arXiv preprint arXiv:1312.7748, 30 December 2013, rev. 17 January 2014, https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.7748. [Open access]","Leonard Eugene Dickson, <i>History of the Theory of Numbers</i>, vol. 1, <i>Divisibility and Primality</i> (Washington: Carnegie Institution of Washington, 1919), https://archive.org/details/historyoftheoryo01dick_1. [Open access]","Tomás Oliveira e Silva, Siegfried Herzog, and Silvio Pardi, “Empirical Verification of the Even Goldbach Conjecture and Computation of Prime Gaps up to 4 × 10¹⁸,” <i>Mathematics of Computation</i> 83, no. 288 (2014): 2033–60, https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-2013-02787-1. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"There is no largest prime number, and this has been known since antiquity.","a":true,"why":"Euclid gives the proof in the <i>Elements</i>, as the proposition that prime numbers are more than any assigned multitude of prime numbers: take the least number the listed primes all measure, add one, and what results is divisible by none of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The argument is about two and a half thousand years old and has never needed improving, though a survey counts some 200 further proofs of the same theorem since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Mathematics","src":["Euclid, <i>Elements</i> 9.20, trans. Thomas L. Heath (New York: Dover, 1956), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0086:book=9:type=Prop:number=20. [Open access]","J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Prime Numbers,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, January 2018, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Prime_numbers/. [Open access]","Romeo Meštrović, “Euclid's Theorem on the Infinitude of Primes: A Historical Survey of Its Proofs (300 B.C.–2022) and Another New Proof,” arXiv preprint arXiv:1202.3670, 16 February 2012, rev. 25 July 2023, https://arxiv.org/abs/1202.3670. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Fermat's Last Theorem went unproved for more than three centuries after it was written down.","a":true,"why":"Fermat wrote it in the margin of his Diophantus — “I have discovered a truly remarkable proof which this margin is too small to contain” — and the note only became known when his son Samuel published the edition carrying it in 1670.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Andrew Wiles announced a proof in June 1993, withdrew it when a hole was found, and worked with Richard Taylor through 1994 before the argument held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The proof appeared in 1995 as two papers in the <i>Annals of Mathematics</i>, Wiles's own and a joint one with Taylor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","cat":"Mathematics","src":["J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Pierre de Fermat,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Fermat/. [Open access]","J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Fermat's last theorem,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Fermat%27s_last_theorem/. [Open access]","Andrew Wiles, “Modular Elliptic Curves and Fermat's Last Theorem,” <i>Annals of Mathematics</i> 141, no. 3 (1995): 443–551, https://doi.org/10.2307/2118559. [Paywalled]","Richard Taylor and Andrew Wiles, “Ring-Theoretic Properties of Certain Hecke Algebras,” <i>Annals of Mathematics</i> 141, no. 3 (1995): 553–72, https://doi.org/10.2307/2118560. [Paywalled]","Diophantus, <i>Arithmeticorum libri sex, et De numeris multangulis liber unus</i>, with the commentary of C. G. Bachet and the observations of Pierre de Fermat (Toulouse: Bernard Bosc, 1670), https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_6QIYIizG_v8C. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"A Möbius strip has only one side and one edge.","a":true,"why":"Join the two ends of a paper strip with a half twist, and a path traced from any point on the surface runs through the point that appears to lie on the other side of it, so there is only one side — the canonical example of a one-sided surface, as a paper on its shape puts it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Möbius came upon the surface in 1858, although by both publication and discovery the precedence belongs to Johann Listing, who found its properties in the same year, independently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Mathematics","src":["J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “August Ferdinand Möbius,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, January 1997, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Mobius/. [Open access]","J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Johann Benedict Listing,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, September 2000, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Listing/. [Open access]","E. L. Starostin and G. H. M. van der Heijden, “The Shape of a Möbius Strip,” <i>Nature Materials</i> 6, no. 8 (2007): 563–67, https://doi.org/10.1038/nmat1929. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"Zero was used as a number in Europe well before it reached India.","a":false,"why":"It went the other way: Brahmagupta set out rules for arithmetic with zero and with negative numbers in seventh-century India — the sum of two ciphers is cipher, a debt subtracted from zero is a fortune — the first attempt anyone is known to have made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Zero reached Europe through Arabic and Islamic mathematics, and when Fibonacci described the nine Indian symbols and the sign 0 around 1200 — his <i>Liber Abaci</i> of 1202 calls it <i>zephirum</i> — it went on not being widely used for a long time after that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","cat":"Mathematics","src":["J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “A history of Zero,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, November 2000, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Zero/. [Open access]","J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Brahmagupta,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, November 2000, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Brahmagupta/. [Open access]","Brahmagupta and Bhāskara, <i>Algebra, with Arithmetic and Mensuration, from the Sanscrit of Brahmegupta and Bháscara</i>, trans. Henry Thomas Colebrooke (London: John Murray, 1817), https://archive.org/details/algebrawitharith00brahuoft. [Open access]","David Eugene Smith and Louis Charles Karpinski, <i>The Hindu-Arabic Numerals</i> (Boston: Ginn, 1911), https://archive.org/details/hinduarabicnumer00smitrich. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"It has been proved that a circle cannot be squared with compass and straightedge.","a":true,"why":"Squaring the circle — constructing a square of the same area with ruler and compasses alone — was one of the classical problems of Greek mathematics, and Lambert's 1761 proof that π is irrational was not enough to settle it, since some algebraic numbers can be constructed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lindemann's 1882 proof that π is transcendental “finally established that squaring the circle with ruler and compasses is insoluble”,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> a result whose full proof Klein set out for students in 1895 and Hobson in his 1913 history of the problem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","cat":"Mathematics","src":["J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Carl Louis Ferdinand von Lindemann,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Lindemann/. [Open access]","J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Squaring the Circle,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, April 1999, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Squaring_the_circle/. [Open access]","Felix Klein, <i>Famous Problems of Elementary Geometry: The Duplication of the Cube, the Trisection of an Angle, the Quadrature of the Circle</i>, trans. Wooster Woodruff Beman and David Eugene Smith (Boston: Ginn, 1897), https://archive.org/details/famousproblemsof1897klei. [Open access]","E. W. Hobson, <i>“Squaring the Circle”: A History of the Problem</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1913), https://archive.org/details/squaringcirclehi00hobsuoft. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The chemical symbols for iron, lead and gold are taken from their Latin names rather than their English ones.","a":true,"why":"Fe, Pb and Au come from <i>ferrum</i>, <i>plumbum</i> and <i>aurum</i>, where the English names are Anglo-Saxon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Several other symbols work the same way, among them Ag for <i>argentum</i> and Sn for <i>stannum</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","cat":"Chemistry","src":["“Iron,” Periodic Table of Elements, Los Alamos National Laboratory, https://periodic.lanl.gov/26.shtml. [Open access]","Royal Society of Chemistry, “Lead,” Periodic Table, https://periodic-table.rsc.org/element/82/lead. [Open access]","Royal Society of Chemistry, “Gold,” Periodic Table, https://periodic-table.rsc.org/element/79/gold. [Open access]","“Silver,” Periodic Table of Elements, Los Alamos National Laboratory, https://periodic.lanl.gov/47.shtml. [Open access]","“Tin,” Periodic Table of Elements, Los Alamos National Laboratory, https://periodic.lanl.gov/50.shtml. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Water can be made to boil at room temperature.","a":true,"why":"A liquid boils where its vapour pressure matches the pressure around it, and the liquid–vapour curve gives water's boiling point at any pressure — 100 °C (212 °F) at one atmosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Seal water at 25 °C (77 °F) and pump the air out, and the vapour above it settles at 0.03 atmospheres — 3.17 kilopascals in the tables — which is the pressure at which room-temperature water boils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chemistry","src":["OpenStax, “10.4 Phase Diagrams,” <i>Chemistry 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/10-4-phase-diagrams. [Open access]","OpenStax, “13.6 Humidity, Evaporation, and Boiling,” <i>College Physics 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/13-6-humidity-evaporation-and-boiling. [Open access]","National Institute of Standards and Technology, “Water: Phase Change Data,” <i>NIST Chemistry WebBook</i>, Standard Reference Database 69, https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C7732185&Mask=4. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Iron that has rusted weighs less than the iron it formed from.","a":false,"why":"It weighs more. Lavoisier burnt iron in a closed vessel and found that the weight the metal gained was exactly the weight the air inside lost, so rust is iron that has taken something on rather than iron that has given something up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Modern chemistry writes rust as a hydrated iron(III) oxide, made when iron meets oxygen and water,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the weight gain of a calcined metal had been noticed long before Lavoisier: Jean Rey argued in 1630 that tin and lead grow heavier on calcination because they take up air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The red oxide of iron is his own renaming of what chemistry until then had called the rust of iron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Chemistry","src":["Antoine Laurent Lavoisier, <i>Elements of Chemistry, in a New Systematic Order, Containing All the Modern Discoveries</i>, trans. Robert Kerr (Edinburgh: William Creech, 1790), chap. 3, Project Gutenberg, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/30775. [Open access]","OpenStax, “17.6 Corrosion,” <i>Chemistry 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/17-6-corrosion. [Open access]","Jean Rey, <i>Essays of Jean Rey, Doctor of Medicine, on an Enquiry into the Cause Wherefore Tin and Lead Increase in Weight on Calcination (1630)</i>, Alembic Club Reprints 11 (Edinburgh: William F. Clay, 1895), https://archive.org/details/essaysjeanreydo00reygoog. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Glass loses quality each time it is recycled, so it can only go round a limited number of times.","a":false,"why":"Glass is chemically stable enough to be “recycled infinitely without degradation of its molecular structure”,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> going round in a closed loop indefinitely so long as it is kept free of contamination, food waste and mixed colours included,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> which is exactly what most plastics cannot manage: their chains crack on remelting and the material comes back weaker each time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Paper fibres suffer the same way: recycled pulp ‘hornifies’, losing its swelling and bonding, and the sheet comes back weaker.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The practical limits on glass are collection and colour sorting rather than the material itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Chemistry","src":["Muhammad Jamshaid Zafar, Hamada Elsayed, and Enrico Bernardo, “Waste Glass Upcycling Supported by Alkali Activation: An Overview,” <i>Materials</i> 17, no. 9 (2024): 2169, https://doi.org/10.3390/ma17092169. [Open access]","Edward Harrison, Aydin Berenjian, and Mostafa Seifan, “Recycling of Waste Glass as Aggregate in Cement-Based Materials,” <i>Environmental Science and Ecotechnology</i> 4 (2020): 100064, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ese.2020.100064. [Open access]","Hailan Jin, Ryota Kose, Nobushige Akada, and Takayuki Okayama, “Relationship between Wettability of Pulp Fibers and Tensile Strength of Paper during Recycling,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 1560, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-05514-2. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Mendeleev left gaps in his periodic table for elements nobody had found, and predicted what they would be like.","a":true,"why":"By 1869 Mendeleev had arranged the known elements by their properties and atomic weights and found prominent gaps, which he named after the element above each one — eka-aluminium, ekasilicon and the rest — and gave a forecast atomic weight and density.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Gallium turned up below aluminium in 1875,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> germanium below silicon in 1886,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and his ekasilicon weight of 72 against a real 72.6 was the closest of the lot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Chemistry","src":["Royal Society of Chemistry, “Gallium,” Periodic Table, https://periodic-table.rsc.org/element/31/gallium. [Open access]","Royal Society of Chemistry, “Germanium,” Periodic Table, https://periodic-table.rsc.org/element/32/germanium. [Open access]","OpenStax, “2.5 The Periodic Table,” <i>Chemistry 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/2-5-the-periodic-table. [Open access]","D. Mendeléeff, <i>The Principles of Chemistry</i>, 2nd English ed., trans. George Kamensky, ed. T. A. Lawson, vol. 2 (London: Longmans, Green, 1897), https://archive.org/details/principleschemi01lawsgoog. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The word 'vaccine' comes from the Latin for cow.","a":true,"why":"Edward Jenner called the cowpox he inoculated with the <i>variolae vaccinae</i> — the smallpox of the cow — and put the phrase in the title of the 1798 <i>Inquiry</i> that reported the procedure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <i>Vaccinus</i> is the Latin adjective from <i>vacca</i>, a cow, and Lewis and Short gloss it simply “of or from cows.”<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The etymology column of the CDC's own journal derives “vaccination” from <i>vacca</i> the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Medicine","src":["Edward Jenner, <i>An Inquiry into the Causes and Effects of the Variolæ Vaccinæ, a Disease Discovered in Some of the Western Counties of England, Particularly Gloucestershire, and Known by the Name of the Cow Pox</i> (London: printed for the author by Sampson Low, 1798), https://archive.org/details/b24759247. [Open access]","Charlton T. Lewis and Charles Short, “vaccīnus,” in <i>A Latin Dictionary</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0059:entry=vaccinus. [Open access]","Nancy Männikkö, “Etymologia: Variola and Vaccination,” <i>Emerging Infectious Diseases</i> 17, no. 4 (2011): 680, https://doi.org/10.3201/eid1704.et1704. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Sugar makes children hyperactive.","a":false,"why":"At least twelve double-blind randomised controlled trials have compared children on diets carrying different amounts of sugar, and not one of them — not even the trials confined to children with attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder — could find any difference in behaviour between the children given sugar and the children given none; a 1995 meta-analysis of 23 such studies found every mean effect size statistically indistinguishable from zero.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That holds for sugar from sweets, from chocolate and from natural sources alike, and for the children whose parents thought them sensitive to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What does change is the parents: told their child has had a sugary drink when the drink was really sugar-free, they rate the child's behaviour as more hyperactive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Medicine","src":["Rachel C. Vreeman and Aaron E. Carroll, “Festive Medical Myths,” <i>BMJ</i> 337 (2008): a2769, https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.a2769. [Paywalled]","M. L. Wolraich, D. B. Wilson, and J. W. White, “The Effect of Sugar on Behavior or Cognition in Children: A Meta-analysis,” <i>JAMA</i> 274, no. 20 (1995): 1617–21, https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.1995.03530200053037. [Paywalled]","Daniel W. Hoover and Richard Milich, “Effects of Sugar Ingestion Expectancies on Mother-Child Interactions,” <i>Journal of Abnormal Child Psychology</i> 22, no. 4 (1994): 501–15, https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02168088. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"Shaving makes hair grow back thicker and darker.","a":false,"why":"A clinical trial as early as 1928 showed that shaving has no effect on hair growth, and later studies confirm it changes neither the thickness nor the rate of regrowth; the razor takes off the dead shaft and leaves the living part below the skin untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In one such study five young men shaved one leg weekly for months and left the other alone, and no difference could be found in the weight, width or growth rate of the hair on the two legs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It feels coarse because a shaved hair has none of the fine taper an unshaved end has, and looks darker because the new growth has not yet been lightened by the sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Medicine","src":["Rachel C. Vreeman and Aaron E. Carroll, “Medical myths,” <i>BMJ</i> 335, no. 7633 (2007): 1288–89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2151163/. [Open access]","Karthikeyan Kaliaperumal, “Tonsuring: Myths and Facts,” <i>International Journal of Trichology</i> 1, no. 1 (2009): 33, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2929550/. [Open access]","OpenStax, <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), §5.2 “Accessory Structures of the Skin,” https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/5-2-accessory-structures-of-the-skin. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The active ingredient in aspirin has a natural relative in willow bark, which was used as a medicine for thousands of years before the drug existed.","a":true,"why":"Willow bark has been used medicinally for more than three and a half thousand years: the Sumerians and ancient Egyptians took it as a painkiller and a fever remedy, and Hippocrates gave it for inflammatory pain in the fourth century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Salicin was refined into aspirin in 1897, the name joining acetyl to Spirsäure, when Felix Hoffmann acetylated salicylic acid to spare the stomach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Medicine","src":["Chun-Ru Lin, Sung Huang Laurent Tsai, Che Wang, Cheng-Lin Lee, Shao-Wen Hung, Yi-Tang Ting, and Yu Chiang Hung, “Willow Bark (<i>Salix</i> spp.) Used for Pain Relief in Arthritis: A Meta-Analysis of Randomized Controlled Trials,” <i>Life</i> 13, no. 10 (2023): 2058, https://doi.org/10.3390/life13102058. [Open access]","J. G. Mahdi, A. J. Mahdi, A. J. Mahdi, and I. D. Bowen, “The Historical Analysis of Aspirin Discovery, Its Relation to the Willow Tree and Antiproliferative and Anticancer Potential,” <i>Cell Proliferation</i> 39, no. 2 (2006): 147–55, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6496865/. [Open access]","M. J. R. Desborough and D. M. Keeling, “The Aspirin Story – From Willow to Wonder Drug,” <i>British Journal of Haematology</i> 177, no. 5 (2017): 674–83, https://doi.org/10.1111/bjh.14520. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"Ignaz Semmelweis's evidence that handwashing cut deaths in a maternity ward was accepted by the medical profession as soon as he presented it.","a":false,"why":"In his Vienna clinic 459 of 4,010 women died in 1846, 11.4 per cent; after he required every attendant to wash in chlorine solution from the middle of May 1847 the figure fell to about 5 per cent, and to 1.3 per cent in 1848.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His teachings were, in his own words, either ignored or attacked,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and he was committed to an asylum in 1865 and died there of sepsis at 47, a fortnight after his admission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","cat":"Medicine","src":["Vanessa Ravel, Chandini Pandiyan, and Krupa Chandran, “Ignaz Philipp Semmelweis (1818–1865) – a public health visionary and champion of hand hygiene,” <i>GMS Hygiene and Infection Control</i> 20 (2025): Doc79, https://doi.org/10.3205/dgkh000608. [Open access]","Ignaz Semmelweis, <i>Die Aetiologie, der Begriff und die Prophylaxis des Kindbettfiebers</i> (1861), in <i>Semmelweis’ gesammelte Werke</i>, ed. Tiberius von Győry (Jena: Gustav Fischer, 1905), 131, 265, https://archive.org/details/semmelweisgesamm00semm. [Open access]","T. D. Noakes, J. Borresen, T. Hew-Butler, M. I. Lambert, and E. Jordaan, “Semmelweis and the Aetiology of Puerperal Sepsis 160 Years On: An Historical Review,” <i>Epidemiology and Infection</i> 136, no. 1 (2008): 1–9, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2870773/. [Open access]","N. Kadar, “Rediscovering Ignaz Philipp Semmelweis (1818–1865),” <i>American Journal of Obstetrics and Gynecology</i> 220, no. 1 (2019): 26–39, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ajog.2018.11.1084. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"The summit furthest from the centre of the Earth is not Mount Everest.","a":true,"why":"That distinction belongs to Chimborazo in Ecuador, which stands one degree south of the equator where Earth's bulge is greatest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its summit is 6,268 metres (20,564 feet) above sea level, yet the bulge puts it more than 2,072 metres (6,800 feet) further from Earth's centre than Everest's peak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Earth science","src":["National Ocean Service, “What is the highest point on Earth as measured from Earth's center?” NOAA, last updated 16 June 2024, https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/highestpoint.html. [Open access]","NASA Earth Observatory, “Chimborazo and Tungurahua, Ecuador,” Image of the Day, 11 December 2005, https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/chimborazo-and-tungurahua-ecuador-6099/. [Open access]","Robert Nemiroff and Jerry Bonnell, “Highest, Tallest, and Closest to the Stars,” Astronomy Picture of the Day, NASA, 25 February 2016, https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap160225.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Sahara has been green grassland with lakes and rivers within the span of human history.","a":true,"why":"A strengthened summer monsoon watered the Sahara, turning the world's largest hot desert into grassland, lakes and rivers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It began about 15,000 years ago and had closed by roughly 5,500, driven by precession, the slow wobble that brings northern summers nearer the Sun<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> — and Lake Megachad alone covered some 361,000 square kilometres (139,000 square miles), carrying fish and hippopotamus across ground that is now sand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Leaf-wax records from sea-floor cores off West Africa show the monsoon rains reaching 31 degrees north, deep into the present desert, in one of a succession of wet phases paced by orbital precession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Earth science","src":["Martin H. Trauth, Asfawossen Asrat, Nadine Berner, Faysal Bibi, Verena Foerster et al., “Early Warning Signals of the Termination of the African Humid Period(s),” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 3697, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-47921-1. [Open access]","Nick A. Drake, Roger M. Blench, Simon J. Armitage, Charlie S. Bristow, and Kevin H. White, “Ancient Watercourses and Biogeography of the Sahara Explain the Peopling of the Desert,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 108, no. 2 (2011): 458–62, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1012231108. [Open access]","Jessica E. Tierney, Francesco S. R. Pausata, and Peter B. deMenocal, “Rainfall Regimes of the Green Sahara,” <i>Science Advances</i> 3, no. 1 (2017): e1601503, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1601503. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Earth's magnetic field has reversed its polarity many times in the geological past.","a":true,"why":"Grains of magnetite in lava line up with the field as the rock cools and hold that direction, and palaeomagnetists found early in the 20th century that rocks fall into two groups, one aligned with the present field and one opposite to it; potassium–argon dating of young lavas in the 1960s showed the two groups alternating in time, as reversals of the main dipole field would require.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The sea floor either side of a mid-ocean ridge carries those two polarities in matching stripes, so the oceanic crust is in effect a tape recording of the reversals, and the palaeomagnetic record shows the field strengthening, weakening and changing polarity again and again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Earth science","src":["W. Jacquelyne Kious and Robert I. Tilling, “Developing the Theory,” in <i>This Dynamic Earth: The Story of Plate Tectonics</i> (U.S. Geological Survey, 1996), https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/dynamic/developing.html. [Open access]","A. Cox, R. R. Doell, and G. B. Dalrymple, “Geomagnetic Polarity Epochs: Sierra Nevada II,” <i>Science</i> 142, no. 3590 (1963): 382–85, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.142.3590.382-b. [Paywalled]","NOAA National Centers for Environmental Information, “Geomagnetism Frequently Asked Questions,” https://www.ngdc.noaa.gov/geomag/faqgeom.shtml. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Alfred Wegener's proposal that the continents had drifted was accepted by geologists soon after he published it in 1912.","a":false,"why":"The scientific community of 1912 “firmly believed the continents and oceans to be permanent features on the Earth's surface”, and the proposal “was not well received”: its fatal weakness was that it could name no force able to move a continent, and Harold Jeffreys showed that solid rock could not plough through the ocean floor without breaking up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Wegener died on the Greenland ice cap in 1930,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and it was ocean-floor exploration after his death — seafloor spreading theorised in 1961,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and the magnetic striping beside it — that revived the idea and became plate tectonics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Earth science","src":["W. Jacquelyne Kious and Robert I. Tilling, “Historical Perspective,” in <i>This Dynamic Earth: The Story of Plate Tectonics</i> (U.S. Geological Survey, 1996), https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/dynamic/historical.html. [Open access]","W. Jacquelyne Kious and Robert I. Tilling, “Developing the Theory,” in <i>This Dynamic Earth: The Story of Plate Tectonics</i> (U.S. Geological Survey, 1996), https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/dynamic/developing.html. [Open access]","Patrick Hughes, “Alfred Wegener,” NASA Earth Observatory, https://earthobservatory.nasa.gov/features/Wegener. [Open access]","Robert S. Dietz, “Continent and Ocean Basin Evolution by Spreading of the Sea Floor,” <i>Nature</i> 190 (1961): 854–57, https://doi.org/10.1038/190854a0. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"Nothing can travel faster than light in any medium.","a":false,"why":"Nothing outruns light in a vacuum, but light itself slows in matter,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and charged particles routinely exceed that reduced speed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The blue glow around a submerged reactor core is Cherenkov radiation, produced by electrons moving through water faster than light does in water.","cat":"Physics","src":["Pavel A. Cherenkov, “Radiation of Particles Moving at a Velocity Exceeding That of Light, and Some of the Possibilities for Their Use in Experimental Physics,” Nobel Lecture, 11 December 1958, 4–5, https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/cerenkov-lecture.pdf. [Open access]","OpenStax, “1.1 The Propagation of Light,” <i>University Physics Volume 3</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/1-1-the-propagation-of-light. [Open access]","Il'ja M. Frank, “Optics of Light Sources Moving in Refractive Media,” Nobel Lecture, 11 December 1958, https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/frank-lecture.pdf. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"A hammer and a feather dropped together on the Moon reach the ground at the same moment, and this has been tested there.","a":true,"why":"At the end of the third Apollo 15 moonwalk David Scott released a 1.32-kilogram (2.9-pound) geological hammer and a 0.03-kilogram (1-ounce) falcon feather together from about 1.6 metres (5 feet 3 inches), in front of a live television camera.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Preliminary Science Report records that within the accuracy of the release the two underwent the same acceleration and struck the surface at the same moment, there being no air to hold the feather back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Physics","src":["David R. Williams, “The Apollo 15 Hammer-Feather Drop,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, quoting Joe Allen in <i>Apollo 15 Preliminary Science Report</i>, NASA SP-289 (1972), 2-11, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/apollo_15_feather_drop.html. [Open access]","Joseph P. Allen, “Summary of Scientific Results,” in <i>Apollo 15 Preliminary Science Report</i>, NASA SP-289 (Washington, DC: NASA, 1972), 2-11, https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19720015164/downloads/19720015164.pdf. [Open access]","Robert Nemiroff and Jerry Bonnell, “Hammer Versus Feather on the Moon,” Astronomy Picture of the Day, NASA, 1 November 2011, https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap111101.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The electron was identified before anyone knew that atoms have a nucleus.","a":true,"why":"J. J. Thomson showed in 1897 that cathode rays are made of particles — electrons — that conduct electricity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The nucleus came more than a decade later, out of Rutherford's investigations into the scattering of alpha rays and the inner structure of the atom that caused it: the gold-foil experiments that put a small, heavy, positively charged nucleus at the centre of the atom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Physics","src":["“J.J. Thomson – Facts,” Nobel Prize Outreach, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/1906/thomson/facts/. [Open access]","“Ernest Rutherford – Biographical,” Nobel Prize Outreach, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/1908/rutherford/biographical/. [Open access]","OpenStax, “2.2 Evolution of Atomic Theory,” <i>Chemistry 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/2-2-evolution-of-atomic-theory. [Open access]","J. J. Thomson, “Carriers of Negative Electricity,” Nobel Lecture, 11 December 1906, https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/thomson-lecture.pdf. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Absolute zero has been reached in the laboratory.","a":false,"why":"Cooling experiments now reach billionths and even trillionths of a degree above zero — a Bose–Einstein condensate at MIT was brought to about 450 trillionths of a degree in 2003 — but never zero itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The third law of thermodynamics is the reason: no process can bring a system to absolute zero in a finite number of steps or in a finite time, and each stage of cooling carries away only a fraction of the energy that is left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Physics","src":["Lluís Masanes and Jonathan Oppenheim, “A General Derivation and Quantification of the Third Law of Thermodynamics,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 8 (2017): 14538, https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms14538. [Open access]","OpenStax, “4.7 Entropy on a Microscopic Scale,” <i>University Physics Volume 2</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/4-7-entropy-on-a-microscopic-scale. [Open access]","A. E. Leanhardt, T. A. Pasquini, M. Saba, A. Schirotzek, Y. Shin, D. Kielpinski, D. E. Pritchard, and W. Ketterle, “Cooling Bose-Einstein Condensates Below 500 Picokelvin,” <i>Science</i> 301, no. 5639 (2003): 1513–15, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1088827. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"The Moon is slowly moving away from the Earth.","a":true,"why":"NASA’s Moon fact sheet gives a recession rate from the earth of 3.8 centimetres (1.5 inches) a year, against a mean distance of 384,400 kilometres (238,900 miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> — a drift measured by timing laser pulses bounced off the reflectors the Apollo crews left on the surface, and about the pace at which a fingernail grows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At that rate the moon has moved about 3.8 metres (12 feet) further off since 1926.","cat":"Astronomy","src":["David R. Williams, “Moon Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/moonfact.html. [Open access]","William Steigerwald, “Laser Beams Reflected Between Earth and Moon Boost Science,” NASA, 10 August 2020, https://www.nasa.gov/missions/laser-beams-reflected-between-earth-and-moon-boost-science. [Open access]","NASA, “Moon Facts,” NASA Science, last updated 12 February 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/moon/facts/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Jupiter is more massive than every other planet in the solar system put together.","a":true,"why":"Jupiter took most of the material left over after the Sun formed, ending up with more than twice the combined mass of everything else orbiting it;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> NASA's planetary fact sheet puts it at roughly two and a half times the mass of the other seven planets together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is 11 times wider than Earth, and still has nowhere near enough mass to have ignited as a star.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Astronomy","src":["NASA, “Jupiter Facts,” NASA Science, last updated 21 April 2025, https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-facts/. [Open access]","OpenStax, “7.1 Overview of Our Planetary System,” <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/7-1-overview-of-our-planetary-system. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Planetary Fact Sheet – Metric,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, last updated 18 March 2025, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Halley's Comet was first recorded by Edmond Halley.","a":false,"why":"Astronomers have linked the comet's appearances to observations going back more than two thousand years — Babylonian tablets in the British Museum very probably record its passages of 164 and 87 BCE — and it appears in the Bayeux Tapestry's record of the Battle of Hastings in 1066.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Halley's own contribution, in 1705, was to notice that the bright comets of 1531, 1607 and 1682 were one object returning, and to predict correctly that it would come back in 1758.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Astronomy","src":["NASA, “1P/Halley,” NASA Science, last updated 3 November 2024, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/comets/1p-halley/. [Open access]","Edmond Halley, <i>A Synopsis of the Astronomy of Comets</i> (London: John Senex, 1705), https://archive.org/details/synopsisofastron00hall. [Open access]","F. R. Stephenson, K. K. C. Yau, and H. Hunger, “Records of Halley’s Comet on Babylonian Tablets,” <i>Nature</i> 314 (1985): 587–92, https://doi.org/10.1038/314587a0. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"Humans are more closely related to mushrooms than mushrooms are to plants.","a":true,"why":"Analysis of the fungal genome shows that fungi, long taken for plants, are more closely related to animals than to plants; protein and ribosomal-RNA comparisons in 1993 placed animals and fungi together as sister groups, with plants on a separate lineage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They keep other animal habits too: they store carbohydrate as glycogen rather than as starch, and build their cell walls from chitin, the material of an insect's exoskeleton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["OpenStax, “24.1 Characteristics of Fungi,” <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/24-1-characteristics-of-fungi. [Open access]","S. L. Baldauf and J. D. Palmer, “Animals and Fungi Are Each Other’s Closest Relatives: Congruent Evidence from Multiple Proteins,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 90, no. 24 (1993): 11558–62, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC48023/. [Open access]","P. O. Wainright, G. Hinkle, M. L. Sogin, and S. K. Stickel, “Monophyletic Origins of the Metazoa: An Evolutionary Link with Fungi,” <i>Science</i> 260, no. 5106 (1993): 340–42, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.8469985. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"A giraffe has the same number of neck vertebrae as a human being.","a":true,"why":"The count of seven cervical vertebrae is highly conserved across mammals, and the giraffe has just seven — long, flexible neck and all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The only mammals that depart from it are the manatees and the two living genera of tree sloth, one running down to five vertebrae and the other up to ten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Luisa J. F. Merten, Armita R. Manafzadeh, Eva C. Herbst, Eli Amson, P. Sebastián Tambusso, Patrick Arnold, and John A. Nyakatura, “The functional significance of aberrant cervical counts in sloths: insights from automated exhaustive analysis of cervical range of motion,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 290, no. 2010 (2023): 20231592, https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2023.1592. [Open access]","Frietson Galis, “Why Do Almost All Mammals Have Seven Cervical Vertebrae? Developmental Constraints, Hox Genes, and Cancer,” <i>Journal of Experimental Zoology</i> 285, no. 1 (1999): 19–26, https://doi.org/10.1002/(SICI)1097-010X(19990415)285:1%3C19::AID-JEZ3%3E3.0.CO;2-Z. [Paywalled]","Irma Varela-Lasheras, Alexander J. Bakker, Steven D. van der Mije, Johan A. J. Metz, Joris van Alphen, and Frietson Galis, “Breaking Evolutionary and Pleiotropic Constraints in Mammals: On Sloths, Manatees and Homeotic Mutations,” <i>EvoDevo</i> 2 (2011): 11, https://doi.org/10.1186/2041-9139-2-11. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The blue whale is the largest living thing that has ever existed on Earth.","a":false,"why":"It is the largest animal — the largest ever to live, as NOAA puts it — which is the narrower claim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pando, a single male quaking aspen in Utah whose 40,000-odd trees share one root system, is called by the Forest Service the largest organism ever found, at nearly 5,900 tonnes (13 million pounds) spread over 43 hectares (106 acres), and microsatellite sampling of 209 stems in 2008 confirmed the stand as one genetic individual covering about 43.6 hectares (108 acres).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is thought to have started at the end of the last ice age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["U.S. Forest Service, “Pando,” Fishlake National Forest, https://www.fs.usda.gov/r04/fishlake/recreation/explore-forest/pando. [Open access]","NOAA Fisheries, “Blue Whale,” species directory, https://www.fisheries.noaa.gov/species/blue-whale. [Open access]","Jennifer DeWoody, Carol A. Rowe, Valerie D. Hipkins, and Karen E. Mock, “‘Pando’ Lives: Molecular Genetic Evidence of a Giant Aspen Clone in Central Utah,” <i>Western North American Naturalist</i> 68, no. 4 (2008): art. 8, https://scholarsarchive.byu.edu/wnan/vol68/iss4/8. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Chameleons change colour chiefly to blend in with their surroundings.","a":false,"why":"A comparative study of southern African dwarf chameleons found that the species with the most dramatic colour change are the ones whose social displays contrast most against their background, and found no evidence at all for camouflage driving it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The colours come from actively tuning a lattice of guanine nanocrystals in the skin, over a deeper layer of larger crystals that reflects much of the sunlight falling on the animal in the near infrared and may serve as passive thermal protection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In staged contests between male veiled chameleons, the males whose stripes brightened most were the ones that approached, and the brightness and speed of the head's colour change predicted who won.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Devi Stuart-Fox and Adnan Moussalli, “Selection for Social Signalling Drives the Evolution of Chameleon Colour Change,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 6, no. 1 (2008): e25, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.0060025. [Open access]","Jérémie Teyssier, Suzanne V. Saenko, Dirk van der Marel, and Michel C. Milinkovitch, “Photonic Crystals Cause Active Colour Change in Chameleons,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 6 (2015): 6368, https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms7368. [Open access]","Russell A. Ligon and Kevin J. McGraw, “Chameleons Communicate with Complex Colour Changes during Contests: Different Body Regions Convey Different Information,” <i>Biology Letters</i> 9, no. 6 (2013): 20130892, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3871380/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Julius Caesar was assassinated in the Senate House of Rome.","a":false,"why":"The senate was not in the Curia that day: it met in the hall attached to Pompey's theatre, and Suetonius says the conspirators settled on the Ides of March because a meeting had been called there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Appian has Caesar turn on one man after another until the blow from Brutus, when he veiled himself and fell at the foot of Pompey's statue — the rival he had beaten — and says several of the assassins wounded each other in the press.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 2.117, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/2*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Caesar</i> 66, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 7 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1919), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Caesar*.html. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>The Lives of the Caesars</i>, “The Deified Julius” 80, trans. J. C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Julius*.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Marco Polo brought pasta to Italy from China.","a":false,"why":"The only two passages in Yule’s two volumes that name a pasta at all are both Ramusio’s, digressions added to the text in the sixteenth century, and the first of them explains Chinese wheat to Italian readers: “this they use only to make vermicelli, and pastes of that description”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second is about Sumatra, where palm-pith flour is “made into pasta in strips and other forms”, which Marco “often partook of, and brought some with him to Venice”, and which “resembles barley bread and tastes much the same”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> So the one thing the book has him carry home is sago, and neither passage has him bringing noodles from China to Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ramusio's Italian has the Chinese eat their wheat only as “lasagne” and other “vivande di pasta”,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and al-Idrisi had already described Trabia in Sicily in 1154 making pasta by the shipload for Calabria and for Muslim and Christian lands, some 140 years before Marco Polo came home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Medieval & early modern","src":["Marco Polo, <i>The Book of Ser Marco Polo, the Venetian, Concerning the Kingdoms and Marvels of the East</i>, trans. and ed. Henry Yule (London: John Murray, 1871), 1:392, https://archive.org/details/bookofsermarcopo01polo. [Open access]","Marco Polo, <i>The Book of Ser Marco Polo, the Venetian, Concerning the Kingdoms and Marvels of the East</i>, trans. and ed. Henry Yule (London: John Murray, 1871), 2:247, https://archive.org/details/bookofsermarcopo02polo. [Open access]","al-Idrisi, <i>Nuzhat al-mushtāq</i> (1154), in Michele Amari, trans., <i>Biblioteca arabo-sicula: versione italiana</i>, vol. 1 (Turin: Ermanno Loescher, 1880), 63, https://archive.org/details/bibliotecaarabo01amargoog. [Open access]","Giovanni Battista Ramusio, ed., <i>Secondo volume delle navigationi et viaggi</i> (Venice: Giunti, 1574), fols. 30r and 52v, https://archive.org/details/secundovolumedel00ramu. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Wright brothers’ first powered flight in 1903 lasted twelve seconds, and the best flight of that same morning lasted nearly five times as long.","a":true,"why":"Orville flew 36 metres (120 feet) in twelve seconds on the first attempt at Kitty Hawk on 17 December 1903.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Wilbur took the controls for the best flight of the day and covered 255.6 metres (852 feet) in 59 seconds, on an aircraft whose own wingspan was 12.3 metres (40 feet 4 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["National Air and Space Museum, “1903 Wright Flyer,” object A19610048000, Smithsonian Institution, https://airandspace.si.edu/collection-objects/1903-wright-flyer/nasm_A19610048000. [Open access]","National Park Service, “The First Flight,” Wright Brothers National Memorial, https://www.nps.gov/wrbr/learn/historyculture/thefirstflight.htm. [Open access]","Orville Wright, “How We Made the First Flight” (1913), with an introduction by Paul E. Garber (Washington, DC: Federal Aviation Administration, Aviation Education Program), https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/education/educators/curriculum/k12/K-12_How_We_Made_the_First_Flight_Orville_Wright.pdf. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The first person to see bacteria did so through lenses he had ground himself.","a":true,"why":"Van Leeuwenhoek's discovery of ‘animalcules’ is where microbiology begins, and the lenses were his own: self-produced, and unsurpassed for more than 150 years — a visitor recorded him saying he ground them all himself in a grinding-cup and scorned blown glass, and his grinding apparatus appears in his letters and in the inventory of his house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Neutron tomography inside two of the surviving instruments found the Leiden one's lens ground and polished in the classical way, while the most powerful that survives, at Utrecht, was made by the blown-globule method Robert Hooke had popularised in 1678.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Science & invention","src":["Tiemen Cocquyt, Zhou Zhou, Jeroen Plomp, and Lambert van Eijck, “Neutron Tomography of Van Leeuwenhoek’s Microscopes,” <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 20 (2021): eabf2402, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abf2402. [Open access]","Clifford Dobell, <i>Antony van Leeuwenhoek and His “Little Animals”</i> (London: John Bale, Sons and Danielsson, 1932), 52, 68, https://archive.org/details/antonyvanleeuwen00dobe. [Open access]","Lesley A. Robertson, “Antoni van Leeuwenhoek 1723–2023: A Review to Commemorate Van Leeuwenhoek’s Death, 300 Years Ago,” <i>Antonie van Leeuwenhoek</i> 116, no. 10 (2023): 919–35, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10482-023-01859-4. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The most complete early human skeleton ever found is more than a million years old.","a":true,"why":"The Turkana Boy, the skeleton of a boy who died beside a lake in northern Kenya about 1.6 million years ago, is the most complete early human ever recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His discoverers reported in 1985 that he was already 1.68 metres (5 feet 6 inches) tall at death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “KNM-WT 15000,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/fossils/knm-wt-15000. [Open access]","Frank Brown, John Harris, Richard Leakey and Alan Walker, “Early <i>Homo erectus</i> Skeleton from West Lake Turkana, Kenya,” <i>Nature</i> 316 (1985): 788–92, https://doi.org/10.1038/316788a0. [Paywalled]","Natural History Museum, London, “<i>Homo erectus</i>, Our Ancient Ancestor,” accessed 17 October 2026, https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/homo-erectus-our-ancient-ancestor.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The smallest braincase found at Dmanisi in Georgia is closer in size to Homo habilis than to Homo erectus.","a":true,"why":"The Dmanisi people were small-bodied, and the smallest of their braincases holds only about 546 cubic centimetres — nearer Homo habilis than the Homo erectus they are usually assigned to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That skull, D4500, is the one Homo cranium that plots beside the australopithecines,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the five Dmanisi crania together run from 546 to 775 cubic centimetres (33 to 47 cubic inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["David Lordkipanidze, Marcia S. Ponce de León, Ann Margvelashvili, et al., “A Complete Skull from Dmanisi, Georgia, and the Evolutionary Biology of Early <i>Homo</i>,” <i>Science</i> 342, no. 6156 (2013): 326–31, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1238484. [Paywalled]","Juan Antonio Pérez-Claros, Paul Palmqvist and Brandon Hedrick, “Heterochronies and Allometries in the Evolution of the Hominid Cranium: A Morphometric Approach Using Classical Anthropometric Variables,” <i>PeerJ</i> 10 (2022): e13991, https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.13991. [Open access]","Ian Tattersall, Pasquale Raia and Dominique Grimaud-Hervé, “Endocranial Volumes and Human Evolution,” <i>F1000Research</i> 12 (2023): 565, https://doi.org/10.12688/f1000research.131636.1. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Acheulean hand-axe tradition begins about 1.4 million years ago.","a":false,"why":"The oldest Acheulean tools come from Kokiselei west of Lake Turkana<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and from Konso in Ethiopia<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and are about 1.76 to 1.74 million years old, which pushed the tradition back some 300,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Ignacio de la Torre, “The Origins of the Acheulean: Past and Present Perspectives on a Major Transition in Human Evolution,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150245, under “A Historical Context for the Acheulean” and “The Hard Evidence for the Earliest Acheulean: The Current View,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920301/. [Open access]","Christopher J. Lepre, Hélène Roche, Dennis V. Kent, et al., “An Earlier Origin for the Acheulian,” <i>Nature</i> 477 (2011): 82–85, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature10372. [Paywalled]","Yonas Beyene, Shigehiro Katoh, Giday WoldeGabriel, et al., “The Characteristics and Chronology of the Earliest Acheulean at Konso, Ethiopia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 110, no. 5 (2013): 1584–91, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1221285110. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Fire became routine human equipment as soon as the first hominins learned to use it.","a":false,"why":"Wil Roebroeks and Paola Villa argued in 2011 that a handful of early sites is not the same as habit, and that fire becomes routine equipment only around 400,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Traces of fire do become far more numerous on sites across the Old World from about 400,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Wil Roebroeks and Paola Villa, “On the Earliest Evidence for Habitual Use of Fire in Europe,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 108, no. 13 (2011): 5209–14, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3069174/. [Open access]","J. A. J. Gowlett, “The Discovery of Fire by Humans: A Long and Convoluted Process,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1696 (2016): 20150164, https://doi.org/10.1098/rstb.2015.0164. [Open access]","Katharine MacDonald, Fulco Scherjon, Eva van Veen, Krist Vaesen and Wil Roebroeks, “Middle Pleistocene Fire Use: The First Signal of Widespread Cultural Diffusion in Human Evolution,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 118, no. 31 (2021): e2101108118, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2101108118. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"A whole new genus of human was once named on the evidence of a single tooth.","a":true,"why":"In 1927 the Canadian anatomist Davidson Black named the genus and species Sinanthropus pekinensis — Peking Man — on the strength of one lower molar,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> a tooth Birger Bohlin had found at Zhoukoudian on 16 October that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Song Xing, María Martinón-Torres and José María Bermúdez de Castro, “The Fossil Teeth of the Peking Man,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 2066, introduction, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-20432-y. [Open access]","Davidson Black, “Further Hominid Remains of Lower Quaternary Age from the Chou Kou Tien Deposit,” <i>Nature</i> 120 (1927): 954, https://doi.org/10.1038/120954b0. [Paywalled]","Paleo Core, “<i>Sinanthropus pekinensis</i> Black and Zdansky, 1927,” Origins nomenclature database, accessed 17 October 2026, https://paleocore.org/origins/nomina/detail/75. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Upper Cave at Zhoukoudian, near Beijing, held more remains of Peking Man.","a":false,"why":"Its people were modern humans,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> at least eight of them, and its cultural layers are dated to roughly 38,300 to 33,500 years ago<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> — a 2018 radiocarbon programme puts the archaeological layers at no less than 35,100 to 33,500 years<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> — hundreds of thousands of years later than the Homo erectus finds at the same site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Francesco d'Errico et al., “Zhoukoudian Upper Cave Personal Ornaments and Ochre: Rediscovery and Reevaluation,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 161 (2021): 103088, §§1–2 and fig. 1, https://shs.hal.science/hal-03445635/document. [Open access]","Feng Li, Christopher J. Bae, Christopher B. Ramsey, Fuyou Chen and Xing Gao, “Re-dating Zhoukoudian Upper Cave, Northern China and Its Regional Significance,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 121 (2018): 170–77, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhevol.2018.02.011. [Paywalled]","Katerina Harvati, “Into Eurasia: A Geometric Morphometric Re-assessment of the Upper Cave (Zhoukoudian) Specimens,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 57, no. 6 (2009): 751–62, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhevol.2009.07.008. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"The gold-rich Copper Age cemetery at Varna in Bulgaria was in use for well over a thousand years.","a":false,"why":"The Varna necropolis was in use for about 250 years, between 4590 and 4340 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first precision radiocarbon dates had already put the cemetery at 4560–4450 BCE,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the genetic studies of its dead work with a span of about 4600–4300 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Raiko Krauß et al., “Chronology and Development of the Chalcolithic Necropolis of Varna I,” <i>Documenta Praehistorica</i> 44 (2017): 282–305, https://doi.org/10.4312/dp.44.17. [Open access]","Tom Higham, John Chapman, Vladimir Slavchev, et al., “New Perspectives on the Varna Cemetery (Bulgaria) – AMS Dates and Social Implications,” <i>Antiquity</i> 81, no. 313 (2007): 640–54, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0003598X00095636. [Paywalled]","Sandra Penske, Adam B. Rohrlach, Ainash Childebayeva, et al., “Early Contact between Late Farming and Pastoralist Societies in Southeastern Europe,” <i>Nature</i> 620 (2023): 358–65, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-06334-8. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The oldest known copper smelting comes from the Balkans rather than the Near East.","a":true,"why":"The oldest known copper smelting is slag from Belovode in eastern Serbia, dated to about 5000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Copper ores were being mined in the Balkans from about 6200 BCE, but the earliest evidence of smelting them comes only around 5000 BCE,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the Belovode and Pločnik workshops stand at the head of a Serbian tradition that ran on through the fifth millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Miljana Radivojević and Benjamin W. Roberts, “Early Balkan Metallurgy: Origins, Evolution and Society, 6200–3700 BC,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 34 (2021): 195–278, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10963-021-09155-7. [Open access]","Jan Piet Brozio, Zofia Stos-Gale, Johannes Müller, et al., “The Origin of Neolithic Copper on the Central Northern European Plain and in Southern Scandinavia: Connectivities on a European Scale,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 5 (2023): e0283007, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0283007. [Open access]","Jack Longman, Daniel Veres, Walter Finsinger and Vasile Ersek, “Exceptionally High Levels of Lead Pollution in the Balkans from the Early Bronze Age to the Industrial Revolution,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 115, no. 25 (2018): E5661, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1721546115. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"People used copper minerals as ornament long before anyone smelted metal out of them.","a":true,"why":"The first use of copper in the Balkans, from about 6200 BCE, is malachite and azurite ground into beads — minerals picked for their green and blue rather than for any metal in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the Levant too, green minerals including copper ores were first sought for beads around the dawn of farming, long before any smelting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Miljana Radivojević and Benjamin W. Roberts, “Early Balkan Metallurgy: Origins, Evolution and Society, 6200–3700 BC,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 34 (2021): 195–278, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10963-021-09155-7. [Open access]","Jan Piet Brozio, Zofia Stos-Gale, Johannes Müller, et al., “The Origin of Neolithic Copper on the Central Northern European Plain and in Southern Scandinavia: Connectivities on a European Scale,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 5 (2023): e0283007, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0283007. [Open access]","Daniella E. Bar-Yosef Mayer and Naomi Porat, “Green Stone Beads at the Dawn of Agriculture,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 105, no. 25 (2008): 8548–51, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0709931105. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Yangshao culture of the middle Yellow River flourished roughly between 5000 and 3000 BCE.","a":true,"why":"It is the great Neolithic culture of the middle Yellow River, spread across what are now Henan, Shaanxi and Shanxi,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> conventionally dated to about 5000–3000 BCE,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with its late phase placed at 3500–3000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Lei Sun et al., “The Demic Expansion of Yangshao Culture Inferred from Ancient Human Genomes,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 23 (2025): 186, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12218936/. [Open access]","Chao Ning, Tianjiao Li, Ke Wang, et al., “Ancient Genomes from Northern China Suggest Links between Subsistence Changes and Human Migration,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 11 (2020): 2700, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-020-16557-2. [Open access]","Chris J. Stevens, Yijie Zhuang and Dorian Q. Fuller, “Millets, Dogs, Pigs and Permanent Settlement: Productivity Transitions in Neolithic Northern China,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 6 (2024): e44, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2024.31. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"New dating has made the earliest farming settlement at Mehrgarh younger than it was long thought to be.","a":true,"why":"Radiocarbon on tooth enamel from 23 burials puts the aceramic cemetery between 5200 and 4900 BCE, lasting two to five centuries, which overturned its older dates:<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> the aceramic village had long been placed in the seventh millennium BCE, perhaps even the eighth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Benjamin Mutin et al., “New Radiocarbon Dates of Human Tooth Enamel Reveal a Late Appearance of Farming Life in the Indus Valley,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15 (2025): 11345, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-92621-5. [Open access]","Jonas Gregorio de Souza, Javier Ruiz-Pérez, Carla Lancelotti and Marco Madella, “Environmental Effects on the Spread of the Neolithic Crop Package to South Asia,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 7 (2022): e0268482, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0268482. [Open access]","J. Bates, K. D. Morrison, M. Madella, et al., “Early to Mid-Holocene Land Use Transitions in South Asia: A New Archaeological Synthesis of Potential Human Impacts,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 2 (2025): e0313409, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0313409. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Sahara has been green only once in the recent geological past.","a":false,"why":"More than 230 such green phases have been counted in the last eight million years, paced by the changing shape of the Earth's orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Climate modelling shows these North African humid periods recurring with the precession cycle over the past 800,000 years,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and cave deposits in Morocco record Green Sahara conditions both in the early to mid-Holocene and in Marine Isotope Stage 5a of the last glacial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Earth science","src":["Juan C. Larrasoaña, Andrew P. Roberts and Eelco J. Rohling, “Dynamics of Green Sahara Periods and Their Role in Hominin Evolution,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 10 (2013): e76514, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0076514. [Open access]","Edward Armstrong, Miikka Tallavaara, Peter O. Hopcroft and Paul J. Valdes, “North African Humid Periods over the Past 800,000 Years,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 14 (2023): 5549, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-41219-4. [Open access]","Yassine Ait Brahim, Lijuan Sha, Jasper A. Wassenburg, et al., “The Spatiotemporal Extent of the Green Sahara during the Last Glacial Period,” <i>iScience</i> 26, no. 7 (2023): 107018, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2023.107018. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"One Saharan massif alone holds more than seven hundred recorded rock-art sites.","a":true,"why":"The Tadrart Acacus in Libya has 707 recorded sites,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and the Sapienza University catalogue of its rock art now runs to more than 740;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> the great concentrations of Saharan rock art lie in the central massifs, the Acacus and the Tassili n'Ajjer in Algeria above all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Marina Gallinaro, “Saharan Rock Art: Local Dynamics and Wider Perspectives,” <i>Arts</i> 2, no. 4 (2013): 350–82, https://doi.org/10.3390/arts2040350. [Open access]","European Commission, CORDIS, “Ancient Saharan Art – Decoding Art through Theoretically-sounded Archive” (ASArt-DATA, project 795744), periodic reporting summary, accessed 17 October 2026, https://cordis.europa.eu/project/id/795744/reporting. [Open access]","Christian Dupuy, review of <i>Atlas of Tadrart Acacus Rock Art: A UNESCO World Heritage Site in Southwestern Libya</i>, edited by Savino di Lernia and Marina Gallinaro, <i>Journal des Africanistes</i> 95, no. 2 (2025): 228–35, https://doi.org/10.4000/15oo0. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Cattle herding reached the Sahara with herders arriving from outside it.","a":false,"why":"By 7,000 years ago people were keeping livestock in the Libyan mountains, and herding seems to have spread among the Saharans already there rather than arriving with newcomers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cattle and small livestock reached the central Sahara at the end of the eighth millennium BP and were taken up slowly by local hunter-gatherers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Nada Salem et al., “Ancient DNA from the Green Sahara Reveals Ancestral North African Lineage,” <i>Nature</i> 641, no. 8061 (2025): 144–50, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-08793-7. [Open access]","Savino di Lernia, Mary Anne Tafuri, Marina Gallinaro, et al., “Inside the ‘African Cattle Complex’: Animal Burials in the Holocene Central Sahara,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 2 (2013): e56879, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0056879. [Open access]","Max Planck Society, “First Ancient Genomes from the Green Sahara Deciphered,” press release, 2 April 2025, https://www.mpg.de/24407329/first-ancient-genomes-from-the-green-sahara-deciphered. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Jōmon people of Japan are read as descendants of people who reached the islands in the Upper Palaeolithic.","a":true,"why":"They are read as the direct descendants of those who carried Upper Palaeolithic stone tools into the islands some 38,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Humans had reached the archipelago by about 38,000 years ago, and genome-wide data place the Jōmon lineage's origin after the early divergences among Upper Palaeolithic East Eurasians;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> the mitochondrial record likewise points to an ancestral population settling the islands in the Upper Palaeolithic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Later surveys of East Asian ancient genomes likewise keep the Jōmon as a deeply diverged lineage apart from the continental groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Takashi Gakuhari et al., “Ancient Jomon Genome Sequence Analysis Sheds Light on Migration Patterns of Early East Asian Populations,” <i>Communications Biology</i> 3 (2020): 437, https://doi.org/10.1038/s42003-020-01162-2. [Open access]","Niall P. Cooke, Valeria Mattiangeli, Lara M. Cassidy, et al., “Ancient Genomics Reveals Tripartite Origins of Japanese Populations,” <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 38 (2021): eabh2419, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abh2419. [Open access]","Koki Yoshida, Yoshiki Wakiyama, Yuka Nakamura, et al., “Demographic History of the Jomon People: Insights from Whole-Mitogenome Analysis,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 134, no. 1 (2026): 15–28, https://doi.org/10.1537/ase.251024. [Open access]","Guanglin He, Yuntao Sun, Shuhan Duan, et al., “Ancient Genomes Give Insight into 160,000 Years of East Asian Population Dynamics and Biological Adaptation,” <i>Genome Biology</i> 26 (2025): 420, https://doi.org/10.1186/s13059-025-03859-1. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Maize was first domesticated in what is now the United States.","a":false,"why":"Maize was made out of teosinte, a Mexican grass, about 9,000 years ago, in a single domestication in southern Mexico,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> where the Balsas valley has maize starch and phytoliths from 8,700 years ago;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> it reached North America long afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Chin Jian Yang, Luis Fernando Samayoa, Peter J. Bradbury, et al., “The genetic architecture of teosinte catalyzed and constrained maize domestication,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 12 (2019): 5643–5652, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1820997116. [Open access]","Yoshihiro Matsuoka, Yves Vigouroux, Major M. Goodman, et al., “A Single Domestication for Maize Shown by Multilocus Microsatellite Genotyping,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 99, no. 9 (2002): 6080–84, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.052125199. [Open access]","Dolores R. Piperno, Anthony J. Ranere, Irene Holst, Jose Iriarte and Ruth Dickau, “Starch Grain and Phytolith Evidence for Early Ninth Millennium B.P. Maize from the Central Balsas River Valley, Mexico,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 106, no. 13 (2009): 5019–24, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0812525106. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Growing maize, beans and squash together — the Three Sisters — is thousands of years old in the American Midwest and north-east.","a":false,"why":"It became common there only by the middle of the 1500s,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> so an arrangement most readers take to be ancient is a few generations older than the colonies rather than millennia old. The common bean, the last of the three to arrive, becomes archaeologically visible in the north-east only in the late thirteenth century CE,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the full complex is dated no earlier than about 1070 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["D. G. Kapayou, E. M. Herrighty, C. Gish Hill, et al., “Reuniting the Three Sisters: collaborative science with Native growers to improve soil and community health,” <i>Agriculture and Human Values</i> 40, no. 1 (2022): 65–82, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9288846/. [Open access]","John P. Hart, David L. Asch, C. Margaret Scarry and Gary W. Crawford, “The Age of the Common Bean (<i>Phaseolus vulgaris</i> L.) in the Northern Eastern Woodlands of North America,” <i>Antiquity</i> 76, no. 292 (2002): 377–83, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0003598X00090475. [Paywalled]","Tania M. Ngapo, Pauline Bilodeau, Yves Arcand, et al., “Historical Indigenous Food Preparation Using Produce of the Three Sisters Intercropping System,” <i>Foods</i> 10, no. 3 (2021): 524, https://doi.org/10.3390/foods10030524. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Four of the five plants first domesticated in eastern North America appear within a narrow window around 3800 years ago.","a":true,"why":"The earliest turn up as charred seeds in rockshelters and river-bank camps in Kentucky, Tennessee, Missouri and Illinois.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Squash, sunflower, marshelder and goosefoot were domesticated there independently of Mexico,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the earliest domesticated goosefoot and marshelder come from floodplain sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Bruce D. Smith and Richard A. Yarnell, “Initial formation of an indigenous crop complex in eastern North America at 3800 B.P.,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 106, no. 16 (2009): 6561–66, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2666091/. [Open access]","Bruce D. Smith, “Eastern North America as an Independent Center of Plant Domestication,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 103, no. 33 (2006): 12223–28, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0604335103. [Open access]","Natalie G. Mueller, “The Sleeping Crops of Eastern North America: A New Synthesis,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 380, no. 1926 (2025): 20240192, https://doi.org/10.1098/rstb.2024.0192. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The great earthworks at Poverty Point in Louisiana were raised by farmers.","a":false,"why":"Poverty Point was raised about 3,300 years ago by people who hunted, fished and gathered rather than farmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No evidence of farming has been found in a secure Poverty Point context.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Seth B. Grooms, Grace M. V. Ward and Tristram R. Kidder, “Convergence at Poverty Point: A Revised Chronology of the Late Archaic Lower Mississippi Valley,” <i>Antiquity</i> 97, no. 396 (2023): 1453–69, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2023.155. [Open access]","State of Louisiana, <i>Monumental Earthworks of Poverty Point</i>, nomination to the UNESCO World Heritage List, hosted by the US National Park Service, accessed 17 October 2026, https://www.nps.gov/subjects/internationalcooperation/upload/Poverty_Point.pdf. [Open access]","Jon L. Gibson, <i>Poverty Point: A Culture of the Lower Mississippi Valley</i>, Anthropological Study no. 7, 2nd printing (Baton Rouge: Louisiana Archaeological Survey and Antiquities Commission, 1985), “Foods,” https://gutenberg.org/cache/epub/62948/pg62948-images.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Pottery in Jōmon Japan was used chiefly for storing nuts and seeds.","a":false,"why":"Across 143 vessels spanning nine thousand years, a large share carry the chemical markers of food out of the water, whatever the phase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The very oldest pots, of 15,000 to 11,800 years ago, had mostly cooked freshwater and marine animals,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and at the other end Final Jōmon pottery on the Aomori coast still processed mainly marine products.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Alexandre Lucquin et al., “Ancient Lipids Document Continuity in the Use of Early Hunter-Gatherer Pottery through 9,000 Years of Japanese Prehistory,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 15 (2016): 3991–96, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1522908113. [Open access]","O. E. Craig, H. Saul, A. Lucquin, et al., “Earliest Evidence for the Use of Pottery,” <i>Nature</i> 496 (2013): 351–54, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature12109. [Paywalled]","Akiko Horiuchi, Yoshiki Miyata, Nobuhiko Kamijo, Lucy Cramp and Richard P. Evershed, “A Dietary Study of the Kamegaoka Culture Population during the Final Jomon Period, Japan, Using Stable Isotope and Lipid Analyses of Ceramic Residues,” <i>Radiocarbon</i> 57, no. 4 (2015): 721–36, https://doi.org/10.2458/azu_rc.57.18455. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"The oldest soybean finds in Japan are older than the oldest azuki bean finds.","a":true,"why":"The oldest Japanese azuki is about 10,000 years old, from the Awazu site in Shiga,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> or about 10,700 years by direct dating of remains from the Tochibara rock shelter;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> the oldest soybean is about 13,000 years old, from Ojiyama in Miyazaki,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and impressions of soybean-genus seeds 12,600 to 12,900 years old have been found on pottery from Ōshikakubo in Shizuoka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Yu Takahashi et al., “Domestication of Azuki Bean and Soybean in Japan: From the Insight of Archeological and Molecular Evidence,” <i>Breeding Science</i> 73, no. 2 (2023): 117–31, https://doi.org/10.1270/jsbbs.22074. [Open access]","Xuexiang Chen, Zejuan Sun, Shuhan Zhang, et al., “The Discovery of Adzuki Bean (<i>Vigna angularis</i>) in Eastern China during the 9th Millennium BP and Its Domestication in East Asia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 39 (2025): e2510835122, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2510835122. [Open access]","Kanazawa University, “縄紋草創期土器に最古の「シソ属」の種実圧痕を発見” [Oldest <i>Perilla</i> seed impressions found on Incipient Jōmon pottery], research news, in Japanese, accessed 17 October 2026, https://www.kanazawa-u.ac.jp/en/miraichi/185046/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Pottery in Jōmon-period Kyushu was carried across open water between islands.","a":true,"why":"At one site in southern Kyushu about one pot in ten had been carried at least 17 to 33 kilometres (11 to 21 miles), some of it across open channels between islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On Tanegashima most Incipient Jōmon pottery was made on the island, but some came from Yakushima or from Kyushu proper across the strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Open water was no barrier there: the islands south of Kyushu had been reached by deliberate sea crossings 35,000 to 30,000 years ago, some of them from Kyushu itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Fumie Iizuka, Jeffrey R. Ferguson, and Masami Izuho, “Late Pleistocene Pottery Production and Exchange: Provenance Studies of Hunter-Gatherer Wares from Southern Kyushu, Japan by Neutron Activation Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 3 (2022): e0265329, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0265329. [Open access]","Kazuki Morisaki, Fumie Iizuka, Masami Izuho and Mark Aldenderfer, “More on Mobility and Sedentism: Changes in Adaptation from Upper Paleolithic to Incipient Jomon, Tanegashima Island, Southern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 1 (2025): e0314311, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0314311. [Open access]","Yousuke Kaifu, Tien-Hsia Kuo, Yoshimi Kubota and Sen Jan, “Palaeolithic Voyage for Invisible Islands beyond the Horizon,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 19785, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-76831-7. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Han River, which runs through Seoul, drains the largest watershed in Korea.","a":true,"why":"Its watershed covers 26,219 square kilometres (10,123 square miles),<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> about 27 per cent of South Korea's area, making it the largest river basin in the country;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> its two great branches, the North and South Han, meet at Paldang above Seoul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["Jong Kwon Im, Yong Chul Cho, Young Seuk Kim, Soyoung Lee, Taegu Kang and Sang Hun Kim, “Characteristics, Possible Origins, and Health Risk Assessment of Trace Elements in Surface Water of the Han River Watershed,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 23 (2022): 15822, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9741419/. [Open access]","Jong Kwon Im, Soon Ju Yu, Sujin Kim, Sang Hun Kim, Hye Ran Noh and Moon Kyung Kim, “Occurrence, Potential Sources, and Risk Assessment of Volatile Organic Compounds in the Han River Basin, South Korea,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 18, no. 7 (2021): 3727, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph18073727. [Open access]","Jung Eun Lee, Rumi Park, Mina Yu, Myeongseop Byeon and Taegu Kang, “qPCR-Based Monitoring of 2-Methylisoborneol/Geosmin-Producing Cyanobacteria in Drinking Water Reservoirs in South Korea,” <i>Microorganisms</i> 11, no. 9 (2023): 2332, https://doi.org/10.3390/microorganisms11092332. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"South Korea has a markedly older population than North Korea.","a":true,"why":"19.3 per cent of the South is 65 or older against 12.4 per cent of the North.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The UN's 2024 estimates put the median age at about 45 years in the South and 36 in the North,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and Statistics Korea counted 9.94 million people aged 65 or over in 2024, 19.2 per cent of the population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["World Bank, “Population Ages 65 and Above (% of Total Population) — Korea, Rep.; Korea, Dem. People's Rep.,” World Development Indicators, https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/SP.POP.65UP.TO.ZS?locations=KR-KP. [Open access]","United Nations, Department of Economic and Social Affairs, Population Division, <i>World Population Prospects 2024</i>, online edition, demographic indicators (medium variant) data file, https://population.un.org/wpp/. [Open access]","“Nearly 20% of Koreans Aged 65 and Older in 2024: Data,” <i>The Korea Times</i>, 26 September 2024 (news report of Statistics Korea figures), https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/www/nation/2024/11/113_383168.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Korea built a long defensive wall across the northern neck of the peninsula in the 11th century.","a":true,"why":"The Goryeo long wall, finished in 1034, marks where the kingdom's frontier stood against repeated incursions by the Khitan and the Jurchen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under King Deokjong in the 1030s the court ordered a wall nearly a thousand <i>li</i> long from the Yalu to the eastern sea,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and further sections were added at its eastern end under his successor Jeongjong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["Gari Ledyard, “Cartography in Korea,” in <i>The History of Cartography</i>, vol. 2, book 2, ed. J. B. Harley and David Woodward (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1994), 235–344, https://press.uchicago.edu/books/HOC/HOC_V2_B2/HOC_VOLUME2_Book2_chapter10.pdf. [Open access]","Homer B. Hulbert, <i>The History of Korea</i>, vol. 1 (Seoul: Methodist Publishing House, 1905), Project Gutenberg ebook 52127, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/52127. [Open access]","윤경진 [Yun Gyeongjin], “고려 현종말∼문종초 北界 州鎭 설치와 長城 축조” [Construction of the Goryeo Grand Fortress in the Northern Realm and the Establishment of Jujin Units], <i>군사</i> [Military History] 79 (2011): 277–312, https://doi.org/10.29212/mh.2011..79.277. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"Human sacrifice in China begins with the Shang dynasty.","a":false,"why":"The practice is older: at Shimao, a walled city on the Loess Plateau, more than 80 human skulls lie buried beneath the East Gate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shimao, a stone-walled centre of about 2300 to 1800 BCE, shows human sacrifice performed before its fortifications were built,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and its sacrificial pits belong to a Longshan-period shift from pottery offerings towards human victims at wall-building rituals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Zehui Chen et al., “Ancient DNA from Shimao City Records Kinship Practices in Neolithic China,” <i>Nature</i> 648, no. 8094 (2025): 659–67, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12711557/. [Open access]","Zhouyong Sun, Jing Shao, Li Liu, Jianxin Cui, Michael F. Bonomo, Qinghua Guo, Xiaohong Wu, and Jiajing Wang, “The First Neolithic Urban Center on China's North Loess Plateau: The Rise and Fall of Shimao,” <i>Archaeological Research in Asia</i> 14 (2018): 33–45, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ara.2017.02.004. [Paywalled]","Yang Qian, “Conflict and Identity: The Ritual of Wall Construction in Early China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 2 (2019), https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76786. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The traditional list of Shang kings runs to thirty-one.","a":true,"why":"Wu Ding stands 22nd in that list and Di Xin last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The count turns on whom one admits: Sima Qian and the Bamboo Annals keep the brief reigns of Wai Bing and Zhong Ren and reach thirty, where the <i>Tongjian gangmu</i>, dropping them, has twenty-eight,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and Keightley’s oracle-bone numbering runs from Wu Ding at K21 to Di Xin at K28.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), cxciii, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [in French] [Open access]","David N. Keightley, <i>Sources of Shang History: The Oracle-Bone Inscriptions of Bronze Age China</i> (Berkeley: University of California Press, 1978), 94, https://archive.org/details/keightley-sources-of-shang-history-1978. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The tomb of Fu Hao held about 1,500 objects.","a":true,"why":"Among them were weapons, bells, blades, mirrors and bronze sculptures of tigers, together with a great quantity of jade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tomb, number 5 at Xiaotun, was found intact in 1976, and Fu Hao's name is cast on more than sixty of its bronze vessels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","“Fu Hao's Tomb,” in <i>A Visual Sourcebook of Chinese Civilization</i>, prepared by Patricia Ebrey (University of Washington), https://depts.washington.edu/chinaciv/archae/2fuhmain.htm. [Open access]","David N. Keightley, <i>Sources of Shang History: The Oracle-Bone Inscriptions of Bronze Age China</i> (Berkeley: University of California Press, 1978), preface, https://archive.org/details/keightley-sources-of-shang-history-1978. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"By the late phases at Anyang, cattle had passed pigs in dietary importance even in ordinary quarters.","a":true,"why":"One head of cattle yields over 200 kilograms (440 pounds) of meat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Anyang's bone workshops were fed mainly with cattle bone, a by-product of large-scale sacrifice,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> in which cattle had replaced pigs as the commonest victims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Yitzchak Jaffe and Roderick Campbell, “To Eat or Not to Eat? Animals and Categorical Fluidity in Shang Society,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 1 (2021): 158–77, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108215. [Open access]","Roderick B. Campbell, Zhipeng Li, Yuling He, and Yuan Jing, “Consumption, Exchange and Production at the Great Settlement Shang: Bone-Working at Tiesanlu, Anyang,” <i>Antiquity</i> 85, no. 330 (2011): 1279–97, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0003598X00062050. [Paywalled]","Lu Peng, Katherine Brunson, Yuan Jing, and Li Zhipeng, “Zooarchaeological and Genetic Evidence for the Origins of Domestic Cattle in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 56, no. 1 (2017), https://hdl.handle.net/10125/64744. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The tallest and heaviest bronze the Shang ever cast is over a metre high.","a":true,"why":"It stands 133 centimetres (52 inches) high and came out of tomb M260 at Xibeigang, east of the cemetery near Wuguancun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sun and colleagues give it as 832.84 kilograms (1,836 pounds) and 133 centimetres (52 inches) high,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> the work of foundries that were turning out cups, goblets and cauldrons weighing well over 100 kilograms (220 pounds).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Wei-dong Sun, Li-peng Zhang, et al., “Origin of the Mysterious Yin-Shang Bronzes in China Indicated by Lead Isotopes,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 6 (2016): 23304, https://doi.org/10.1038/srep23304. [Open access]","OpenStax, <i>World History Volume 1: To 1500</i> (Houston: OpenStax), §5.1 “Ancient China,” https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/5-1-ancient-china. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Mycenae was still a functioning town when Athens and Sparta went to war.","a":false,"why":"In 468 BCE the Argives besieged and stormed it, sold its people into slavery, dedicated a tenth of them to the god and razed the town — though the walls were beyond removing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo found not a trace of the city left,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and Pausanias blamed Argive jealousy of the eighty Mycenaeans who had fought at Thermopylae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.65, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0084%3Abook%3D11%3Achapter%3D65. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 8.6.10, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D6%3Asection%3D10. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.16.5, trans. W. H. S. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D16%3Asection%3D5. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Mycenae has a single tholos tomb, the so-called Treasury of Atreus.","a":false,"why":"Nine of them stand at Mycenae, built between about 1525 and 1275 BCE, and Alan Wace sorted them into three groups by their masonry and their ornament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yannis Galanakis has traced how the Aegisthus tomb anchored that tripartite scheme,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and Cambridge's Mycenae archive still arranges the nine, from the Cyclopean tomb to the Treasury of Atreus and the Tomb of Clytemnestra, by it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 19: Mycenaean Tholos Tombs and Early Mycenaean Settlements,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-19-narrative/. [Open access]","Yannis Galanakis, “The Construction of the Aegisthus Tholos Tomb at Mycenae and the ‘Helladic Heresy’,” <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 102 (2007): 239–56, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0068245400021481. [Paywalled]","Cambridge Digital Library, “Mycenae Tomb Photographs,” collection description, University of Cambridge, https://cudl.lib.cam.ac.uk/collections/mycenae_tomb_photographs. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Skulls from a royal grave circle at Mycenae have been rebuilt as faces.","a":true,"why":"Seven skulls from Grave Circle B were rebuilt in 1995, and the reconstruction found the man in the electrum mask to resemble the woman buried beside him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two more faces, from Shaft Grave VI in Circle A, were later set beside the seven,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> by the Manchester team whose reconstructions also include Philip II and an Etruscan noblewoman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["J. H. Musgrave, R. A. H. Neave and A. J. N. W. Prag, \"Seven Faces from Grave Circle B at Mycenae,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 90 (1995): 107–36, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0068245400016105. [Paywalled]","Lena Papazoglou-Manioudaki et al., “Mycenae Revisited Part 1. The Human Remains from Grave Circle A: Stamatakis, Schliemann and Two New Faces from Shaft Grave VI,” <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 104 (2009): 233–77, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0068245400000241. [Paywalled]","Jean Turfa, review of <i>Seianti Hanunia Tlesnasa: The Story of an Etruscan Noblewoman</i>, by Judith Swaddling and John Prag, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.01.03, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.01.03/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Cumae, on the Bay of Naples, is the oldest Greek city on the Italian mainland.","a":true,"why":"It was in the ground no later than 730 to 720 BCE and was settled from Pithekoussai, the island off the bay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo called it the oldest of all the Sicilian and Italiote cities,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and Livy traced its settlers from Chalcis by way of the islands to the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 5.4.4, trans. H. L. Jones, Loeb Classical Library, LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/5D*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.22, trans. Canon Roberts, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D22. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Capitoline is the largest of Rome's seven hills.","a":false,"why":"It is the smallest,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> some 460 metres (500 yards) long and 180 metres (200 yards) wide on average, lying between the Forum and the Campus Martius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Capitolinus Mons,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 95–98, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Capitolinus.html. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner, <i>The Topography and Monuments of Ancient Rome</i>, 2nd ed. (Boston: Allyn and Bacon, 1911), chap. 11, https://archive.org/details/cu31924019169485. [Open access]","Gregory S. Aldrete, <i>Daily Life in the Roman City</i> (Westport, CT: Greenwood Press, 2004), https://archive.org/details/dailylifeinroman00aldr. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"A hut on the Palatine said to be Romulus's own was still on record a thousand years after Rome was founded.","a":true,"why":"The casa Romuli was still recorded in the 4th century CE, more than a thousand years after the settlement it stood for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius saw it kept in repair on the Palatine slope above the Circus in Augustus's day,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and Vitruvius cited the thatched roof of Romulus's hut as a relic of ancient simplicity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Casa Romuli,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 101–2, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Casa_Romuli.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.79, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1B*.html. [Open access]","Vitruvius, <i>On Architecture</i> 2.1, trans. Joseph Gwilt, LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Vitruvius/2*.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The League of Nations began with fewer than a dozen member states.","a":false,"why":"It came into existence on 10 January 1920, and forty-one states opened its first Assembly at Geneva that November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the ballot that made Paul Hymans the Assembly's president, forty-one states voted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["United Nations Office at Geneva, \"The League of Nations,\" UN Library and Archives Geneva, https://www.ungeneva.org/en/library-archives/league-of-nations. [Open access]","League of Nations Union, <i>The First Assembly: A Study of the Proceedings of the First Assembly of the League of Nations</i> (London: Macmillan, 1921), 22, https://archive.org/details/firstassemblystu00leag. [Open access]","United Nations Office at Geneva, “The League of Nations,” overview, https://www.ungeneva.org/en/about/league-of-nations/overview. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Every treaty that ended the First World War was imposed on the losing side rather than negotiated.","a":false,"why":"The Treaty of Lausanne of 24 July 1923, between Turkey and the Allied powers, was the one post-war settlement that was genuinely negotiated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Talks ran from November 1922, broke down in February 1923 and resumed on 23 April before the signing,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> by Turkey with Britain, France, Italy, Japan, Greece, Romania and the Serb-Croat-Slovene state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["Alan Sharp, \"The Paris Peace Conference and its Consequences,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2022), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/the-paris-peace-conference-and-its-consequences-1-1/. [Open access]","Charles Evans Hughes to Henry Cabot Lodge, 5 May 1924, in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, 1924</i>, vol. 2, doc. 625, Office of the Historian, US Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1924v02/d625. [Open access]","“Treaty of Peace with Turkey Signed at Lausanne, July 24, 1923,” from <i>The Treaties of Peace 1919–1923</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Carnegie Endowment for International Peace, 1924), World War I Document Archive, Brigham Young University Library, https://wwi.lib.byu.edu/index.php/Treaty_of_Lausanne. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Industrially produced trans fat is estimated to cause up to half a million deaths a year.","a":true,"why":"It is a by-product of hardening vegetable oils, and it is entirely replaceable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A 2016 global burden study put the toll at about 537,000 coronary deaths in 2010,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> while the WHO's current fact sheet attributes more than 278,000 deaths a year to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Medicine","src":["Lindsay Steele, Elizabeth Drummond, Chizuru Nishida, Rain Yamamoto, Francesco Branca, Cristina Parsons Perez, Lorena Allemandi, et al., “Ending Trans Fat: The First-Ever Global Elimination Program for a Noncommunicable Disease Risk Factor,” <i>Journal of the American College of Cardiology</i> 84, no. 7 (2024): 663–74, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2024.04.067. [Open access]","Qianyi Wang et al., “Impact of Nonoptimal Intakes of Saturated, Polyunsaturated, and Trans Fat on Global Burdens of Coronary Heart Disease,” <i>Journal of the American Heart Association</i> 5, no. 1 (2016): e002891, https://doi.org/10.1161/JAHA.115.002891. [Open access]","World Health Organization, “Trans Fat,” fact sheet, 24 January 2024, https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/trans-fat. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The alpha helix and the beta sheet were first described after the structure of DNA was solved in 1953.","a":false,"why":"Both were proposed by Linus Pauling and Robert Corey in 1951, two years before the DNA double helix.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The α-helix paper, with Herman Branson as third author, appeared in <i>PNAS</i> on 1 April 1951,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and its bond lengths were not bettered for forty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","David Eisenberg, “The Discovery of the α-Helix and β-Sheet, the Principal Structural Features of Proteins,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 100, no. 20 (2003): 11207–10, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2034522100. [Open access]","Oregon State University Libraries Special Collections & Archives Research Center, “Bombshell,” in <i>Linus Pauling and the Structure of Proteins: A Documentary History</i>, narrative page 37, https://scarc.library.oregonstate.edu/coll/pauling/proteins/narrative/page37.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"A protein cannot find its folded shape by trying conformations at random.","a":true,"why":"Levinthal's paradox of 1969 pointed out that sampling them at random is implausible — there are far too many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A bias of only a few kT towards the native state is enough to shrink the search to biological times,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the random-search picture has given way to many routes down a funnel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Omer Faruk Kuzu, Lars Jørgen Tvenge Granerud, and Fahri Saatcioglu, “Navigating the Landscape of Protein Folding and Proteostasis: From Molecular Chaperones to Therapeutic Innovations,” <i>Signal Transduction and Targeted Therapy</i> 10, no. 1 (2025): 358, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41392-025-02439-w. [Open access]","R. Zwanzig, A. Szabo, and B. Bagchi, “Levinthal's Paradox,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 89, no. 1 (1992): 20–22, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.89.1.20. [Open access]","M. Karplus, “The Levinthal Paradox: Yesterday and Today,” <i>Folding & Design</i> 2, no. 4 (1997): S69–75, https://doi.org/10.1016/S1359-0278(97)00067-9. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"A fatty acid written as 18:1 has eighteen carbon atoms and one double bond.","a":true,"why":"Chains are written as a pair of numbers giving length and the count of double bonds, so palmitate is 16:0, stearate 18:0 and oleate 18:1.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The IUPAC-IUB rules give 16:0 for palmitic and 18:1 for oleic acid,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and oleic acid is monounsaturated, with a single double bond in its C18 chain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Jacob T. Talley and Shamim S. Mohiuddin, “Biochemistry, Fatty Acid Oxidation,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556002/. [Open access]","IUPAC-IUB Commission on Biochemical Nomenclature, “Nomenclature of Lipids: Recommendations Lip-1 and Lip-2,” hosted by Queen Mary University of London, https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/lipid/lip1n2.html. [Open access]","John McMurry, <i>Organic Chemistry</i> (OpenStax), sec. 27.1, “Waxes, Fats, and Oils,” https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/27-1-waxes-fats-and-oils. [Open access]"]}
];
